      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>CHRISTMAS PREPARATIONS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS PREPARATIONS.</p>
        <p>A visit to "Ye Sign of ye Glove" in Church-street Liverpool, brings one in touch with a leading authority on hand coverings of every description. Mr. Henry. Miles, who probably holds the largest and most com- plete stock of gloves in England, makes a speciality of -his patent seamless glove, which is alwaysa safe present. Furs claim premier place as seasonable gifts, and here we observe beautiful necklets of sable, and other skins, -with muffs to match; also some extremely pretty muffs and collars for children at moderate prices. There are also a variety of umbrellas with. handsome handles, which would make very useful presents. The fancy counter is alluring with its load of sparkling knicknacks. There is a line display also of sachets, fans, perfumes, fa ncy hairpins,combs,companions, chatelaines, purses, garters, belts, beaded chains, pearl ropes, necklets, rolled gold bracelets, rings, charms, brooches, &amp;c.. any of which would make a pretty and inexpensive present. Pocket handkerchiefs of all kinds are always popular as gifts, and the choice of them in this establishment is most attractive as is also the selection of pretty ties, collars fronts &amp;c. In the grottndfloor showroom is a very charming assort- ment of evening blouses and some exceedingly tasteful coats and pelisses for little folks. A charming present would be one of the beautiful tea gowns or a cosy dressing gown. There are very pretty styles in smart caps and aprons for servants' presents. In the millinery showroom are some exceedingly smart winter hats and a lovely collection of children's millinery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRADES COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Hours of Labour in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. PODMORE jun., presided at the monthly—meet- ing of the Wa,rrington Trades and babc-mr Council held In Bold-street Wesleyan Schools on Tuesday evening. There was a good attendance and matters of consider- able local interest were discussed.</p>
        <p>EMPLOYMENT •or CFIELDRMS ACT.</p>
        <p>The SEC-RE:TARTY -(Councillor Stott) • inforrnei the delegates that the Town Clerk was. preparing draft by- laws under this Act and they would probably be sub- mitted at the next Council meeting. The CFLAIRMIN said he had already received com- munications from various branches of the federation as to how the Act would affect their (the hairdressing) trade. They employed lather boys in the evening, but the statutory clauses of this measure prohibited the employment of children after nine in the evening and before six in the morning, and it also prohibited trad- ing in the streets by children. Local authorities, how 7 ever, would have a tremendous amount of power on local option lines. If an authority was very progressive it could frame rules which would very materially affect some children employed and indirectly affect children who were at present the means of bringing some grist to the mill in their homes. it would therefore be necessary to consider the question from two aspects—the advantage to the children, and also to see that no undue harm was done to the homes which subsisted to some extent upon the few shillings earned by those children.</p>
        <p>THE HOURS OF LABOUR.—TIME or oprorrrt-sr: FOR A</p>
        <p>REDUCTION</p>
        <p>Mr. BUCKLEY re-introduced the question of the reduction of hours of labour in Warrington and moved " tLat the secretary be instruc_ted to write to the chamber of commerce asking thin to meet a deputation of this council in conference to discuss the advisability of shortening the hours of labour in Warrington,and the best means of bringing it about." Mr. STRING FELLOW seconded. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. EDGAR (cabinet makers) said his society con- sidered the present a most inopportune time to bring the matter forward. The suggestion to reduce the hours of labour would no doubt be receivei with open tie arms by the employer but would the demand for in- creased wages be acce dto as well? The thoughts of workingmen should, now be centrA on the question of labour representation, and he advocated that the matter of reduced hours should be allowed to drop until they had a Government which would alter the decisions of the judges on trade union law. (Hear, hear.) He moved an amendment "That in the opinion of this Council the matter relating to the general reduction of the hours of labour in Warrington should be allowed for the, present to fall throughAs we consider the time to be very inopportune, having due regard to our position to enforce the same." "kr.. . T. SMITE{ (wire;lrawers) seconded. He con- sidered that the employers would be unwilljng to ad- vance wages—on the contrary they were waiting for an opportunity to reduce them at any moment. They meant to do so before Mr. ' Chamberlain's scheme was passed, so that some of them. would he able to say they had advanced wages afterwards, owing to Protection. Mr. P.Ltrmit (wheelwrights and blacksmiths) said he had been instructed to move a Similar amendment. _</p>
        <p>Mr. EDGAR asked why it was proposed to take such a matter before the Chamber of Commerce. Were not their executive committees capable of dealing with it and advising the socioties on the subject. Why take it before the masters ? The suggestion smacked to much of officialism for his liking and his trade had suffered a great deal from Such actions. • Mr. Atlcicr,Fx said that in taking the matter before the Chamber of Commerce they did not want to tight that body about it.Theywanted the reduction in hours tobe brought about peaceably. The slackness in trade, he considered, was an argument in favour of shorter hours. When they introduced the unem- ployed question last year it was pointed out, that whilst some men were not working at all, others were working eighty or ninety hours per week. It *could be a benefit such trades as • the joiners' if a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>uniformity of hours for the whole year could be secured. In regard to wages, he contended that other firms would be willing to follow the example of Messrs. Brunner. .Mond mad Company who, while reducing the hours frot4 511 to 43 per week, paid the meu exactly the same wages. On a note being taken, the amendment . was carriel by II votes to 4. TEff, rAut. CONT111("1!S CLAYSE,</p>
        <p>•The CHAIRMAN', in congratulating Comscillor Stott upon his election for Orford Ward, said he was pleased he had taken the first opportunity of referring to the observance of the Fair Contracts Clause—a matter which required watching. He believed Councillor Stott's'preserice. in the Town Council would be the means of bringing about a better state of things than had been experienced in the past. An iateresting discussion on the represeztation of the borengh is reported in anothbr column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>November and December are not favourite months in which to get 'tarried, but the end of the year has been remarkable for a large number of society weddings. They have been a source of joy to the fashionable dressmkers, who all complain that the year has been anything bat a good one for business, in spite of the more active life at Court and its corresponding influ- . once upon Society. The fact is, that since women have entered into all the pleasures and activities of life,they have so many. more demands upon their allowance. Their clubs, Motoring, gaffing, travelling, all absorb money that would otherwise go to dressmakers, furriers and milliners. But economy is seldom the dominant note eta wedding, and unusual splendour and display have characterised many that have taken place. That. of Lord Helmsley and Lady Marjorie Greville is to be solemnised next month, and Lady Marjorie has decided to have six bridesmaids. The Royal wedding of Prince Alexander of Teck and Princess Alice of Albany is ex- pected to take place in the presence of the King and Queen at Windsor Castle, in February, and for which a large number of royal and distinguished visitors will be entertained by their Majesties.</p>
        <p>Women Lawyers not Wanted.—The application of Miss Cave for permission to practice at the Bar is a matter concerning which different opinions will beheld by different people, but there can be no doubt that the Benchers are right in their decision that the regula.- tions of the Inns of Court in London point only to the admission of men to practice as counsel. Miss Cave has no reason to complain—nor probably does she com- plain—that her application has not 'received sufficient consideration, but when seven judges have decided against her, one is impelled to the conclusion that until the regulations of the Inns of Court are altered it will be useless for any lady to apply for call to the English Bar. Meanwhile the nearest thing that is open to her is the London L.L.8., which has already been taken by Miss Orme, and possibly by other ladies. It is a very good degree involving as exhaustive an ex- amination in law as that provided by the Inns of Court, but it does not include any anthorit-y-to practisceither as council or ass solicitor.</p>
        <p>Mrs. or Miss.—Somebody has noticed that a practice is growing among busy men of signing their names only with initials. Probably the writers think that the initials are sufficiently well-known or that if they are not they ought to be. There is one lady—a member of an aristocratic family—whose initials run M. H. E. H. B. B. E. V. de 0. P. T. S. T., and if she signed all of them, her friends could scarcely err as to the identity of the writer. A more .practical question particularly In the estimation of business firms, is the habit which so many women have in signing their names without saying whether they are Mrs. or Miss. In some cases it is impossible to determine from the handwriting the sex of the signatory, and the result is that ludicrous mistakes occur, women being addressed as Esq., or even in some instances as "Bev.' In one case a lady whose initials were the same as her husband's was addresSed by a firm who had consulted a directory as "Colonel." Toys and Playthings.—Vhildren's presents are an ever recurring topic at this time of year, and. every Christ- mas the es-ray of toys and playthings exposed for sale at the bazaars, grows more elaborate and expensive. Parents and uncles of the older generation, recollect that in their own youth, peg-tops and wooden swords were supposed to suffice for childhood's amusement, but it is very different now, and anything less than a costly model or elaborate piece of mechanism is considered scarcely the thing for a Christmas toy. But thiSelabora- tion can be carried too far, and there is a story of the I Queen of Italy which goes to ptove it. As.may be imagined, the most elaborate and expensive toys were provided for her little girls, but one day the Queeu dis- covered that they were mostly beyond their childish understanding, and she told the nurse to throw or give them all away and go and buy some of the ordinary toys in the shops, such as su.iiced for less favoured children. Afternoon Tea.—Hotels and restturants are becoming more and more the rendezvous for five o'clock meet- ings, as well as for dinner parties, and at some houses the little use that is made of the dining and drawing rooms suggest that these apartments might almost he done away with. No doubt it is more convenient to drop in at some central place for afternoon tea- when one has been" trying on," or doing shopping. That is why the practice has extended amongst women, but it is now the fashion for men to invite their lady friends at whose houses they dine, to afternoon tea. This, however, has led very often to the functions being transferred from the fashionable tea room and pastry- cooks, to the hotels because there are many men.whose idea of afternoon tea takes the form of an American cocktail which is-only obtainable on. licensed premises. • The New Velvets.—The new mousseline velvets. and chiffon velours, so much in vogue for evening goins and wraps, are .also being greatly utilised for coffee coats and tea gowns. Nothing more exquisite has been seen for many years than these new velvets, which have none of the oblects of being an over weighty material, mi was the case with the old varieties. They have an upstanding pile with a scarcely perceptible rib, but they are wonderfully light, and quite the reverse of heavy looking in appearance. The New Fur alovos.—NevPr were muffs more modish and fascinating than they are just now yet many women object to this accessory mainly on the grounds that it is much more bother than it is worth. Needless to say these individuals are mostly of the athletic persuasion, and to such the new fur gloves which the furriers are offering, and which arelboth warm and pliable, will prove invaluable. They can be had too, at quite moderate prices owing to the skill and ingenuity of the furriers in making rabbit and the common or garden cat skins, pass muster for the costly varieties of fur, A Dainty Accessory.—The latest idea for brightening up an evening or afternoon blouse will be welcomed by the impecunious home worker. All one needs is a cluster of some small flowers. Violets, snowdrops, or rosebuds, for:example, which should be tied about the stems firmly. The ribbon—ln shade the same , the flowers and about half an inch in width—must be wound round the stems, and knotted into three or four short loops, several ends of uneven length, being left to fall from the cluster. E.,!ch end should be finished off with one of the small flowers, and a stem tor two, to give it weight. These clusters are largely taking the place of the one time favorite chiffon chou, and are distinctly more gireful, and artistic in effect. A Common Triek.—From time to time attention is drawn in the orrani of the drapery trade, to a system of bogus sales which is practised all over the country. Bills are delivered at private houses, giving particulars of drapery goods, which are stated to be for sale, at prices that are obviously ridiculously low. It is men- tinned on the bill that it will be called for, and shortly afterwards a traviller arrives and asks for the bill, and ' shews samples of the goods referred to. On these how- ever he can only book orders. He has other goods with him which ho can sell for cash, and these he produces offering at the same time to take orders for the articles specified in the handbill, which will be delivered, very shortly. The bait is frequently taken, and the traveller sells for cash sonic of the goods he has with him, which can generally be purchased much more !cheaply from a local draper. The order for the other goods is booked but they never arrive. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, December 12, 1903.</head>
