      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>PRtTTY SKIRT.</head>
        <p>A PRtTTY SKIRT.</p>
        <p>there is little fear that the girl of to-day will ever advocate a style of dress which would interfere with her outdoor sports. .Nowadays women suit their clothes to their occupations, thereby asserting their common</p>
        <p>sense.</p>
        <p>Equally uncomfortable, or almost more so, were the tightly tied back eel-skin skirts of quarter of a century ago which restricted the movements of the wearer and rendered them often ludicrous. At present the most marked points of dress are the long sloping shoulder lines and the fanciful sleeves which fitting- the upper part of the arm tightly develop into frills and puftings from the elbow downwards to the wrists, where a tight band with points coming almost to the knuckles holds the ful- ness into . place. Elbow sleeves- which fall freely in frills or flounces are often used on afternoon or evening blouses. The pretty skirt sketched would look well for the afternoon or evening to wear with a smart silk blouse. It is Made o black voile (although cashmere would do equally well with the fulness gauged at the waist, while a shaped full flounce springs from a little below</p>
        <p>A SMART TOIL</p>
        <p>;TTEI</p>
        <p>the knees! Black silk and braid redallions and trimming are arranged to form a beading to the flounce and Smarten the skirt. These could be used in cream or twine colour, but it is newer and better style to have the skirt all 3f one colour without any ;tote of contrast in the trimming. Short trotioir skirts are in ;reat demand still for country gowns and !rven for morning toilettes in town, for they 'save proved their worth, and the ortcomf there is in being able to walk along without the necessity of grasping embarrassing folds 9f cloth whichin spite of all efforts to the con- trary succeed in coming in contact with mud and conveying a share of it to our boots and shoes, thereby ruining our tout ensemble. ;till, for indoor wear the trailing skirt is e.ertainly the more graceful style and appears m all the newest gowns. It adds height and elegance to most figures, and is altogether Drettier than the more business-like ab- )reviated.style.</p>
        <p>The smari toilette illustrated was expressed Ln navy blue cloth and is trimmed with a narrow edging of beaver. The shoulders are zovered with a cape-like collar edged with the fur, while . a narrow piping of green velvet niarmonises with the green silk with which both coat and skirt. are lined. The coat which fits tightly at ic back and sides is full</p>
