      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>FRENCHMAN'S BLUNDER</head>
        <p>A FRENCHMAN'S BLUNDER</p>
        <p>BY DOROTHY MARTIN,</p>
        <p>Author of "Father Fox, " "Three Little Travellers,"</p>
        <p>"Sorrel, " &amp;t</p>
        <p>They had been very happy together that fresh springtime—the yonng• 12:nglishnian, who was apparently entirely taken up with golf, and a girl of eighteen, his usual companion upon the links. He.wns fond of golf, and she liked nothing better, and in that small hotel in the South of France, where they both were staying, nothing was more natural than that they should become acquainted.</p>
        <p>There were no troublesonle elders to interfere with this acquaintance, which gradually grew into somethin,,,more, so that bright springtime was a period of bliss to Guy Dorrington and little Rachel Herbert.</p>
        <p>lie was a young barrister spending his holidays on the southern coast of France. She was the only daughter of an old Indian who spent his life in travel, and who forgot to keep a proper watch over his pretty daughter at the small French hotel. ' Hitherto everything had gone very smoothly with them, but just %%lien Guy was going to tell Mr. Herbert about his love for Rachel their peace and happiness was suddenly interrupted. It was one evening during the long table d'hOte. Guy, happy, fortunate Guy, who was spoken of at home as having been born under .a lucky star, saw three persons sitting at the Herberts' dinner table. The third was n stranger. a lively Frenchman, evidently an old friend of Mr. Herbert's. Rachel in her white frock, looking exasperatingly pretty, was laughing and chatting with him. Guy scowled across at their table, then . bit his nails hi desPera- tion when Rachel made the new-coiner laugh and look at her with undisguised admiration. He knew that she was true to him, yet be hated to see another man admire her,. and lie longed for one smile or one look from his love to reassure hint of her</p>
        <p>The mot cautious young man throws caution to the winds when he is in lore, and behaves often in a most unrestrained manner, while his mistress keeps her head through all difficulties, is cool and self-possessed at the most critical moment, and so makes a love 'affair prosper and succeed when all might have failed without her calm-- discretion. Such was the case with Guy and Rachel.</p>
        <p>Guy, generally a most .sensible and discreet young man, was fast losing his usual self-command at this first sign of alarm, and nearly spoilt everything by coughing and fidgeting in the hope of attracting Rachel's attention. But she was an atom afraid of her father, who at present was ignorant of her affairs, and she continued to laugh- and talk with the Frenchman, and cruelly ignored those most obvious signs at the end of the room, fearing that their -plans might be overthrown if her father guessed the truth before the morrow, when Guy intended to break it to him with all due respect and good sense.</p>
        <p>In-the salon after dinner Guy grew perfectly desperate when he found that Rachel was still amusing the new-comer. Mr. Herbert sat con- tentedly watching his daughter. Feeling that he was not wanted Guy pushed back his chair front the table where he had been drinking his coffee snd hastily left the room, with a last frown at his rival. The Frenchman looked up at him as he passed out into the hall. He noticed the young man's expression of discontent and annoyance. No doubt Guy's behaviour in the dining-room had not been lost upon him. A look of malicious amuse- ment and triumph crept into his dark, sallow face, and he turned away and began to talk to Rachel with renewed vigour. The large salon grew hotter and hotter; the noisy laughter and talk never ceased. Presently Rachel bade her father and his friend good-night and crept quietly from the room. The Frenchman watched her movements with . his keen eyes.</p>
        <p>"My friend," he said, when she had left the room, "you must keep your eyes open, or you will have your daughter marrying that young Engltsh- man. The truth is I have a mind to wed her myself. She is pretty enough to reign in my château and to shine in Parisian society. What say you, my friend ?" Mr. IHerbert looked pleased. M. Petitjean was very 'rich, and -lie seemed to him -a most eligible suitor for his daughter. "You may try to win her," he . said. "I will back you up, Petitjean. Rachel is always anxious to please me." In the meantime Guy was -slowly taking down his coat in the hall, when a light hand was placed upon his arm and a voice very dear to him said: "Guy, what is it ? Something has put you Out. Tell me." He looked down into the sweet fare at his elbow. "Come for a stroll," he said. "Here is my coat. I will help yon into it." So they passed out of the. hotel together. A wind was blowing from the sea, and they turned away from it down the narrow, sheltered street. There was no moon, but it was not a dark night; a star or two shone overhead, and Guy and Rachel paced arm-in-arTA through the little town content to. be together. But a dark figure, unseen by both, followed them a,t, a distance, occasionally hiding behind a projecting buttress, or creeping cautirrusly across the road to avoid a gas lamp or a passer-by. Suddenly Guy stopped. -He held both Rachel's bands in his:</p>
        <p>"My dear," he sail, passionately, "you will be true to me ? I feel to-night as if all will not go so snu,othly with us as we wish. I feel as if troubles lay ahead ; yet you will trust me always, Rachel ?"</p>
        <p>"Yes," she murmured, almoSt, frightened at. his earnestness.</p>
        <p>"And whatever difficulties arise you will still ho true ? You will believe in me, say it, Rachel, always?" She .gently repeated the word. lie looked relieved.</p>
        <p>"I believe you," be said, walking on again. "You can trust dear."</p>
        <p>She drew closer to him with tears in her eyes. She could not speak just thep, and the dark, stealthy figure still followed, watching their every movement, until they reached the hotel.</p>
        <p>"To-morrow I will speak to your father without fail, Rachel. I would have spoken before but I was a coward, afraid of disturbing our bliss."</p>
        <p>He kissed her there, iii the din i light, where they thought. no one would see. It was a quiet good- night, almost sad in its earnestness.- Rachel ran into the hotel. Guy, feeling more at ease, went and .stood by the sea. He took out a cigarette, struck a match and lighted it, then puffed gently at it, grailually growing more calm. The sea• lay at his feet—a great, black, rolling mass, with a white line of waves beating regularly upon the shingle. He watched a few moving lights far away from the land, then a voice broke suddenly into his reverie:</p>
        <p>"Hallo, old fellow, what are you doing here ?" It was a golfing friend of Guy's, an English- man, who, like himself, was taking an evening stroll.</p>
        <p>Now a few days back these two young men had bad a rather severe altercation on the cliffs over an important golf question. A gendarme and a few passers-by had .watched with interest the hot- tempered young Englishmen, who had not been able to make up their quarrel, but had parted with indignant words. The onlookers had shaken their heads gravely, wondering what would be the end of such a violent quarrel. ,</p>
        <p>His anxiety about Rachel made Guy long for sympathy from almost his only English friend in that foreign town ; so, anxious to forgive 'and forget, he said to his friend : "Come for a walk, .Rivers."</p>
        <p>Martin Rivers had a kind heart. He knew by the tone of his friend's voice that something was troubling him, so he muttered rather gruffly : ‘,‘Shake hands, old fellow, you have something on your mind. Let uS see if I can help you in any way." Guy willingly shook his friend's hand, and they left the sea and walked silently through the town. That dark figitre still, followed then'. They paused away from the houses on the edge of the cliff. Below them was the sea, and all about them lay gorse bushes, golden and heavy with blossoms, denoting their presence in the dim light by a rich, peach-like scent. The dark figure came to a stand- still behind a gorse bush. Then Guy poured out his troubles to his friend, Martin Rivers.</p>
        <p>"Martin," he said; slightly 'raising his voice above the noise of the Ivaves, "I believe that she and I are meant for each other. Surely nothing can ever part us." His friend was about to answer him. There was a monie”t's stillness, then a sound of the report of a firearm, some smoke, a flash of light, a whizzing sound through the air, a cry of pain, and Martin Rivers was lying stretched upon the ground. Guy stood perfectly still for a moment.. He took one hasty look round, but no one was in sight. He bent down by his friend's still form.</p>
        <p>"Martin," he cried, "old chap, what has haKsened ? Are you badly hurt?"</p>
        <p>No answer. The little waves still lapped and cooed and whispered among themselves below, and a faint wind • rustled through the gorse bushes. Guy struck a match and held it near his friend's face.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>"Good God 1" he exclaimed. "He is deatl</head>
        <p>"Good God 1" he exclaimed. "He is deatl</p>
        <p>'The bullet had entered at a vital point, and the dark earth beneath his head was drenched with blood.</p>
        <p>Then a sort of frenzy came over Guy. He was young and inexperienced. The sudden blow was too great for him. He did not realise, he could not understand that his friend was dead. .</p>
        <p>He rubbed the cold hands, he unfastened the wet collar, he did all in his power for him. "I must go for help," he said, aloud, staggering to his feet.</p>
        <p>At last realisation began to creep into his mind as he stood looking down upon the awful .stillness of the prostrate figure, and a feeling of agony seemed to cut right into his heart. . "I shall be suspected," be muttered, "because of that quarrel, and no one knows that we made it up to-night. They will call me the murderer. I have the marks of blood upon my hands. They will not believe my words when I say • I am an innocent man. I had better die too. My poor Rachel! My love! My love!" Then he stood upon the very edge of the cliff looking down upon the dark water. What could be easier than to die there at once, alone and unseen? Would not his death prevent an infinite amount of pain and uncertainty both for Rachel and himself? Then he glanced up to the sky, and he saw a gentle, grave star looking straight down upon him. It brought a vision of Rachel's sweet, pure face to his mind. She had promised to trust him always; she had repeated the word over to him many times. Great sobs shook his frame.. " I will not be a coward," he said. "I. will at least do what I can to free myself from the false accusation which is sure to fall upon me. .I will get away. I will leave the place at once. I will tell them first at that cottage -near that there is a dead man's body to be buried." Guy had &amp;en very little of the dark side of life. This was the first great crisis, the first real pain that he had ever known, and it was fast bringing out the noble side of his character. He determined to act so as to save another from as much pain as possible. He turned away from the sea, but the minutes had florwn. He was too late ! Steps were coming down the road from the town; lights, and voices, the regular tramp of feet, and the clanging of swords were fast drawing near. . .</p>
        <p>Guy's first impulse was to run in the opposite direction, but he stopped himself at once. "If I fly and they chase me like a fox or a hare, they will at once think me guilty. No, I will walk quietly towards them and tell them what has happened. lam innocent, and I nnist not act the part of a murderer." The steps grew quite close, and four gendarmes stopped in front of him. "Monsieur," said the officer of the party, "a gentleman who was on this road a few minutes ago has just sent us here. He heard a shot fired, and a cry. He said be saw you shoot, and then throw the revolver over the cliff. Monsieur, he described your figure to me, and surely it is you I saw qmirrelling a few days ago on the cliffs with another Englishman?" "I have seen no one," said Guy. "I am an innocent man. There is the dead body. A shot was. fired suddenly when I was standing by him on the edge of the cliff. What gentleman informed you of this? "Monsieur, I do not know; a stranger, possibly a traveller passing by this spot on his way between two towns. Probably he was seeking a night's shelter in one of our hotels ; he was horrified, he directed us to come here:" The gendarme bent down over the still-form. • "And here-is the mark of the bullet," he said, "and, Mon Dieu! I recognise, without doubt, the same gentleman with whom you were quarrelling the other day, • and we find you here, as the gentle- man described. I fear it will be a hard case for you, monsieur,' a very hard case; you will come with us at once." •</p>
        <p>"CErtainly," said Guy, "I must come with you, but lam innocent, thank God! I am an innocent man." '</p>
        <p>Two of the men stopped behind with the body, the others marched one on each side of Guy, down the lonely road and through the town. "Stop,'" cried Guy, with a look of agony on his face, as they passed the hotel. facing the sea; he realised how- hard his case would be. 'Unless the true culprit chose to come forward, he, Guy, was sure to be accused as the murderer of his friend. He hoped for justice, for a trial which would result in his innocence lfeing proved, and yet he saw, as the officer of the gendarmes had said, that it would be a hard case, a very hard case. "I must leave a message, a note, anything for— for one whom I love; you will not .deprive me of this one comfort ? "</p>
        <p>Guy felt for paper .and pencil, he had only a pencil; in desperation he tore off his cuff and wrote a few words upon it. "To be given to Miss Rachel Herbert: "A sad thin. has happened to me, darling; lam wrongly 'accuse'd of a wicked action; I trust all will come right. Trust me, dear one, as you prontiped ; your belief in my innocence will be my one comfort and support. God bless you, and help us both.—From Guy." The officer calmly read the liastily written lines, then handed the cut! to the porter at the door of the hotel.</p>
        <p>The little procession passed on under Rachel's window, and a terrible weight of grief seemed to bow down Guy's spirit and to chill his very heart.</p>
        <p>She had been cruising in the Mediterranean about the Italian coast, that gay little yacht with her white sails and graceful movements. Her behaviour had been perfect for many days, but one fatal even- ing she forgot all her past good reputation and hopelessly sullied it by catching fire. She lay far from land, gently rising up and down upon the swell, with flames flying up from her cabin and terrible- panic on board. They did what they -could for her, but it was all of no use; the passengers and crew had been obliged to take refuge in the small boats and to leave her to her fate. It was a quiet evening, with just a soft regular breeze coining from the land; the sea was still and blue, a very dark blue, just after the sun had set ; it had left a red glow in the western sky, and the brave little yacht, which had done good service to her owner, was rapidly burn- ing. The angry flames leaped out seawards, and a lurid light shone upon those who had escaped from the fire in the boats. In one of them lay a sick man, a burning beam had fallen upon him and fatally injured him : he was the owner of the yacht, and now he was dying, and likely to pass away very soon, perhaps at the same moment as his vesseL In the bows sat an oldish man and a young girl watching the burning yacht.. It was Rachel Herbert and her father.</p>
        <p>• Almost immediately after the arrest of Guy Dorrington, M. Petitjean had persuaded Mr. Herbert and his daughter toogo for a cruise with him in his yacht. Mr. Herbert bad gladly -agreed to the plan, and this was the end of it—the Owner of the vessel was dying, and the pretty little yacht hei self was also fast passing away. And Rachel, poor broken-hearted Rachel, she was passin. through great. trouble. Guy was now in prison after his trial,awaiting his fate. M. Petitjean had given his evidence against, him, and he had been found guilty. Rachel had prayed for him`; she had done all in her power for him, but it was of no. use. Guy was to die far away from her, and yet she trusted him ; she had written to tell him so. She would have gladly died on the burning yacht, but the boats had been at hand, and Rachel had hc.en the first to be lifted into one of them. She :at now in the bows, pale, still, almost stunned with grief.</p>
        <p>• " Irachel, M. Petit jean wishes to speak to you he is very ill, 1 don't think he can live long.' It was Mr. :Herbert speaking, he touched his daughter's arm. "Rachel, go .to him, poor fellow, he is dying, and is very anxious to speak to you." Rachel silently obeyed. She bent down close to the still form lying in the bottom of the boat ; she bated the Frenchman, he had come between .her and her lover, he had given evidence against him; lloW he had managed to do so she could never make out ; she had refused to marry him, and yet he had still worried her and pained her beyond measure. "Rachel," gasped the Frenchman—already his face wore a •death-like pallor—" I am very 'weak, but I will struggle to say what I wish, what must be said ; 1 cannot die without telling the truth ; lain wrctched now ; ma fol, why did. I -do that dreadful deed! "</p>
        <p>The boat rose gently up and down as she cut through the ivater towards the land, and Rachel s ill thought of Guy in his solitude. "Rachel," gasped M. Petitjean, catching hold of the girl's hand and holding it in a feeble, 'nervous grasp, "I am the murderer ; I did it ; it was jealousy, e!tild, my longing to have you as my wife, my little queen of the château and of my tich3a, au...1 I crept that night, that dark night, after yoa and Guy Dorrington ; I watched you, t tilatil your steps, then I stole lon the cliff with</p>
        <p>my revolver. I meant the shot for Guy, but it missed and hit his companion. It was I, child, I tell you again; I am the murderer, and I sent the gendarmes to take him." He had raised his voice and his words seemed to pierce through the gloom and weight of sorrow which had bowed down Rachel's fair head.</p>
        <p>"What do you mean ?" she almost screamed. "What have you done? Guy is suffering instead of you; my Guy, my own darling! And now you are dying and will not be able to set it right! 0 my dying, ! Great God in Heaven, have mercy, help us, 'oh, help us!" She was standing up now in a perfect agony of excitement, which was almost as terrible to witness as her previous hopelessness and despair. Then her own sweet common sense seemed suddenly to return to her; she trembled still with excitement, but surely her prayer had been answered, that wild, earnest cry from the very depths of her soul. "You must write it all out," she said, in hurried tones. "My father and the sailors, there are two in the boat, they must witness all ; you are very feeble, be quick; oh, say you will do it? And Guy; 0 Guy, you shall be saved! His end is not. far off, M. Petitjean, but if we are quick we may yet save him. We will do it; he shall be saved."</p>
        <p>It was done. The dying man with almost pain- ful force of will feebly dictated a few lines shewing that he was the culprit., that Guy was innocent. Mr. Herbert and two a the sailors witnessed his signature, and then dropping the pencil from his hand, M. Petitjean died, and his fair yacht passed away almost at the same time ; she drifted away seawards, moved on by the gentle land breeze—a sad, black, charred object, no longer beautiful and buoyant.</p>
        <p>They 'were leading him to his execution, Guy Dorrington, the young barrister, in the midst of his strength and manhood. He was crossing the prison courtyard, between two warders, to his doom --young, strong and grave, very grave, and perfectly quiet. Little sparrows were chirping and 'hopping about his path, the morning sunlight was creeping across the high, grim walls down into the gloomy yard, upon the little birds and the prisoner. Ho smiled a very faint, sad smile as he watched the busy little creatures ; he thought they were his last sight of life—that beautiful, fresh, strong life, which he loved. All, how he loved it! He had tried to subdue that great love for it in his lonely cell, he had endeavoured to look forward bravely and cheerfully to the future, to the other side ot the grave, but he had not been successful in his struggle. Ile had never before realised how much he loved and valued life.</p>
        <p>A hasty step overtook the melancholy procession, the sparrows were all put to flight, and a panting, breathless messenger stood beside the warders.</p>
        <p>"Stop," he cried. "The prisoner must not die, he will be released; fresh evidence, greatly in his favour, has been suddenly brought to light.' He unrolled- and 'sliewed to the warders an official document. Guy was led back to his cell, hardly knowing or realising what had happened to him. Then that life so dear to him was not. yet to be snatched away from, him, not just yet, and he might think again of his love, perhaps lie might soon see her, and fresh hope gradually dawned upon him and seemed to lighten his prison cell.</p>
        <p>He was a free man once more. He met Rachel outside the prison. She had travelled night and day in order to save him • he had been allowed .one interview with her just before his release, but now, they were united, and their love was strengthened—if that were possible—by the long, terrible separation. "I trusted you," murmured Rachel, resting her small white hands on Guy's shoulders. "You told me to trust you, and I did all through, Guy, during those awful days—too terrible to be spoken of; and father is satisfied, dear, he is not against our plans." Guy could scarcely speak. "My dear," he said, with a great sob of joy shaking his voice, "then you can trust me, and believe in me,always ; you said that word before, Rachel, and it has been my comfort through weeks of pain and sorrow; will you say it again, just once again ? " • She looked up at him, a beautiful smile shining through her tears. "Darling, always," she murmured. "Yes, always." [Tr. END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>7%IODISE</head>
        <p>A 7%IODISE</p>
        <p>HAT:</p>
        <p>in front and caught up at the waist by a band of navy blue patent leather._ The large hat is of navy blue furry-surfaced felt, and is trimmed only by some blueish-green flat_ feathers ,and a few twists of green velvet under the brim in froat. The toilette is ex- ceedingly smart and up-to-date in cut and fashion and looks most stylish. Brown • cloth would also look very well trimmed with the fur and with pipings or cordings of silk of the same colour. It is a noticeable fact thi:, season that every well dressed woman who merits the title `-` smart " has abandoned • ior outdoor wear the gaily coloured silk under- skirts which have hitherto been so .popular, and while eschewing likewise those of lawn and lace 4ppears, sensibly clad in a much frilled black silk skirt. This is a much more sensible plan for nothing looks so ugly as a faded dirty petticoat of once • delicately coloured silk. The modest hat which I have here de- picted is of a very becoming shape in Smooth left trimmed with a silk ruche. The , hat sketched was of red felt with a black silk ruche and a bow of black ye:vet ribbon at the back. , It fits the head closely at the back in the approved style and projects over the face in front. • The; brim is bound with narrow black velvet. The marquise or, tricorne hat is still very fashionable, and will hold its own among the smartest styles during the winter. A particularly stylish hat of this shape I recently -saw was of white felt with the brim covered with sable, and was worn with a flat sable necklet trimmed with humerous tails and a large flat muff of the granny order. Fur appears in the form of tails and little heads on many hats, and toques, while as bands it is also much in evidence.</p>
