      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHRISTMAS CARDS, CALENDARS, ETC.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS CARDS, CALENDARS, ETC.</p>
        <p>"Chr:stmas is coining, the goose is getting fat "—and I Messrs. Raphael, Tuck and Son's magnificent selection I of Christmas cards, calendars, postcards, and art novel- ties are already on sale. It would be " painting the lily " for us toi recommend the house of Tuck and its , productions. Neither need recommendation, but we I are sure our readers will be desirous of getting an ink- ling of what specialities the famous firm has prepared I for the coming festive senson. We have received a I sample box front Raphael House, and the contents them- selves are an ample indication that Messrs. Tuck's high artistic and original standard has been maintained, • while the bulky catalogue almost embarrasses one with the contemplation of riches. I Among the high art productions is a handsome panel bearilig a head by the late M. Asti. Its delightful vand-yke brown tones, which are again suggested in its ample mount, fit it for framing and mural decoration in almdst any type of apartment. Next comes a series of sporting calendars representing vivacious scenes, in which horses and stylish vehicles and smait people play a prominent part. They are the very thing for the bill iTird room."Home,Sweet Home,"is the title of another calendar of the type containing a quotation for every day in the year. It is decorative, and the quotations are apt. Our space Would not permit our doing justice to the large variety of cards, and we must content ourselves by dealing with the absolute novelties, or, rather, the most striking ones, for all are, novel. There are some splendid effects in red • and 'black, notably three men playing bridge, the glowing reflec- tion of a tire being admirably suggested. There is a billiard-playing card, ntitled. "An awful fluke." a mirthful item to send to a friend whose cue is habitually sympathetic. The post card selections are almost unlimited. Series No. 1,957 represents children of th e Brownie type playing pranks: and Series No. 1,966 is decorated with holds of fair women. Gains- borough in.. type, and almost silverpoint like in execution.</p>
        <p>Children's books for little ones of all ages are legion, and a happy thought is the post-card painting book, by Hilda Cols-ham ; the pages being perforated, the cards after being painted in accordance with the examples an be detached and posted. Father Tuck's annual is ost as it always is, full of beautiful and glad thines or the child-heart. In fact, to say that Messrs. Tuck's election for this year eques those of. the past is now he highest praise one can offer.</p>
