        <p>authorised to complete the final arrangements for the transfer of the two war vessels. The battle;• ship 3 are declared by technical experts to be more powerful both in artillery and armour protection, having regard to their size, than any other warships - afloat., . • The Libertad has already -completed very satisfactory deep- sea speed trials, having succeeded in maintain- ing . a speed of seventeen and a quarter knots for twenty-seven hours continuously. The</p>
        <p>THE LIBERTAD, WHICH IS OF THE SAME TYPE</p>
        <p>AS THB CONSTITUTION.</p>
        <p>battery of each of the new ships comprises four io in. guns of the latest pattern, firing a 450 lb. shell, and capable of piercing 31 in. of steel ; fourteen 7.5 in. guns of great length, firing a zoo lb. shell, and capable of piercing 29 in. of steel ; and fourteen i4-pounders guns. In pro- tection these ships have a belt of 7 in. steel amidships, tapering to 3 in. at the ends of the vessel. The 10 in. guns are in two barbettes plated with io in. Krupp steel ; and the 7.5 in. guns are behind 7 in. armour.</p>
