      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>THIBET EXPEDITION.</head>
        <p>THE THIBET EXPEDITION.</p>
        <p>Really little is known in England about mur latest war—its cause, its prospects er its reason. All, in fact, we, as the public know, is that we are sending a column up to Lhassa, the capital of Thibet, under Colonel Younghusband's command. Lord Kitchener's organisation, the latter is sufficient guarantee --for most Englishmen, however little they know about the subject: and with his usual dislike to war-correspondents 'sublicity, Lord Kitch- ener has refused to allow any journalists to accompany the expedition. Thus, we shall be entirely dependant upon official sources for information, as the papers will not be supplied -with information in the ordinary way, The expedition itself is wrapped in considerable mystery from start to finish and a good many people are inclined to grumble at it already and to -carp at its probable cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WEDDING OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE WEDDING OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>A. very brilliant marriage, which exci ed much interest, was that at St. James's, Piccadilly, on Saturday, the contracting parties being the Hon. William Mrs. Keppell, a si•Aer of Lord Saltoun,. and Admiral Sir Henry Stephenson, First and Principal Na..val A.D.C., and Extra Equerry to the King. The church was beautifully decorated for the occasion. Captain Faussett, R.N., Equerry to the Prince of Wales, was best Man. The bride, who was given away by her brother,</p>
        <p>ADMIRAL SIR HENRY AND LADY STEPHENSON. Lord Saltoun, wore a gown of dove grey satin, richly embroidered in shaded grey paillettes and draped with lace, and a grey velvet toque, with garniture of grey roses. There was no 'formal reception after the ceremony, only relatives of both families meeting at 18 Lowndes Street. subsequently the newly married couple left for Amport, St. Mary's, Andover, the seat of the Marquis of Winchester. The numerous presents included: From the King, an embossed silver bowl, inscribed, "To Admiral Sir Henry Stephenson, G.C.V.0., K.C.8., on the occasion of his marriage, sth December, 1903, from his old friend, Edward „ ; from Queen Alexandra, an electric lamp. with shade, and a small Empire table. . • PROG.RESS` OF FREEMASONRY. • The fact that Freemasonry has now no fewer than 1,439 lodges in the, provinces and 535 in London suggests that in the near future thc popular query of "Are You .a Mason ? " will have undergone revision into " Are you not a Mason ? " The advance of Freemasonry has not been of a mushroom char- acter ; in fact, its progress at times, in genera- tions gone by, though always sure, has been so sluggish as to arouse the belief among out- siders, who had not troubled to find out the real cause of rigor- ous 'restrictions as to member- ship, that the</p>
        <p>movement had 11R. H. FITZHERBERT WRIGHT, reached that zenith which is believed to mark the beginning Of decay in all successful enter prises. Freemasonry, in fact, relies on its slow progress, and it is not expected that the coming iestival of the Royal Masonic Benevolent Institution will reveal any startling brightening of the state of funds. but it is known that there will be an increase on the income for 1902 ; and when it is considered what a stupendous Itgare that was, it will be conceded that the devotees of the cause are justified in feeling satisfaction. - This Year's total liability in respect of relief is estimated at £22,000, including expenses. of administration and maintenance of the asylum at Croydon. The total income avail- able to meet this outlay, made up of the annual grants by grand lodge and grand chaptor and the interest on invested capital, will reach about 1205,000. For.the important office of treasurer this year Mr. H. Fitz-Herbert Wright, J.P., 'D.L., the popular Derbyshire colliery owner, -who will be remembered. ds the man who suc- .ceeded in reducing the hitherto impregnable majority of Sir Walter Foster in the Ilkeston Division, and Mr. Edwards, the well-known London auctioneer, have been nominated. Mr. Fitz-Herbert Wright may be looked upon as elected already, for, apart from his indisputa- ble qualifications for filling the onerous position satisfactorily, it is considered quite time that the provinces should again supply the treasurer, Lon'don having had this honour eight years during the past twelve, and London's number ,of lodges being so small in comparison to those of the provinces.</p>
