      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>OUR NEW CHILIAN BATTLESHIPS.</head>
        <p>OUR NEW CHILIAN BATTLESHIPS.</p>
        <p>The purchase by the Admiralty at the in- clusive price of 1,875,000, of the two battle- ships, Libertad and Constitucion, under -construction for the Chilian Government at Elswick and Barrow, is generally conceded to be a good bargain: In February last it was stated in the House of Commons that the Admiralty were not prepared to purchase these ships, the price, 2,200,000, being too much, and Russia .and Japan were said to be would-be purchasers. It is possible that the purchase Was made in order to prevent awkward questions arising in case either of the two Powers now at variance Should have endeavoured to buy them. The example of the Alabama shows the danger of sales of warships, though the Alabama was sold to the Confederate Government when war was actually in progress. The negotiations for the sale were conducted primarily at Santiago, and the Chilian Legation in London was atm</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CORRUPTION OF THE GERMAN 'ARMY.</head>
        <p>CORRUPTION OF THE GERMAN 'ARMY.</p>
        <p>The world is very much indebted to Lieutenant Bilse for his valuable exposition of life in the German Army, and though his startling ex- posures have cost him his freedom for a time there can be little doubt that he feels perfectly satisfied with , himself in hay- ing been the first to show what corruption ex- ists in German military circles. Undoubtedly Germany's best course would have been to ignore "In a Little Garrison Town" as an exaggeration too palpable to be treated with anything but silent contempt, but it seems the cap fitted too LIEUTE.NA NT EMS well- to avoid a</p>
        <p>panic, and, al- though Lieutenant Bilse had resigned his commission, he was brought before a court- martial at Metz. The defence was that Lieu- tenant Bilse's 'motive was to bring about reform in the army, which he admired, and which he only left in ,order to be free to write the book. He was sentenced to six months'. imprisonment, his book being confiscated and destroyed. The book tells how an entire battalion is under the control of the wife of one of the captains, who is also the mistress of the colonel. Holding this double position, the woman uses her power to forward the interests of the officers she likes, to ruin those she dislikes, and to promote the ad- vancement of her , husbnd, ambition, and not love, leading her to this course. The colonel has delivered himself into her ha n ds,having associated himself with her in a lie. Besides the colonel, two lieutenants sharelier favours ; by means of one of them she cripples her husband, and with the other, whom she induces to desert, she elopes. The other officers mainly figure in the book as forgers, swindlers, and perjurers. The characters Nyere perhaps a little over-drawn, but it is in- teresting to note that they were truthful enough for certain officers of thi- garrison of Forbach, where Lieutenant lilse was quartered, to recognise their portraits.</p>
        <p>"PICKWICK. M.P."</p>
        <p>In - politics, with the exception of Mr. Chamberlain, no more paragraphed person exists than Mr. Alfred Davies, -Member for. Carmarthen Boroughs. As for nicknames and names he has been called in print, he has a , very long list of- them, which just over a year ago he publish- ed for the public edification and amusement. his speeches, too, are always wel- Hcoornuesde ~ oifn Cotrnhe. mons, as he is by way of being • a humorist; and anything which , livens up the droning dreari- ness of political life is always ac- ceptable at Wes tminster. Meanwhile,how- ever, " Mr. Pic- - wick, M.P.," as he i 4 most popu- larly called,fincis MR. AI-RED DAVIES, M.P. himself some- what freely cri- ticised . in his, own constituency ' by Mr. Thomas . Terrell, K.C., a leading., Liberal, who claims to represent local Liberalism more thoroughly than Mr. Davies. He created great amusement and no sm.ill sensation by producing a number of private letters from the sitting member to his "committee of six," who worked privately in his interests. A small annual subscription and a box of cigars apiece at Christmas were the terms mentioned, and for that consideration ' these six gentlemen lacted as unofficial agents for Mr. Davies and forwarded his interests in the constituency. Now there appears to have been something of a split: hence these revelations. Naturally, the whole thing has been very much talked about, principally owing to the public interest in Mr. Davies. ..</p>
        <p>131\ Ji.. -- 4., At •</p>
        <p>4 . ,L ezfl cw*- , 44/i....5., ; 4L''''' Ntf tAf /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December l2, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WAR BLUNDERS.</head>
        <p>THE WAR BLUNDERS.</p>
        <p>BY Ell MACNAMARA, M.P.</p>
        <p>I will ask you to carry your minds back to January 1900. The Schtth African War had been raging three months ; 6,00 of the flower of the British Army 1;y 'detd upsin the fields of Glencoe, Belmont, Graspao, Molder River. Stormberg, Magersfontein, and Colenso. Ladysmith, Kimberley, and Mafeking . were invested. Haller, Gatacre' and Mathuen had been rapulsad. Penn- Symons, Wanchopa, and mmy another gallant hero was ntun!)ared aa )n,7, the slain. We were all of us lAt..erl disappointed and deeply depressed. NV() were mor,,. We were amaize•l at the stupendous dimensions of the forces against us. Why did we not know'?_ Those</p>
        <p>" Long tAni !"HwMIS it we were not advised of their existence? Arr. Balfour goes down to his con- stituents in Erzt Man.-±ester on January Bth, MOO. As to the question of the startling nature of the Boer preparations for the war, why blame the Cabinet ? Said he :</p>
        <p>This was not a question on which the Government had, could have, or ever pretended to have, special 1112a113 of information. The man in the street knew as mucha,s the man in the Cainnet. " Taerefore gentle- men, I do not feel the need, so far as my colleagues or I are concerned, of any apology whatever ! " The other dray (Friday, November 27th) at the United Club dinner at 'the Hotel Cecil, Mr. Balfour adopted precisely the same line of defence. "It was bucaus3," said he, " the tag': before us proved to he greater, far greater, than any critic, military or civilian, ever suggested, that what are now called the deplorable War 0 flee blunders had their origin. Therefore, again, gentlemen. I am not going to make the slightest apology, or attempt the smallest excuse, for anything that went w:ong in the</p>
        <p>• war." Well. now, --1$ 113 see. T tire t 3 Appeadix A. page 155, of the Report of the Royal Commission appointed to inquire into the military preparations and other matters connected with the war in South Africa. (This report is numbered 1,789, and is, priced at 2s. 7d. It can be obtained through any bookseller,or from Messrs. Eyre and Spottiswoode, East Harding-street, Fleet- street E.C. it should be on. the - table of every public library, and, mice), in every genuinely p ttriotic lishman's- home.) I say. I turn to pige 135. I find there is a series of most, remarkable " Notes" on the military situation in the Traesvaal and the Free State, covering the years 1816-7-8 and 1833. Never before, I should say, in the history of civilised war- fare, was a Government so fully and accur- ately advised as t 3 the nature and propor- tions :• of the forces against it. in Oatober 1893, Sit- John Ardagh and his Tritellig- °ace Department advise the G3vern-nent that the F33ers are spending ,C 2,330,030 on military pre- parations, "ir.,aluding :the provision of artillery, rifles, ammunition, and fortifi.3ations." They add that this " large 'expenditure can have no other explanation than an anticipation of war." From time to time similar specific facts are forwarded, to be pigeon-holed in Pall Mall. That fine. soldier. Sir William. Butler, was in military commtni at the Cape from November, 1898, to August, 1811:1. On January 25th, 1839, he warned the Government that we were drifting into a conflict" the ultimate consequences of which no one can adequately estimate." Numberless communications passed between him and the Government. In one despatch he tells them that "the Boers will fight to the very last mart if you menace their independence." Again he says: The women will help, and the children will fight"; and so on In his evidence before the Royal Co:umis- sion he says (Report, Vol. 11., page 85) : _ . -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>• THE NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>• THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>• THE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>NORTIIU.NIBERLAND AND DURLIA.AI V. LANCASHIRO.</p>
        <p>This championship match was played at South Shields on Wednesday, before about 1,500 spectators. The visiting county opened strongly, and from a pass, Wilson scored a try, but no goal gresulted. The game had barely been restarted, when Lancashire again forced play, and a scrummage being formed under the home posts Lomas got over with a second try for Lancashire no goal again resulting. Some capital play on tne part of Lancashire ended in Fish scoring, the. same player easily converting. Lancashire continued to have the best of the Way, and Wilson put on another try, the kick at goal failing. fora,timethe homecounty played up ninch better, but failed to reach the visiting line. Liancashire afterwards came away, and Tanney dropped aer the line, no goal resulting. From near the centre Fish obtained a splendid goal. Lancashire continued to have the best of the play up to the interval. Half- time :—Lancashire, 19 points ; Durham and North- umberland, nothing. After the interval the visitors again pressed strongly. Fish scored in the corner, the kick at goal failing. The home county made strenuous efforts to reach the half- way line, but their efforts proved futile, and 140111/1S added another try for the visitors, Fish easily convert- ing. While play was in the visitors' territory Fish received from Baxter, and, running nearly the full length of the field, scored under the posts, the same player easily converting. Lancashire continued to have the best of matters, their forwards playing a dashing game. When near the centre Hogg got the ball and dodged past a host of players before he threw to George Board- man, who easily scored, Lomas converting. On restart- ing Lancashire again forced play, and some splendid passing was witnessed amongst the visitors' three- quarters, Fish just being prevented from scoring by Ritson., Hogg received near the corner and romped over with the tenth try, Lomas being successful with, the goal kick. The combined counties were thoroughly beaten, and played a loose, disjointed game, the team being completely rearranged from that which, met Cumberland, changes necessitating the playing of nine Shieldsmen. Result :,-Lancashiie, six goals and ten tries (42 points); Durham and Northumberland, nothing. , The following were the teams .</p>
        <p>Lancashire.-" Smith (Salford), back; Fish (Warring- ton), Wilson and Hogg (Broughton Rangers), three- quarter backs; LOMS, (Salford) and Baxter (Rochdale ,Hornets), half backs; Boardruan (Leigh), Tnnney, Heath, and Buckler (Salford), Ferguson (Oldham) and Boardman (Warrington), forwards. Durham and Northumberland.—Gurr (Shields), back; Ritson (Hull), Thompson, and Mawhood. three-quarter backs; 31`Cann and Ellis, half backs; Cox, Peat, New- man, and Sowerby (Shields), Shaw, and Bell, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WARRINGTON v. WIGAN.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Wilderspool on Sat,urday for the first time this season. Both had absentees, NVarrington being short of Isherwood and Harris, while Wigan were withont Anderson. lough the morning had been fine and clear,a fog came on after_ noon, and at the time for kicking off it was only just possible to see across the ground. Play proceeded however, the ' got worse, I and during the last 30 minutes only brief glimpses of the players could be obtained from the touch-line. There were about 6,000 spectators present, several hundred having c,ome from Wigan. Mr. Kennedy was the referee, and the teams were as follows : Teams : Warrington: J. Hallam, back; Hockenhall, Dickenson, Mahoney and Fish', t hreequarters ; Davies and Bate half backs; Morrison, Boardmaga, Thomas, Cook, Jolly, Edulundson, Naylor and Swift, forwards. Wigan: Winstanley, back; Cooper, Harris, Rees and Sharrock, threequarters; .Brown and Davies, half hacks;, Collier, Brown, Hilton, Barton, Wolfenden, Hatton, Whitaker and Halliwell, forwards. Wigan started, and Hockenhall immediately got away and kicked down to the Wigan line, where Davies pissed to Mahoney, who made a strong run, Rees- and IVinstanley holding the Warringtonian up when he was. in the act of grounding. Wigan again had another narrow escape, and profiting by this inability to put on the finishing touches by the • locals, the visiting vanguard rushed the packs and Warrington were , perforce compelled to fall back to neutral quarters. From a scrimmage Davies (Wigan) dribbled cleverly through the IVarrington backs, and crossing to Hilton, ! , Hallam was beaten, and the first tangible record wasl , notched by the visitors. No goal followed. The reverse roused the Warringtonians, for a round of passing gave Winstanleytrouble,.and after Hockenhall had punted to the line the Warrington hrwards took the ball over, and Cook equalized. A desperate off art was next made by Fish, who repeiving from Bate, was pushed into touch at the corner, whilst the next minute Davies was held up on the line. Wigan rallied, and a clever bout of passing almost ended in a try by Harris, Bate effecting a fine tackle. Warrington got the ball safely out of danger until near the interval, when Winstanley essayed a futile shot at goal. The teams crossed over with a try each. Fora long period in the second half, play was waged on the reserve side, but from what could be gathered from the shouts of the spectators Wigan was pressing hard, and eventually the visitors scored through the medium ofj Barton, who took advantage of some bad mulling by the home backs. 'Winstanley failed at an easy place kick. Warrington pressed immediately on re- starting from centre, and a score looked imminent when Dickenson dribbled away, and hadiapparently a clear course. The Wigan custodian, however, effected aline tackle. Warrington maintained the pressure, andafter Fish had been bulked by Cooper near the corner, he kicked across to Dickenson, who took the ball in fine style, and •although he slipped in making a dash for the line he quickly recovered himself,and doubling past Winstanley and Rees scored in a goodposition, for Fish to plabe the goal. Having gained the lead the " Wire- pullers " made the pace a cracker, and Wigan were forced on their lines,and in thelast few minutes Board- man managed a kick and scored a third try. Warrington thus winning by a goal and three tries (eleven points) to Wigan two tries (six points.) . BROUGHTON RANGERS v. 'HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>A well-contested game was played by these teams on the Rangers' ground, which ultimately ended in a draw of three points each. A fine passing and •repassing movement, however. between Riley, Williams And Hartley was the means of Halifax scoring. Widdeson from a pass by Wilson scored a try, which S. James failed to improve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SALFORD v. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SALFORD v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>This match, on the Salford ground, was stopped early in the second half through fog, and will have to be re- played on a future date. During the first half Salford had shown much superior f. rm to Runcorn, and had scored a goal and a try. This was the work of Preston, who followed up a forward rush and grounded the ball over the line close to the posts.. Lomas easily placed a goal. Jolley and Butterworth several times got their side out of difiiculties,but right up to half time Salford held the upper hand. Ends were changed with tha score five points to nothing in favour of Salford. The fog in the second half became so thick that it was - im- possible to follow the play, and after a consultation with the touch judges the referee decided to abandon the game. ' league</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HUNSLET v. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>HUNSLET v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>At Parkside, in foggy weather, before ~2,009 .people. Spottiswood rad- two tries for Oldham before the finish, and the visitors won by six points to nothing.</p>
        <p>LEIGH V. LEEDS</p>
        <p>I Played at Leigh, in a thick fog.- .Leeds had the best of the play in the first few minutes, and after some , clever kicking and a fine dribble by the forwards Grace made an opening for Davis, who feigned to pass and then ran in a fine try. The Leigh forwards rushed the ball to the Leeds end, and after Sinnott had almost scored Johnson made his.mark and Orrell•placed .a goal. The latter stages of the game were in favour of Leeds, Final :—Leeds, one try (three points); Leigh, one goa two points).</p>
        <p>I. .KEIGHLEY V. HUDDERSFIELD. These teams mot at Eel ghteY in dull weather and before 3,000 speetatoth. The game which ended in semi-dark- ness, resulted in a win for Huddersfield by one try (thre3 points) .to nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>BATLEY v. BRADFORD</head>
        <p>BATLEY v. BRADFORD</p>
        <p>An evenly contested galue at Batley resulted in the victory of the home side by one goal (two points) to nothing. In the first half Tnrton dropped a goal for Batley, this being the only score in the match. Brad- ford attacked in determined fashion after .the interval, but Batley showed asplendid defence. The match was finished in semi-darkness.</p>
      </div>
