      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>16-8-39 -Si ONE.</head>
        <p>16-8-39 -Si ONE.</p>
        <p>Strange holding, isn't it ? Mrs. Dixoa, of Crost- wight, East Norfolk, knows what a lot it rnelns. She tried some sixteen various 'remedies' for indigestion and biliousness in vain, had had eight doctors, and suffered for thirty yefixs.. Her husband couldn't smoke his pipe in the house because the smoke brought on her biliousness. Then she tried one more remedy—has. Forde's bile beans, and was cured. _ _</p>
        <p>Crostwight is Only a little village. Mrs. Dixon has lived there thirty-seven years, so her case has naturally aroused- ConSiderable interest. Reader if you are bilious or have spells of digestive disorder, or headache, if you can't eat Much, and when you do eat, your food repeats with a nastyacid taste, just rememberj,his—Nlrs. Dixon suffered all that -3,70tt are suffering for thirty years. To-day she's cured. You've tried so. many things? That's just what she Said ! Have you tried Chas. worde's bile beans? She, hadn't at one time, and suffered. ,She has now, and is well. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>• Do you think her case is widely different from your own? Bile Beans differ from all other preparations in one important particular—they don't merely relieve, they cure! All chemists stock them at one and three- half-pence or two and nine per box (family size). Don't take substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>BY-ELECTION IN BEW3EY WARD.</head>
        <p>THE BY-ELECTION IN BEW3EY WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr. Peter Peacock Returnei.</p>
        <p>To fill the vacancy oh. the Warrington Town Council, caused by the death of Councillor- Brophy, an election took place in Bewsey Wardson Nlonday. On the previ- ous Monday, the electors returned Mr. Samuel Gough (N.-T.), who defeated the Liberal candidate, Mr. Omen Toole, by 50 votes. The Liberal Ward Committee determined to do their level best to win the seat on Monday last, and worked strenuously on behalf or Mr. Peter Peacock. He was Opposeg by Mr. James Dickenson, Non-Trader, whose norninotionpaper the Mayor declared 'to be invalid on the ground that it had bean signed by a married woman. _ . .</p>
        <p>The j polling booths were at the mission room, Hoyle. street, and St. Ann's school. Dannett-street. The pre- siding officers were Messrs. C. H. Unsworth, and J. H Fisher, and the poll clerks Messrs. F. H. Wilson and H Essenhigh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>RESULT OF THE POLL.</head>
        <p>RESULT OF THE POLL.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES FRONt THE TOWN HALL STEPS. 'immediately on the ballot boxes being taken to the Town Hall, the counting commenced. It was found that 714 electors had recorded their votes, 277 at Dannett-street and 437 at Silver-street. There were two spoiled papers. The result was as follows Mr. Peacock Mr. Dickenson '312</p>
        <p>Majority</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was tendered to the Returning Officer (Alderman Fairclough) on the proposition of Councillor P. PEACOCK, seconded by Mr. DICKENSON. Loud cheers were raised when the Returning 0 licer called out Mr. Peacock's name from the Town Hall steps. Councillor PETER. PEACOCK, who wig greeted with load cheers, said he thanked the intelligent electors of liewsey Ward for electing him a member of the Town Council, in spit oof all the unfair opposition he had. had at the works where the leader of the purity brigade was employed. He thanked them heartily for the con- fidence reposed in him, and promised not to disgrace them. (Cheers.) Mr. DICKENSON, who also had a hearty reception, thanked the electors for the manner in which they had treated him during the past three days. Comparatively speaking he said, he had only had three days in the field. (A Voice: "That is enough!' and laughter.) His nomination paper went in on the Monday--(a Voice: " Was it. right? ") —and was objected to on the Irue3day. This was.the first time he had ever been in the field. (A Voice: "And the last.") It was the first time he had ever taken plat in a municipal election, but he did not say that it would be the last time. (Applause.) Councillor D3DDS also si»ke. He quoted Byrea's familiar lines— For freedom's battle once begun, Though baffled oft, is ever won.</p>
        <p>They had been fighting he continued, for parity, for righteousness and justice. (Rear, hear.) They were never beaten, and they would go on until .theyhad cleaned the Town Hall of what they complained. (Laughter and cheers.) It was said that the Non- traders were a defeated and a discredited party, but what were the results? -There had been 15 elections since he was returned to the Council on the Ist November last year, and out of the 15 the Non-Traders' had got ten in. (Cheers.) They w.u.e not done with. nor would they be until they had rid the Town Hill of all that was irregular. He was quite sure that the men E' who had voted against them that day would in their F quiet moments regret having voted against parity and justice. (Cheers.) SPEECHES AT THE LIBERAL 'CLUB.</p>
        <p>The successful candidate was escorted to the Liberal • Club by a larg,4, number of his supporters and oeveral speeches were delivered in the large committee room. Councillor PETER PEACOCK whe was loudly cheered, ! thanked his friends for the magnificent assistance they had given him that day. They had worked under ex- ceptional difficulties. The men had been practically told in the forge that if they did not work for ,Dickea- son and vote for him they could not be supportedibut he was pleased to think that the majority of the electors had had the courage and determination to respect the ballot box. (Cheers.) He also thanked the ladies for their assistance, and was sure that they polled more that day 'than at the last election in the ward. They had all no doubt worked harder, belanse of the. crushing defeat .received on the previous Monday. He had no hesitation in 'saying that the next time Mr. Owen Thole put up he would get in. (Cheers.) He thanked Mr. Toole for the assistance he and his Catholic friends had rendered him in the ntest. He '(the speaker) would do his best to merit the confidence reposed in him, although he had only had a majority of 28. (A Voice : "ft is enough.") riliey had saved him the trouble of taking any action with regard to Mr. Dickenson's invalid nomination paper. (Hear, hear.) Mr. OWEN 'I7ooLE Who had • a hearty reception, aid he was satisfied that the defeat received last week had been an object lesson to the committee.- Nothing could have given him greater pleasure that the result of that day's election, except, perhaps, unseating Mr: Gough. (Laughter.) After the way Mr. Peacock worked for him. and the way he (the speaker) had worked for Mr. Peacock, he was sure that neither would be happy until they sat side by side in the Council Chamber. (Ap- plause.) With.their permission he honed to be there before long. (A Voice: " Next time.") He promised Mr. Peacock that he would receive the assistance of his (Mr. Toole's)Catholic friends and he was sure they were all satisfied it had been given and that Mr. Peacock was noW a Councillor. (Applause.) Mr. JAMES WOOOS said they were-disheartened last Monday; that evening they ware elated. (Cheers-) He was talking to a Non-trader that day in Dannett-street and he said "We are going to crush you worse to-day than we did last Monday." (Laughter.) Mr. Woods contended that the Non-traders were not a ourity party owing to theirconduct at •election times. He concluded by moving a vote of thanks to the secretaries of the ward and all the workers, ' councillor .Aarritutt. PEACOCK seconded the vote Of thanks, and said the workers had done yeoman service for his brother and he Was glad their efforts had been row irded with success. 7 The vote was unanhnously agreed to. Mr. E. A. SANnErts. in responding, spoke strongly of the tactics of the Non-Trading party at election times. Mr. NITRtiE also responded and referred to the excel- lent work of the ladies.</p>
      </div>
