      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>• -4, ;31 km.t. e ' larb –</head>
        <p>• -4, ;31 km.t. e ' larb -</p>
        <p>tion until a fortnight after they should have been complied with. T. turn now to the question of the supply of ammuni- tion for small arms at the outbreak of the -war: On page 7-1, Volume I, of the evidence, Sir Henry Bracken- bury goes on to relate that at the close of March, 1899, (16,000000 rounds of Mark IV.cartridges were delivered. It was subsequently found that the bullets in these cartridges had a knack of " stripping" and leaving the nickel envelope behind in the grooving of the rifle. Loaded again in this condition, there would be a blow hack from the breech that, as likely as not, would cut the soldier's eye out, or lay his cheek bare to the bone Consequently, these 66,000,000 rounds of ammunition were withdrawn as "non-effective for purposes of war.' Says Sir Henry Brackenbnry : " Vlre were driven to great straits at one time, because we had actually got reduced in this country to two or three boxes of Mark H. ammunition." And on November 20th, 18)9, eighteen days after the investment of Ladysmith, Mr. Brodrick had to cable to Sir tteclvers Buller : " There is only eight waks' supply of 11 trk Tr. .303 in ball ammunition in the country, and all gun ammuni- tion will be exhausted before eight weeks." And as with the ammunition, so with the other war- like stores. Take the question of rifles. Sir Henry Brackenbury (Volume 1., page 83) tells us that after 25,000 IteServists had gone out with the Lee-Entield it was suddenly discovered that-the 'sighting" was so far wrong that a truly-aimed ;lot hit 18in, to the right at 500 yards. He says: What we proceeded to do immediately was to make a back-sight with a differently-placed 4iotch on the leaf, and we sent these out to South Africa to be put on the Lee-Enfleld there, so that they could alter their rifles there. But it was an awful blow just at the iniment when we were beginning to take this new weapon into use, to find that this mistake had been</p>
        <p>. Foil-owing the close of th; Bloemfontein Confer- ence. I 'sent a series of telegrams and despatches to . the War 0 lice, in which I extended to the utmost limits of official language my warnings of what war with the Dutch Republics would mean I think lam within the me ming of those communica- tions when I aver that the gravity of the warnings therein given could not easily have been greater. But the momentous wtrningc and the specific infor- mation of 1896-7-8- and 1899 are not all. In June, 1899 (four months before the outbreak of hostilities), a special handbook is prepared for the Government be Sir John Ardegh and the Intelligence Dapertmeat. It gives minutely the state of the Boer and Free State armaments. 'Take big guns. At the outbreek of the war we were all astonished by the p tralysine effect of the Boer big Crueiots—the " Loag TO1113." Weere -did they get them? floW was it that we ware ignorant of their existence ? The late Lord Selisbury (January 30th, 1900) assured the - House of Lords that the Government really had no means of knowing. Said he : • The guns were generally introduced in boilers and lozomotives, and the munitions of war were intro- duced in piano-cases and tubs. We had 113 power of *arch; we had no power of knowing whet munitions a war were sent in. . . .We know it now—we have the best reason for knowing it. But that we knew it to the extent to which It existed in June last I en- tirely deny. . Well, now, let us see. Here is a document prepared for the Cabinet and dated Lord Salisbark's identical June. It tabulates almost like a trade prospectus the precise description.of the Boer big guns. It describes for us the number of 151 min. Creusots, 120aun. Howitzers, 75mm. clef. Krapps, Nerdenfeldte, Hetchkisses, Arm- strongs, ac., Ste. It fixes the total number of big guns in the Boar possession in June, 1879, at 107. At the end of the war we were able to verify, by official papers at Pretoria-, the exact state of attars. The total number of Boer big guns in use during - the campaign . Was 93! 01, machine guns this hand- book of •Tune, 18e9, advised the G ivernment as to :34 then in the Boer possession; the actual number was 27.- As to small arms, the handbook estimates a total of 99,261; the actinl number used during the campaign was 96,011. As to rifle ammunition, the handbook tells the Cabinet that the Boers have 3:3,000.000 rounds in hand. How many does it appear they Read off ?-33,050,000 rounds! - . Therefore, it is abundantly clear that the Geveriunent was most carefully advised* as to • the nature of the volcano over which we were leaning in 1899. That the members of the Cabinet should have neglected to read these oft-repeated warnings thrust tinder their very noses tiara and azein for three veers running is the most, shameful and criminal piece of negligence the history . of our coantry in modern times has disclosed. Men's [The coperight of this article, which completes the lives were hanging in the balance. The 834-1 e went first series contributed to oer columns by leading mom- down against them acause—the Cabinet was too indiffer- bars of the Eighty Club, is strictly reserved.] ent to de the work for, which it was paid! es. twill now ask you to carry your minds back to .Tune, . 1895. It was a time of profound peace. Happily, there I WARRINCU3N RNILWAY EXTENSION. was not a suggestion of war on any part of the horizon.' The summer was a very hot one; and, acting upon the ' --- snecific advice of its military advisers, the Liberal Clityln sa tin fo? Intariarms with a Government of the day had kept the supply I of cordite very low. Great heat, I may tell 1 Road. you, makes it very difficult to keep cordite in balk. Very good. Finding that the supply of cordite 1 An i npeztaat claim Luli r the Lands Cleans Con- was low, the great patriotic Tory party was filled with solidetion Act of lals was heard at the Warrington the most righteous indignation. So it came down to Coroner's Ceart on T