        <p>made. Yes; but the discovery was not made till the December of 1599. -Meanwhile thousands of Reservists —arm who had left wives,families, and civil avocations to spring with enthusiasm to the call of duty—had fought at Graspan, :I.lodder River, Stormbeig, 'Wagersfontein, and Colenso. At 1,000 yards' range the truest, shot from the best of them wouldbe'a yard to the right! At the General Election of 1900 these were the very men whose courage, endurance, good tempel., and patience were impudently exploited by the Tory party in order to snatch another term of political power ! Lord Esher. a ft,)yal Commissioner, says that Sir Henry Rrackenbury's statement " shows that either the Secretary of State was culpable of neglect, or that he was ignorant of the facts." Sir George Tanbman- Goldie, 'another Royal Commissioner, adds : " Only an extraordinary 'combination of fortunate circumstances, ext3rnal and internal, saved the Empire during the early months of 1000." The question is, What are non, the voter who reads these lines, going to do about it, ? If you trust these Incapables again you will do so with th 3 fall assurance that national disaster in some form or other will stare us all in the face. The Report of the War Commission makes that, at any rate, an absolute certainty</p>
        <p>T. .T. MACXANT.UIA.</p>
        <p>Nearly 200,000 Free Battles of LINEEL LINIMENT have been se.nt out within the Tact month. in response to as many letters, which can be seen at the Company's Offices. And in no single instance that has come to our knowledge has it been known ta fail in absolutely curing pais, in five minutes.</p>
        <p>YOU ARE NOT ASKED TO BUY Simply send a penny stamp for postage, mentioning this paper, and a Free Bottle will be sent by return post. Every day we receive extraordinary and grateful letters, testifying to the efficacy of LINEEL LINIMENT in curing pain. It would cost a lot of money to publish all these, and it is much more convincing and satisfactory to send you a free bottle, so that you may test it yourself. Sold at all Branches of Boots' Cash Chemists, and by all Chemists, &amp;c., at 1/land 219 per bottle.</p>
        <p>lof the oldand new roads, and carters who had carted over both of them, and they would all say that the new road was positively better and more convenient than the old. There were deep and heavy ruts in the old road, and parts of it were sometimes flooded. Indeed, the wheels of the carts got so stuck sometimes that chain horses had to be obtained to move them. Also, the carts had oftentimes to wait owing to the level crossing gates being closed, as trains went through at the rate of more than three an hour. Those who under- stood the matter would say that the conditions now were better than they were three years ago, ariO there- fore it followed, if they accepted his (Mr. Page's) levidence that no damage had been done to the claimants at all, and if there was no damage there was no necessity for compensation. -IMr. John Barton, master carter, Warrington, said he - knew the old road. It was not wide enough for carts to pass one another in places. It was sometimes under water and they had difficulty in. carting.—Cross-ex- amined: He could not remember any date when the- road was under water.. Mr. G3orge Buckley, foreman carter, said in his opinion the new road, ,was better than the old, and if the two existed to-day, with the old one in the condi- tion it used to be, he would use the new one.—Cross- examined: Tie admitted that Buckley and Company, when he was with them, charged Bd. and 10d. per ton cartage and afterwards asked is. 6d. 1 Mr. Edward Janies George, secretary to flichardEvans and Company, colliery proprietors, said their firm supplied coal to Kenyon's. There was no difference in the cost of carting now and before the new road was made. In 1900 the master carters applied for an advance in the price for carting and talked about sixpence a. ton. Nothing, however) came of it, and the carters got no increase Whatever. Mr. Boyle remarked that if the Railway Company would undertake to do claimants' carting on the !same terms as they had it done when the old road was in existence, they would withdraw the claim. Mr. Page: Oh, that is not it. Mr. Boyle: I thought -you would not like that. (Laughter.) Mr. Page said it was not proper to make such s. sug- gestion, when the matter was under the consideration of the jury, and they would soon be deciding upon it. Mr. Boyle: Never mind that. I will withdeaw this claim if you will undertake to do our carting at the old price. The Assessor remarked that the claim was before the jury now and they would decide it. Mr. Boyle then continued his cross-examination of Mr. George, and asked him if he did not recollect the letter he wrote to Messrs. Kenyon in 1900. stating that the carters insisted upon an increase Of 6d. per ten, carriage over the new'road, and that if they (Kenyon's) had not been compensated for the extra cost they • certainly had a claim for it. Witness said that that letter was written on the in- formation given by the agent. The master carters did not insist upon the increase, and had never got it. Mr. Bolton, stationmaster, who was formerly foreman shunter at Bank Quay Station, spoke to seeing carts wait to cross at the level crossings. Mr. Hugh F. Perkins, a divisional engineer in the employ of the London and North-Western Railway Company, expressed the opinion that the new road was infinitely better than the old. Mr. John White, estate agent and surveyor. Warring- ton, said he remembered the old road very well. He thought the bridge and the new road was much to be preferred to the crossings and the old road. Bethought the bridge was an advantage, and he had hard work to persuade himself that there was any damage to the claimants at if Uwe was any twopence a toe- would cover it. To his opinion ten years' purchase was a fair and full allowance. .Mr. T. T. Wainwright, land agent and surveyor, Liver- pool, Considered that about twopence per ton would cover any extra. cost of carting. Twenty-five years' purchase was altogether too much, as the land and the, business themselves were not worth that much. The jury awarded the claimants £9OO compensation.</p>
        <p>The libel actions which Miss Marie Corelli is bring— ing agianst Mr. lomd Winter, of Stratford-on-Avon, and Mr. Boyden. of the "Stratford Herald," in connection with the Henley-street dispute, will be opened at Biz, mingliani Assizes on Wednesday. / __._</p>
