      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>DBOIIMI3I3R 12th 1903.</p>
        <p>THERE is not much encouragement for Mr.</p>
        <p>CAN.,:._DA AND</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN. CHAMBERLAIN in the striking speech of Mr. 'CLIFFORD SIFTON, the Canadian Minister of the interior. Mr. SIFTOW spoke of the Preferential policy with almost disconcerting frankness. We have been warned that if Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S proposals are not accepted the Colonies will negoti- Ate with foreign countries reciprocal trade arrange- ments which must have the effect of disintegrating the Empire. "You must either organize the Empire on my plan," says Mr. CHAMBERLAIN," or see it. break up." He has managed to convey the impression that at the Conference of which so:much has been said, the representatives of the Colonies -entreated him to adopt this policy so as to avert a -catastrophe which they could see looming in the not distant future. Apparently this alarming vision is concealed from Mr. SIFTON. He is openly contemptuous of the suggestion that 'Canada will seek commercial union with the United States if she does not get a British pre- ference on her wheat. He is evidently of opinion -that the agricultural development of the Colony is quite sure upon normal lines. Nay, he went so fir as to say that it was not to the interest of • the Empire for the Colonies to harass Great Britain with " extra burdens." This may well be regArded by Mr. CHAMBERLAIN as a very unkind .cut. He has employed all his ingenuity to make T"is believ that there cannot he any extra burden if we accept his proposals.He has worked out pretty little sums to show that, so far from beiog bur- -dened, the country will actually he enriched. The -demand for labour will increase,wages will increase -with it, and the clerk with £l5O a year will be .astonished one morning to find on his desk a fatherly little note from his employer appel g- ing him that his salary has been raised by £.50. 'The transformation scene of the pantomime pales its ineffectual splendour before the boons which Mr. OFIAMBERIiArieS wand is to shower upon our -delighted island. But the unfeeling Mr. SIFTONT does not believe a word of it. He takes it for granted that the Preference policy means a loss to 'Great Britain, and he intimates that it is no use exoecting Canada to make that loss good by concessiens to British manufacturers. Of course the Colony will consider any pro- posal in a friendly spirit ; hut this out- sP/ken Minister warns us to cherish no illusions. Plainly he does not believe that any 'truly reciprocal relations of tariffs can be devised.</p>
        <p>All this is consistent with that loyal adhesion to the Mother Country which Caned:4 has always -*hewn. on one point only does Mr. SIFTON'S independent attitude conflict with what public</p>
        <p>opinion at home holds to be Imperial interests. He objects to any contribution by Canada to the maintenance of the British Navy, and he does this on the extraordinary ground that Canada needs no protection by zea. What will happen to her ocean trade in the event of a great war? An, maritime Power with whom we were in conflict would:strive to destroy the British ships which were carrying Canadian produce. A war against England would be a war against every one of her Colonies, and it would be the business of our cruisers to protec their commerce against the common enemy. That a Canadian Minister should ignore this palpable consideration is rather singular. He does not seem to have mastered the elementary purpose for which the Imperial Navy is kept up. Canada, he says, needs protection by land only, and he talks as if this must be provided solely by her own people, and by a systematic training to arms. But he must be aware that in the most unlikely event of an American invasion of Canada an expedition- ary force would be promptly sent from this country to help the Canadian riflemen. Mr. SIFTON says that Canada will rot give up any of her autonomy but it would be a curious autonomy which would refuse any military aid from the Imperial forces in the hour of need. We can scarcely picture Mr. SIFTON in that emergency solemnly declaring that it would be an infraction of Canadian auto- nomy to send British ships over the sea to help in repelling the invader. On this point there is no danger of a misunderstanding. But Mr.CuAmBER- LAIN'S policy has undoubtedly excited suspicion in the Colonial mind that the Fiscal autonomy of the Colonies will be imperilled. His unhappy sug- gestion at Glasgow that they should not foster industries which competed with British mann- fac Lures was subsequently modified into a piece of gentle advice to foster industries on "natural lines." Under a Protectionist Tariff there is no distinction between the natural industry and the artificial, and it is not surprising that the Colonial manufacturers should still suspect Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN of a desire to induce them to abate their advantages for the sake of their British corn petitors.</p>
        <p>Our challenge to thelSecretary of the Labour Representation Committee in regard to the action of " the Liberal leaders" in Councillor Stott's recent election has not been taken up, and we are not surprised thereat. It was plainly evident that Mr. 0. Buckley had overshot the mark in charging the Liberal leaders with working against the Labour candidate in conjunction with Con- servatives and licensed victuallers, but it was necessary that so serious; a misrepresentation should, like tha false coin, be promptly nailed to the counter. Mr. Buckley volunteered a decidedly weak explanation at the meeting of the Trades Council on Tuesday. He admits a " slight dis- crepancy " in saying "the Liberal leaders" instead of "some Liberal leaders." The discrepancy is only " slight" in a literal sense. Actually it is very serious, and the concluding portion of Mr. Buckley's remarks on Tuesday evening tempts one to believe that it could not have been entirely inadvertent. He is determined to saddle the Liberal party with the full responsibility for the irresponsible and very unwise actions of one or two members, or he would not so illogically add "and this is the party we are asked to ally ourselves with ! " Why this obvious hostility to the Liberal party? It can only be, we think, on the part of the Labour Representation Committee, and is not a true indication of the feeling among Labour men as a whole'. Anyhow the Lilieral party is only going to suffer blame for what it is responsible for, and not for what Mr. Buckley conveniently throws on its shoulders. Councillor Stott ovvas a good deal to Liberal assist- ance—the which he was fully entitled to.We,as the Liberal newspaper, have consistently supported him, as we should support any candidate with pro- gressive views, and no one condemns more strongly than we do the action of those few Liberals who, for reasons of their own, worked for a Tory publican against the progressive Labour candi- date. Their view of what Liberalism means must have been as grievously distorted as is Mr. Buckley's conception of the Liberal party.</p>
        <p>At the same meeting of the Trades Council ; there was an informal, but instructive discussion_ on the question of Parliamentary labour represen tation. What has become of the complete un- anii-nity which the town was led to believe existed in Labour circles on this topic P There seems to be a very decided feeling now that it would be a mistake to oppose Mr. A. H. Crosfield,and we are glad Labonr men are coming round to this view. We are still in sympathy witb labour representa- tion, and always shall be, but not when it involves the opposing of a Liberal candidate in a straight fight with a Tory member. That is not the way to secure the desired representation or to advance the interests of the workingmen. We do not desire to harp upon the unwisdorn of the Labour Representation Committee in reck- lessly complicating the political issue in Warring— ton, but to impress upon that committee the fact which is becoming every day more generally admitted, that in a three-cornered fight the Tory would simply "walk home," Such a consumma- tion is as undesirable from a Labour as from a Liberal point of view. Liberal and Labour must be united for the objects of either one or both to be attained, and this feeling is rapidly gaining ground all over the country. There is already in existence a Liberal, Labour League, and branches are shortly to be formed in. Manchester and Liverpool. A similar rapprochommt is very desirable in Warrington. Did it exist there would be no suggestion of a candidate from each body entering the same electoral field. Labour men should realize that where there is a progres- sive Liberal before the constituency they can best secure their own interests by giving him their support. In Mr. Crosfield they will find a man who is nt'epared to go a vow y long way with them towards the goal they desire to reach, and we should like to emphasis the view of the "Man- chester Guardian "that the only hope of winning the renre:sentation of the b.-voile:li for the forces of Free Trade consists in the union of the Liberal and Labour parties under the lead of 3lr. Oros-</p>
        <p>Our readers will ha gratified to learn that the great inquiry is in full swing at last. There was a sittinv, attended by all six Corn missioners on Tuesday, and the business of one committee extending over a period of two years was investigated. On Thursday the Com- missioners met again and concluded the examina- tion of the seven years transactions of this Com- mittee, and, what is more important practically settled the reporting difficulty. Then they ad- journed for a fortnight, and wili recommence seri- ous business in the new year. There is reasorable around for hoping now that the inquiry will b steadily pushed forward to a satisfactory conclusion</p>
        <p>Municipal trading was referred to by Sir Michael Hicks Beach in the course of his speech last Saturday, and an anonymous correspondent has since favoured Coudeillor Dodds with a copy of the speech, at the same time sn4zgesting that it applied in some way to the trading which had been going on in Warrington. Councillor Dodds at once wrote to the Ex.-Chancellor of the Exchequer Pointing out the nature of the trading which had been objected to inlWarrington and received the following interesting reply: "There was nothing in my remarks which con- demned any just _criticism or exposure of the improper use by municipal officials, for their personal advantage, of the confidence reposed in them by the ratepayers,—Yours faithfully, M. E Hicks Beach."</p>
        <p>The proposed memorial to the officers and men oi the South Lancashire Regiment who lost their lives in the South African war has taken definite shape and it promises to be a fitting tribute to their valour, bravery and patriotism. As the militarylleadquarters of this famous regiment, Warrington has a peculiar interest in all that con- cerns its welfare, and its triumphs redound equally to the credit of the town inasmuch as many con- nected with it can claim kVarrington as their birthplace. A painful ensation was caused in the town when the news arrived that the regiment's gallant commanding officer had been shot whilst leading the battalion in their splendid charge at the storming of Pieter's Hill, which cleared the way to Ladysmith, and nowhere was Colonel O'Leary's death more deeply regretted than in Warrington It is now proposed to erect a statue in Queen's Gardens depicting the Colonel's gallant deed and to place on the pediment of the statue, tablets bearing the names of those connected with the regiment who died on service during the war. Among the fallen were some of our own Volunteers and the memorial will emphasize in a permanent form the great sacrifices which our Volunteers made from a high sense of duty at a time when they were most needed. Mr. Drury has been mentioned as the probable artist to be entrusted with the work and we do not think the com- mittee could place the commission in batter hands. The work he has already done is of suffici- ent merit to warrant the belief that he would execute the commission in a manner worthy of the subject.</p>
        <p>Warrington is fortunate in having such a keen educationist as Mr. F. Monks to preside over the deliberations of the Education Committee. The speech which he delivered on Monday evening contains a great deal of valuable information re- specting the position of education in the B irough. Much progress has been made by the local Education Co:runittee since the Edu- cation Act came into operation and there are few education authorities who have so quickly and effectively carried out some of its most important provisions, as the Warrington committee has done. Whilst claim- ing the Educition Act as a step forward in the direction of a national system of education, Mr. Monks is fully alive to its blemishes. "The tyrannical use of a democratic majority," he says " has done its worst in the framing of clauses which the best friends of the church have already condemned.' "Let us hope," he continuos, "that its defects will be remedied in the light of experience and not by the exercise of party strength combined with religious bitterness." It is a pity that the best friends of the church were not more powerful when the Bill was being drawn up, for no measure within recent years has caused so much religious strife and bitterness as this new Education Act. Nonconformists will never rest satisfied until the pernicious clauses have been removed, and in the coming general elec- tion their voice will be heard in this matter, despite tne fiscal and other important questions which will engage the attention of uhe el.ictors. The agitation, it is true, has somewhat obscured the iinporcant provisions in the Act relating to higher education, but in Warrington, at least, they have not been lust sight of. At the present time the Education Committee is evolving a scheme of higher education in Warrington, and we trust they will succeed in introducing a har- monious system which will enable a poor lad with brains to pass from the elementary to the highest branches of study. Mr. Monks has this object in view, and we may be within a more reasonable distance of realising it than many people think.</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday December 12, 1903</p>
        <p>A marriage has.been arranged, and will take place very quietly early in the New Year, between Mr. Henry Tollemache, M.P. for the Eddisbury Division of Cheshire, and Katherine, widow of Mr. Henry Champion Streatfield, and daughter of the late Rev. Henry Aikwright, vicar of Bodenham, Herefordshire. The Rev. C. Ernest Watson, who has been pastor of the Lymm Congregational Church for about five years, has accepted a call to Oakhill, Somersetshire. Mr. Harr' Wall, student of the Congregational Institute, Nottingham, has been appointed Assistant Minister to Rev. J. Yonge, at Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>A GOOD RESOLIITION.—Wear ALE' TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.—AUvt. Fire at Howley.</p>
        <p>Information wa-s received at the Fire Station between six and seven o'clock on Tuesday evening that a fire. had broken out on the premises of Mr. George Fern, brush manufacturer, Ellesmere-stceet, Howley. The tire brigade turned out and 8(4 extinguished the flames. The damage amounts to •a,l»ut ;ClOO.</p>
        <p>Good Templary. • The usual weekly session of the Temperance Mission Lodge was held. on Wednesday in the Ebenezer Church, Buttermarket-street, Bro. F. Merlin, C.T., presiding. The programme for the evening was " Musical night," and the following items were admitted :—Pianoforte solo, Bro. Jackson; solo, " Sankey's 110," Bro. Maidment ; violin solo, "Lullaby," Bro. T. Hughes, jun. ; song, "The Wolf," Bro. Sergeant Stamford; pianoforte solo, Bro. W. Jackson; song, "The song that reached my heart,' Bro. Egerton;'" :duet, "Excelsior," Bros. Hughes and C. J. Sheath; violin solo, Bro. T. Hughes, jue. ; solo, "Scenes that are brightest." Sister E. Littlewood ; solo, "The Irish Piper," Bro. Sergeant Stamford; Bro. W. Jackson presided at the piano. Votes of thanks were passed to all who took part in the programme and a pleasant evening was spent. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, TINDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-ST REET.—AdYt- Legh - street Primitive Methodist Sonde; School. • A grand concert was held on Thursday in the above school, when the following programme was rendered :—Miss Edith Birchall, one of the best local lady violinists, exquisitely contributed " Tarantelle " and" Intro und Polonaise" ; receiving a vociferous encore. She complied with " Simple Avue." Miss Alice Bridge, the leading soprano of the choir, who is the possessor of a rich voice, rendered " Dear Heart" and" Love the Pedlar "and an encore being demanded she sang " Zuyder Zee." Miss Bessie Eckersley the popular contralto delightfully rendered two splendid selected solos. Mr. Arthur Griffiths was decidedly successful in "Good Company," and two selected solos. In response to an encore,Mr.J.Broad- hurst, a promising baritone, rendered " Will o' the Wisp "and ''Anchored" which were sung in excel- lent style. Mr. Pemberton, the humouriSt caused much amusement by his solo and recital, 'his last effort---" The stuffed monkey "being his best contri- bution. Mr. Fred Podmore accompanied on the piano throughout. The chair was taken by Mr. John</p>
        <p>Griffiths, who gave a short, but excellent address. Mr. Bissell seconded the vote of thanks to the artistes, Mr. Albert Spruce was the musical director. ASK for CUNSDIGHAM'S HIEFIT-CLASS AIINERAL WATERS.—Advt. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOCK.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Congregational Bazaar at Lymm. We are all familiar with .the old-time willow- pattern, but a "willow-pattern bazaar" is certainly a novelty. Such an event took place at Lymm last week-end, the Congregationalists of the village having hit upon .that method of raising funds. '.£400 were required for the purpose of completing the Chief Rent Fund and decorating and repairing the church, buildings. When the bazaar was opened, the school presented- a very bright and ;cheerful appearance, willow pattern decorations having been artistically carried out by members of the congregation. Mrs. Mumford was responsible for the paintings. The stalls were heavily laden with a variety of wares, the following ladies being in charge No. 1 Stall: Mrs. Mumford, Mrs. Bre:irley, Mrs. Gotobed, Mrs. Leather, Mrs. Watson, and Miss Porter. No. 2 Stall : Mrs. Raper and friends. No. 3 stall : Mrs. J. D. B. Smith, Mrs. Porter, .Mrs. Turner, Mrs. Willaii, and the Misses Owen. and West. Refresh- ment and Tea Room : Mrs. Groves, Mrs. Hewitt. Mrs. Hinton, Mrs. Ben Moston, Mrs. Simpson and the Misses Heesom, Leah ,and Mclntyre Flower and Plant Stall: The Misses Boden, Brearley, L. Brearley, Drool:man. Hesketh and E. Hesketh. Stamp Stall : Miss Barbara Mumford. The opening Ceremony on Thursday a fternoon was performed by the Rev. Principal kdeney, DJ/. and on Friday by Mrs. Henry Roberts. During each day an excellent programme of entertainments was sub- mitted. A ladies' orchestra, under the leadership of Miss Muriel Raper, played selections and in the even- ing songs were rendered by. Mrs. Mumford and Mr. Hartley, recitations were contributed by Mr. Mumford and Miss Hoult, and violin solos by Miss Q. Raper. There were also dances by the Misses I. and B. Raper, and B. rtimford. Two amusing sketches entitled "The mouse trap" and "Love tabloids" were admir- ably presented. In the first the characters were . sustained by Miss Raper, Miss Madge Raper, Miss Watson, Miss Jessie Taylor, and Mr. Hartley, and in the second by the Misses Raper, Madge Raper, Gladys Raper, Clara Taylor and Jessie ' Taylor, Mr. E. Raper and Mr. Wilfred Raper. The proceeds on Thursday amounted to £BB. On Saturday afternoon Mr. Alfred Arnold, of Manchester; re-opened the bazaar, Mr. C. Raper presiding. A hearty• vote- of thanks was accorded to the chairman and Mr. Arnold on the pro- position of Mr. E. Mumford seconded by Mr. G. Willan. At the close of the day's proceedings,the treasurer (Mr. C. Raper), announced that the total amount realized was :C166. Great praise is due to the officials and committee and especially to Mr. J. D. B. Smith, the indefatigable hon. secretary.</p>
        <p>GIVE TEIEII A TM-AL.—ALP. TAYLOR'S wonder- ful boots for everybody.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Ellesmere-street Mission Temperance Union:— The Breath of Life.</head>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Mission Temperance Union:— The Breath of Life.</p>
        <p>A 'public lecture of a very interesting .character, filled the evening at the last meeting of this Union. The lecturer was Mr. H. H. Taylor, of Museum-street his subject being the "Breath of Life." In introducine, the lecture, the chairman,Mr. W. H. Williams of Man- chester(a fellow medical student ofthe lecturer and who assisted in the numerous chemical exrceriments by which the lecture was illustrated)mentioned that while every living being depended for health and life on' the purity of air 'few understood much about its corn- posit. • He instanced the difference in health and beauty between town and country bred persons, flowers. &amp;c. The lecturer in'openin.g his subject remarked that as oxygen formed the most vital constituent of pure frest air, its properties, uses' and action would receive the greater part of his attention. Chemistry had its alphabet—like reading and other things, the symbols of the more common elements were explained; -matter is indestructible ; this was shown by burning a candle in a tube and retaining the products of com- bustion. The necessity of oxygen was illustrated by burning a candle in a sealed glass jar, the flame sbon dying out. The products of combustion were indic- ated and the similarity between the action of fire and the human process of breathing was explained and illustrated by experiments. The action of breathing was illustrated by a small bellows attached to a pair of sheep lungs ; following this the circulation of the blood and the work of the heart were explained. The reason for the selection of the " ring finger" on the left hand—it being in most direct cOmmunication With the heart—was mentioned, in passing, A. few hints on "first aid" were then given—how to deal with faintings, bleedings, &amp;c. The necessity for it constant change of the air in living rooms, meeting rooms, &amp;e., were then dealt with, the, theory and practice of ventilation being also illustrated. Oxydisa- tion of metals—another form of combustion—was then touched upon, as was also the .marvellous economy !of Nature which provided that trees and other plant and vegetable life give off what human life needs dnd use what human life gives off. In conclusion the lecturer hoped that some hints thrown out during the lecture on experiments would prove beneficial to all. He declared that science and religion went hand in hand. The more one saw of the wonders of science the More real his reverence for the Divine Creator would be- come. He wished to take as guiding principle the words `• Open Thou mines eyes that I may see the wondrous things out of Thy law." After the lecture a great many objects of .interest which further illustrated the lecture wore more closely inspected. Hearty votes of thanks, to Mr. Taylor and the chairtnan(moved by the president Mr. W. H. Sher- burn and Mr. John Ellison) brought a most profitable and enjoyable evening to a close. We are pleased to see the progress which is being made at this Mission and would commend the efforts for a permanent building which are being put forth to the warm sympathy of those able to assist them.</p>
        <p>PuisLic BENEFIT. UYnutt the CLocK for Bus BOCTS in LANCASHIRE—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. At Legh.-street Primitive Methodist Church to-morrow (Sunday) evening the Rev. J. G. Reed will preach on " Every man's life a special plan of God." The annual general meeting of the trustees and managers of the Savings Bank will be held at the Bank on Tuesday morning next. The annual concert in connection with People's College will be held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening. An excellent programme has been arranged including the cantata "Britannia and her daughters." The " Dante" Lectures. Pressure on our space this week compels us to hold over our report on the concluding Dante lecture which took place on Wednesday. The Employment of Children. The Town Clerk has issued a notice containing the most important section of the Employment of Children Act, 1903, which comes into operation on the Ist day of January next. One of the most im- portant provisions specifies that no child under the age of 14 years shall be employed after nine o'clock in the evening. UNDER the CLOCK or GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. The Smallpox Outbreak. It is reassuring to learn that the outbreak of small- pox appears to be subsiding, as since our last issue only four additional cases have been brought to light. The last was reported on Monday, and during the week a number of patients have been discharged from the sanatorium at Great Sankey. Christmas Hot-Pot. The Mayor, in accordance with a long-standing custom in Warrington will distribute hot-pot and mince pie; to the deserving poor on Christmas Day. The food will be served out in the Drill Hall from eleven to twelve o'clock. SEQUEL TO THE RECENT ELECTIONS. I Plucky Constable injured. At 11.15 oa Thursday moraing James Reardon, fish talesman, Bewsey road, had oCcasion to leave his horse and cart unattended in Church place. The animal took fright and galloped along Church street but when it reached the " Cross Keys "Inn, Police Constable Phillips attempted to stop it. The officer was caught by the wheel of the vehicle and knocked down his head being cut, and his left hand scarred. Dr. Peacocke attended him and he was afterwards treated at the Infirmary. The affrighted horse ultimately came to a standstill at tile corner of S;;otland..o td . Burning Fatality. Leonie Sillars, aged four years, residing with her parents at 20, Garabaldi-street, died in the Infirmary, at six o'clock last evening. During the temporary absence of her mother the child's clothing was ignited at the kitchen fire. Her screams attracted the attention of the next-door neighbour, Mrs. Ancock, who raised an alarm and the flames were extinguished by Thomas Ellison, 32, Priestley-street. Unfortun- ately the burns were so serious that tine child succumbed. St. John's Literary Society. 'rho members of this society held their Usual fort- nightly meeting on the evening of Thursday last, when Mr. R. H. Albery, B.Sc., gave a lecture oa " Soap Bubbles " with lantern and experiments. Avoiding technicalities, Mr. Albery dealt in a highly interesting and instructive milliner with the peculiari- ties of liquids, Willi and bubbles. The experiments, illustrated by lime-light, proved a specially attractive feature of the lecture. Oa the motion of the Rev. L. Milne, 8.D., the lecturer was ac.orded a hearty vote of thanks. BASSINETTES ANT) NAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Tronmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.--Advt. Dinner at the Town Hal!. Alderman Heaton, chairman of the Paving and Sewerage Committee, gave a dinner at the Town Hall on Monday evening to the members and a number of invited friends, including his Worship the Mayor. Several toasts were proposed and responded to, and Mr. Bate sang a number of songs in creditable style. 'rule accompaniments were supplied by Mr. B. Wadsworth. - The Fiscal Problem. On Monday night at the Beaeensiield Club, Bury, a lecture or address was given on the Fiscal Problem by Mr. H. P. Hughes, of Stockton Heath. The lecturer took up the position that, by putting the elementary facts in hornelv language, this fiscal pro- blem was quite within the range of the ordinary workingman's opportunities for study and comprehen- sion. That the members were much interested in the. facts and figures submitted to them was evident from the repeated calls to continue the address, when, at different points in the lecture, Mr. Hughes asked if his audience was weary of the subject. Couneillor Cox, Councillor Smith, -Mr. Wilkinson, M.A., and Mr. Storey took part in the proceeding;, and the members unanimonsly voted that they had passed a most enjoy- able and instructive evening.</p>
        <p>Pity the sorrows of a C.mservative candidate who wishes to support Mr. Chainb,Jrlain, ye, from fear of tha electors,dare not venture beyond utter- ing the coldest of sympathy with the ex-Colonial Secretary's Imnerie.k deas ! Major Coates, the candidate for Lewisham, is just now wrestling with the impulse which would carry hint to Mr. Chamberlain's side. Invited by one of his sup- porters to explain exactly how he stands, Major Coates has given the most derightf al picture of a candidate in distre,s that has recently been seen. "As a candidate," he remarked, he prefers retal- iation; " personally" he sympathises with the ideal of Mr. Chamberlain ;but "as a business man" he demands Vs know what the Colonies are going to give us in return for what we are askA to give them. Major Coates's hat s9erns to cover three personalities, but by pressing him, sufficiently the Lowisharn electors will probably find that his elaborate finesse only imperfectly conceals a thoroughgoing Protectionist.</p>
        <p>How Mr. W. J. Shaw was Nominated.</p>
        <p>A sequel to the November election in Orford Ward was heard at the Warrington County Court on Thursday by his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands. K.C., when George Edwards, 12, Oliver-street, sued William James Shaw, of the "Greyhound" Inn, Dallam-lane, for 2 for the hire of a committee room and services rendered by the plaintiff and his son. Mr. Browne defended, but plaintiff was not legally represented. In answer to the Judge, plaintiff said he was a voter in the ward but he did not vote at the election.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined he denied that on the Friday before nomination day he told defendant he wanted to propose him as a "bluff," and that Mr. Shaw said he wouldhave nothing to do with it. Defendant asked his opinion in regard to the election and he (plaintiff) said he thought he might "have a chance." He did not offer his ser- vices and the use of the room free of charge. John Warren spoke to delivering the account which defendant returned.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said the defendant had no right to pay a voter in the ward and all engagements for electio n work were made in writing. Mr. Shaw did not want the plaintiff to get him nominated. Plaintiff, however, said it was merely " bluff" in order to make an elec- tion. and that it would cost defendant nothing as he (plaintiff) would give kis services and allow him to use the room free of charge. _</p>
        <p>Defendant was called and said he had no idea of coin- ing out as a candidate until the plaintiff suggested it to him. Plaintiff told him that there had been two meetings during the week but the ward committee had been unable to decide upon a candidate. Witness said he had had quite sufficient of municipal election work and he did not feel inclined to stand. Edwards said it was "no use allowing them a walk-over and he would get a nomination paper if it was only to bluff them." He added that the election would cost him nothing as far as he (plaintiff) was concerned. A nomination paper was subsequently obtained by the plaintiff and filled in. On Monday witness wrote out a notice of withdrawal, but it was sent in too late. •</p>
        <p>Plaintiff's son stated that defendant said he wished to have the use of the room and witness's services during the election. His Honour said he was satisfied that no instructions were given to the plaintiff and he gave judgment for the defendant without costs. He suggested that de- fendant might give the plaintiff some remuneration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CHRISTMAS HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>THE CHRISTMAS HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>Railway and Post Office Arrangements.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington and District Grocers and Provision Dealers' Association have de- cided to close their places of business on the Saturday following Christmas Day, but they will forego the Thursday half-holiday on the daybefore Christmas Day, On New Year's Day (Friday) the shops will be closed. On Thursday December 31st. the half-holiday will .be dispensed with. The Town Clerk has issued a notice to the effect that the markets will be open on Wednesday and Thursday in Christmas week, and closed entirely on Saturday, December 26th (Bank Holiday.) The London and North-Western Railway Company. have made complete arrangements for the collection quick transit, and prompt delivery of Christmas parcels in all the principal towns on their system, and all parts of Wales, Scotland, and Treland. Special through Vans will be run on the principal trains for the accommoda- tion of the traffic, and additional delivery and collec- tion services by parcel vans alnd carts betwoien the receiving offices and the railway stations will be in operation during Christmas week. Full particulars as to charges can be obtained at any of the Company's Stations and Receiving Offices. Letters and Christmas cards for delivery in the Town District on Christmas Day will be accepted in advance at the counter of any Post Office in the Town and Rural Districts between 8 a.m. on the 17th December and 12 noon on the 23rd December (Sunday, the 30th, excepted) and will be retained until Christmas morn- ing, and then sent out by an early special delivery. The arrangement applies only to Christmas corres- pondence for town delivery. It will further help the Post Office, if, where the letters so posted are numerous they are , handed in in tied bundles,with the addresses they uPwards. The letters must be Landed in over, the counter, and not posted in the letter box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
