      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>scorch</head>
        <p>scorch</p>
        <p>SHORT BE Xl3</p>
        <p>The ":Crawford" Quality. HIGHLAND. Thick Cakes. LOTHIAN. Thin Sections, snored on top. AYRSHIRE. Thin oblong Cakes, sugared on top. Sold by Grocers and Bakers everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <p>Notes on Games and Athletics. The notes by ".will be found on pigs 7 O f this issue.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Dramatic Performances.</p>
        <p>A. sumwof 50 has been handed over from the St.• Mary's Dramatic Society to the NurAng Association, as a first instalment of the proceeds of the recent performances, and Mr. F. Monks J.P., has thankfully acknowledged the ,donatiou on behalf of the Associa- tion. We are requested to remind t cket-holders Ivho have not returned: their ticket, to kindly do so to the hon, secretaries a, soon as p A .Fruiterer's Bankruptcy.</p>
        <p>. A receiving order has I;3en made in the Warring.ton County Court, on the debtor's own petition, against William Edward Boylin, of 59, Hardy-street, War- rington, fruiterer, formerly residing at 76, Scott- street, Warrington, and carrying on business at the Afarket Hall, Warrington. He had been adjudicated bankrupt.</p>
        <p>Hunting _Accident to the Duke of Westminster.</p>
        <p>Whilsi hunting with the North Cheshire Hounds on Wednesday the Duke of Westminster met with a serious accident near Chester. His horse whilst taking a. fence fell, and the duke, falling he:ivily, sus- tained a fracture of the left collar bone. He was con- veyed to Eaton Hall, where he was attended to by Dr Dobie, of Chester. On inquiry 4. Eaton a correspon. dent was informed that the duke is progressing- favourably, and had been able to go out in his motor- car.</p>
        <p>Action Against a Cheshire Farmer. A. strange situation arose at Northwich 'minty Court on Wednesday, when there was called before Judge Bowen Rowlands an action by .a farm labourer to recover A:5O from Jesse Norcott, farmer, Crowley, for kicking him in the eye, and also for breaking two ribs. An array of witnesses, was present, but Mr. Brown, defendant's solicitor, declined to have the case adjudicated at Northwich Court on the ground that Glrowley is in the Warrington district.. The bailiffs declared they repeatedly served s'ainmorises at Crowley and cases were heard at Northwich. His honour was compelled to accede to defendant's re- (pest, aad the case will have to be heard by the samo judge at Warrington. • Farm Fire Near • Warrington.</p>
        <p>At. 3.30 On. Wednesday afte—rnoon information was received at the fire engine station, through the Cheshire Lines telegraphic system, that a fire was in progress at the farm premises of Mr. James J. Brook, llins Green. The brigade turned out promptly under the direction .of Deputy-Superintendent Turner. On arrival they found a corrugated bay containing two stacks—one of corn and another of hay--L: ell alight, and the shippons and stables had also caught fire. Fortunately the cattle ipd horses were removed by the farm servants and escaped unharmed. The stacks were completely destroyed, and consider- able damage was done to 'farm implements and the outbuildings. The damage is covered by insurance. A tramp has been detained in custody on a charge of wilfully setting fire to the stacks.</p>
        <p>Padgate Wesleyan Young Men's Class. The me,mbns of this class and their friends. spent a pleasant social evening on Thursday. Nearly one hundred persons assembled, and the proceedings were presided over by Mr. Councillor Brough. The first portion of the programme consisted of vocal and in- strument'''. music Songs were contributed by Miss Evans and Mr. Ernest Pearce ; violin solos were played by Mr. Lewis Wood, and Mr. Winstanley manipulated the ~rramaphone. A pianoforte solo was well executed by Miss Lena Duckworth and Mr. Duckworth gave a Lancashire sketch. At the close of the programme, Councillor Brough briefly ad dressed the gathering. Various games were after wards engaged in.—The proceeds were in aid of the Wesleyan Missionary Society. Wycliffe Temperance Society. In connection with the above-named society a very interesting and well-attended meeting was held on Wednesday evening. The chair was taken by Mrs. Sherburn and Mrs. Moores and Mrs. Simpson were the speakers. . The chairwoman said there was mach work in -connection with the temperance cause that could be done by women. She sp Ike of visiting the women in their homes • and trying to urge and help them to do the right and refrain from taking strong drink. She thought much good was done -by a kindly interest being taken in the women at their hoines.—.Mrs. Moores delivered a short and pithy address on "Liberty." She spoke of liberty of conscience, liberty from the evil influences of alcoholic drink. The speaker thought that all true Christians ought tO be temperance workers and that every man, woman and child, by the influence of a good life, could help to set at liberty those who were slaves to the drinking habit.—Mrs. Simpson urged temperance workers to aim at securing the young on the side of temperance She spoke of the child as a great imitator of its parents, and gave some gelling illustrations taken from a work written by Lady Henry Somerset. ,Mrs. Simpson thought temperance workers should agitate for the Government to pass laws for the suppression of alcoholic drinking and also see to the carrying out of such laws as we 'already possessed. An interesting musical programme was prOvided. Misses. Alice Griffiths and Elsie Spruce delighted the. audience with their good singing. Mr. Millington contributed two songs. A duet "The Convent Bells" was sung by Masters P. Smith and.J. H. iddon. Master P. Smith also contributed a pianoforte solo. Mr. G. Whitfield's humorous recitations highly pleased the company. Votes of thanks were passed to the ladies for thei r addresses, on the proposition of Mr. G. Colley,seconded by Mr. Fred Morrall. Northern Photographic Exhibition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church Sale of</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church Sale of</p>
        <p>Work.</p>
        <p>The Sal. of \--Nio-rk opened in St. John's schoolroom on Friday of last week and was continued on the follow ing day (Saturday), afternom and evening. There wasn. very satisfactory attendance, especially in the even- ing, when the room devoted to entertainments was largely patronised. " Professor " White gave another of his attractive conjuring exhibitions, and Miss Turner and her orchestra rendered a series of musical selec- tions which were much appreciated, Miss Beatrice. Hayes acting as accompanist in a highly creditable manner. The stalls were almost cleared by ten o'clock, when it was found that the amount realised during the two days was :e4q.</p>
        <p>D3ath of Mrs. Sad low. Many of .our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. Margaret Sudlow, wife of Mr. Charles Sudlow. of Win Wick-street, who passed away on Sunday after a bag and painful illness borne with the greatest fortitude. The deceased lady was .•,ixty-three years of age. Mr. Sudlow has for. some time been seriously ill, and he is now slowly recovering his health.—The. funeral -took place at the Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon, the Rev. E. C. B. Carleton efficiating. The, chief mourners were : —Mr. Joseph Sudlow (son), Messrs. William Bellian and Joseph Bellian (brothers), Messrs. C. B. B, Sutton. B. J. Sutton, W. Sutton, H. P. Sutton, and W. Lockerbie (grandsons), Messrs, R. Lockerbie, M. G.. Hearne.and J. D. Leigh (sons-in-law), Mr. B. Shearer, (brother-in- law), Messrs. W. Bellian, A. Shearer, J. Sudlow, Fearnhead, and G. Hodgkinson (nephews). Amongst the friends present were Mr. T. Longdin, Dr. G. Braide, Mr. W. Litton, and Mr. McDowell. Some beautiful floral tributes were forwarded by Mr.C. Sudlow (husband), Robert •and Tissa, Joseph and_ Fanny, George and Madge, Frank and May, John and Polly, Lyn, Willie and Harry, Ted and Sara, Mrs. P. Leigh and family, Mr. and Mrs. W. Leigh, Miss A. W. Coulborne, Manchester, Winnie and Mabel, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Longdin, Mr. and Mrs. S. Wakefield,Miss Allen and Miss Marshall, Mr. W. Fletcher, Mr. and. Mrs. Litton, Hr. and Mrs... Shearer, and Nurse Boardman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>TWELVE YEARS FOR A FATHER. Peter Doward (40), collier, was convicted at Liver- pool Assize on Thursday of two criminal assaults on his own daughter at St. Helens, and was sentenced to 12 years' penal servitude. THE DISTRESS AT ST. HELENS. The Mayor of St. Helens, Alderman J. Massey, pre sided on Monday at a meeting at the Town Hall, called to consider the question of the distress which is said to exist in the town. In 1894 a Mayor's fund was opened, in conse- quence of the distress following the coal strike. The balance which remained was invested in the names of some members of the Corporation, and the future disposal of it was left to a‘ committee, consisting of the Mayor and the chairmen of the Corporation committees. This committee met on Monday, bat many of the gentle- men present doubted if there was sufficient distress in the town to warrant the opening of a fund. It was state.i. that St. Helens was in a mach better position than many Lancashire towns. The Mayor consequently said he would make inquiries from the Charity Organlza- tion Society and the ministers of the different deuominations before anything further was done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN GRAPPLTNG CORPS. the Runcorn Police and Grappling Corps boat, number two, was christened the Runcornian," and launched from the far side of the Bridgewater Canal at the Top Locks on Saturday afternoon. The boat, which has been built by Mr J I Stubbs, of Runcorn, is well suited for the work for which it will be used. It will be stationed on a trolley at the Top Locks. NVidnes has already presented the corps with a boat, fully equipped with four oar; mast and sail, for river work. A third boat is needed for the Bottom Locks, at Rungorn, for work in the river Weaver, and the Weaver canal. Mr. Byfield is the captain of the corps,and Mr. J. H. F. Waring the vice-captain while Superintendent Ennion has given an assurance that the police will help the corps in every way possible.---The boat launched on Saturday was christened by Mrs. F. J. Norman. It was announced that Mr. F. J. Norman would launch the boat, but an important educational engagement in Liverpool prevented his attendance, and the ceremony was performed by Mr. J. Evans, the Runcorn agent of the Bridgewater undertaking. Mr. S. Taylor presided, and in the course of a short address,heartily commended the work of Mr. Byfield and the corps. Mr. Byfield gave an interesting history of the work, and appealed to the public to assist him in completing the equipment. Mrs. Norman then Voke a bottle of champagne over the prow of the boat and christened it the • Runcorn- tan." Mr. Evans released the fastenings and the boat. enterea the water at the same time that loud cheers were raised 15y the onlookers. On the motion of Mr. W. Handley, seconded by Mr. Noble, of the Mersey Mission to Seamen, Liverpool, a cordial vote of thanks was passed to Mrs. Norman and Mr. Evans, and the proceedings concluded with a similar vote to the Chair- man. A collection in aid of the cost of Nos. 2 and 3 .boats amounted to £3 is. sd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>W VIERED MILK. On Thursday, at the Newton Police Court, Sarah Taylor, farmer, Lowton, was fined 20s. and costs for having sold milk at Golborne which the county analyst proved to contain eight per cent. added water..</p>
        <p>TRAMP'S ALLEGED TNCENDIARISM. At the Newton Police Court on Thursday morning, Edward Kelly, tramp, was charged with wilfully setting fire to corn stacks, the property of James .1. Brook, farmer, Rixton, near Warrington, on Wednesday after- noon. Prisoner was remanded to the Warrington County Sessions next Wednesday. ST. JOHN'S CHURCH, EARLESTOWN. Oa Thnrsday evening on organ recital was given in the above Church, by Mr. J. T. Hughes, astistant organist, Chester Cathedral, in aid of the fund being raised for the extension of the Chnrch organ. Thera. was a very fair audience, and an excellent selection of music was submitted in masterly style. A couple of solos " Hear ye, Israel" (Elilah) and "With verdue clad" (Creation) were sung by Miss Green, and were received with evident satisfaction. DISTRESS AT EARLESTOWN. During the last few weeks the majority of theEarles- town people have been experiencing great distress owin,, to the London and North-Western railway waggon works placing their hands, numbering close on two thousand, on short time, and also dismissing a number of smiths, painters. moulders, and waggon builders. At the present time the men remaining at the works are only working on an average forty hours per week-. At a. meeting of the Newton-in-Makertield Urban Dis- trict Council, on Monday evening, it was decided that a committee be appointed to inquire into the distress in the town and make suggestions for the relief of the sufferers to the Council. The police, tradesmen, and the local football club are also working hard in the interests of the poor people. The head teachers in many of the 'schools have been informed bychildren that they have been sent to school without breakfast or dinner, and a committee of ladies has taken the matter in hand, and each day the children aro given a good dinner con- sisting of soup, hot-pot, ttc. The shortage of work ia owing to the enormous quantity of rolling waggon stock that has accumulated, and the bad trade up and down the country. Happily two 'other large engineering firms in the neighbourhood are oti full time.</p>
        <p>The prosptetus has just been published of a Photo- graphic Exhibition on what promises to be an exten- sive scale, in the Walker Art Gallery, Liverpool, early in the spring of next .year. The Liverpool Amateur Photographic Association, acting in con- junction with the Manchester Amateur Photographic Soci.3 ty and the Leeds Camera Club, are the prime movers in the project which has now taken practical shape. The exhibition is, we learn, to be "inter- national and epe,n " in its character. - The Arts and Exhibitions Sub-Committee of the Liverpool Corpor- tion having granted the use of certain, galleries in the Walker Art Gallery—Probably the, finest suite of rooms for such a purpose to be found in the kingdom ----the claim made that this will be the largest and most important photographic exhibition held for over twelve years in Liverpool might, we think, be safely widenec.l to include in 45 sweep the whole of the northern counties. The fact that the Lord 'Mayor of the city has . consented to open the, Exhibi- tion confers upon it a prestige and a status which will doubtless- materially assist the Committee of Management in popularising it. The Executive is thoroughly representative of the initiators of the scheme, and include the names of manr well-known workers in the domain of photo- graphic art in Liverpool—Dr. Llewellyn Morgan being , its chairman, and Mr. Chas F. Liston, F.R.P.S., the exhibition hon. secretary. From - the printed pros- . pectus we gather there are to be four sections :—(1). Pictorial Photographs, (2) Scientific and Technical Prints and Slides, (3) Pictorial Lantern Slides mid Stereoscopic Work, and (4) Trade Exhibits. The „., „ .I'. MAIO'S MISSION TNSTIT UT E.—A good number judges (six in number) are of the highest repute, and of members were in attendance at the #sual weekly include the well-known names of Harold Baker, A meeting on Monday night 'last. The Re. J. R. Jones Horsley Hinton, Thomas IRMO, R. 1., R.E., Alex- occupied the c