      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Lsgh Street Primitive Methodist Church. 4..)u Monday last the Rev. J. Grady occupied the pulpit at thef morning service,when Mager Clatworthy the talented boy organist, officiated at the orgag, his clever manipulation of the instrument delighting the congrega- tion. Councillor W. Brough preached in the evening, when Miss Alice Woodland sweetly sang—" Ora pro nobis," accompanied by )Ir. Fred Podmore. Baptist Church, Colborne Street.</p>
        <p>• The Pastor preachod his monthly sermon to young people on Sunday evening last, taking his text from Proverbs .14.12,"There is a way that seetneth right unto a man, but the end thereof is death.", It was quite pos- sible for men and women to be mistaken as to the character of the path in which they were travelling, and although•many were treading the wrong path un- consciously, it none the less surely .ended in death. The Preacher urged his congregation to make sure of being in the path which leads to life and emphasized the importance of putting first things first. Other things had their proper place but • must not come between the soul and Christ. Foreign Missionary Anniversary at . Bewsey-road.</p>
        <p>Although the w‘eather was most unfavourable, there were good congregations at the foreign missionary anniversary in connection with Bewsey-road, Wesleyan Church, which was celebrated on Sunday. The Rev.- R. Wilkins Rees preached at each service. In the morning his subject was "Ancient religions and modern mis- sions" and he selected for his text the words" Many prophets and righteous men have desired to see those things which ye see and have not seen them; and to hear those things which ye hear, and have not heard them." He pointed out that in practically all religions there was a groping for god—the idea that "No man bath seen God at any time." It was for Christians to guide the believers in those faiths to the religion of Christ. In the afternoon a meeting was,held under the presidency of Dr. James Bennett who referred to his associations with Bewsey-road Church and spoke of the necessity for an enthusiastic spirit in connection with foreign missions. The Rev. R. Wilkins Rees gave an address on "Stories from heathen lands." At the evening service Mr. Rees again premhed, his subject being "Most important reasons for foreign missions." Basing his discourse on the words "And He saith unto them 'Go ye into all the world and preach the gospel to every creature'" he said that Christ's idea and the purpose of His gospel was that it should be preached to all the world, and Christ's disciples and successors ought to do that. The preacher mentioned that in 1796 a proposal was brought before the Scottish Church to evangelise the . . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>BETTING RAIDS AT NEWTON AND COLBORNE. Bookmakers Fined.</head>
        <p>BETTING RAIDS AT NEWTON AND COLBORNE. Bookmakers Fined.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning, at the Newton-le-Willows Police Court, two cases of keeping betting-houses came before the bench. The first case was against Frank Doyle, hairdresser, 26, Wargrave-road, Earrestown, for offences on the 23rd, 24th, and 25th ult., to which he pleaded guilty.—Mr. J. W. Knowles. for the police, stated that several persOns were seen to visit defendant's premises, but did not stay long enough to get a shave,. but theonly bet of which a record could be found was one made by a. police officer.—Defendant made a state- ment-that he had given the betting business up, and on undertaking not to repeat the offence he was let off on. the payment of ss. and cost 3. Abraham Nuttah, refreshment-house keeper, Golborne was 'charged with a similar offence. Mr. Knowles stated that it was a case in which there was evidence that a good deal of business had been dorm* Between ten. minutes to ten OA the 1 night of the 27th ult. (when a police-constable made a bet in an assumed name, found subsequently in a betting book) and twenty minutes to two on the afternoon of the following day, when the police raided the place, no less than 158 bets were re- ,•orded in the sante book. One b •tting book showed '216 entries, and a paying out book 233. He described i.he case as a clear catch, and asked for a substantial penalty.—After evidence by the police, Mr. A. Smith , who defended,withdrew the plea of not guilty, and sub- mitted that the bulk of the bets made by defendant , were taken outside the Manchester races, and that as the police evidence only showed less than twenty possible bets made in the house, he pleaded for a miti-. gated penalty, •as it was defendant's first case.—The magistrates fined defendant £2O and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>LOVELY- LANE V. RYLANDSIBROS. Played at Lovely-lane, On Saturday, the home team winning by three goals:to one.</p>
        <p>THORNS AND CYCLISTS</p>
        <p>Jonathantiammond, a well-known, farmer, of Hand- forth, was fined ss. and costs at Wilmslow on Thursday, for allowing hedge-cuttings to remain on the highway for a longer period than one hour after sunset. The police stated that some Alanchestercyclists complained about the cuttings near defendant's land, and he had prqViously been warned. In this case it was said defend- „nt allowed the cuttings to be on the road for six hours,</p>
        <p>THEFT FROM A RUNcORN PUMIC HOUSE. At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Mr. W. Timmins (in the chair) and Mr. S. Taylor, John Carney, alias Peter Carney, a native of St. Helens, was charged on remand with having stolen a box of a hundred " cabinet "cigars, the property of James Tat- lock, licensee of the Holyhead Harbour public-house, High-street, Runcorn, on the second inst.—Prisoner pleaded guilty. There were 17 previous convictions And be was committed to gaol for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WARRINGTIt,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTIt,</p>
        <p>Personal. • . A marriage has been arranged, aid will take pla very quietly early ,in the New .:Year,_-betWeen Mr. Henry Tollemache, M.P. for the Eddisbury Division of Cheshire, ,and Katherine, widow of Sir. Henry Champion *Veatfield, and daughter of the late Rev. Henry Arkwright, vicar of Bodenham, Herefordshire. , The Rev. C. Ernest Watsod, who has been pastor of the Lyinni • Congregational Church for about five years, has accepted a call to Oakhill, gornersetshire. Mr. Harry ,Wall, student of the. Congregational Institute, Nottingham, has been appointed A.ssistaut Minister to Rev. J. Yonge, at Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>A GOOD RESOLUTION.—Wear ALF TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.—Advt. Retirement of Major Roe. - - -</p>
        <p>Major _Roe, Govern-ment InspecOr of Factories for Manchester and, district, which includes Warrington, has retired on pension after a service with the Home Department of 23 years: He will be succeeded by Mr. A. Les, whi has -been . in charge of the South Wales distrit.</p>
        <p>FOR CLASS AND CHINA of every description try J. S. GLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55. Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Ragged School Band of Hope.</p>
        <p>Asocial for the members of the above was h _ld on Wednesday evening in the schoolroom. •. There - were eighty members pres,3nt, and after partakinc, of an excellent 'tea,' games, &amp;c., were indulged in. partaking S. and A. Titenard were responsible for a most enjoy- able entertainment. GIVE THEM A TRIAL.—,ALF. TA.Y.E.OR'S wonder- ful boots for everybody. . Death of Mrs. Sudlow. • Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death which-took place at her residence, on Sunday, of Mrs. Margaret Sudlow, wife of 'Mr, Charles Sudlow, bo.yt and • shoe. Maker, Horsemarketstreet. • The deceased lady who was in her sixty-third year, had been in a delicate state of health for a considerable time. .</p>
        <p>UNDER the CLOCK or GOOD BOOTS.-Advt. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—Ae-t, Fire at Howley.</p>
        <p>Information was received at the Fire Station between six and seven o'cloc..k on Tuesday evening that a fire had broken out on the premises of Mr. George Fern', brush manufacturer, Ellesmere-street, HOwlev. The fire brigade turned out and soon extinguished the flames. The damage amounts to abut</p>
        <p>FIRE GRITHS of every description.—J. S. Girt- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Ring 0' Bells, Stratton. At Daresbury Petty Sessions, on Tuesday, Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, on behalf cf Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Company, submitted plans .for certain alterations proposed to be made at the Ring o' Bells, Stretton., .Mr. Browne stated that the drinking area of the house would not be increased i any way. The alterations which had been approved by the Chief Constable- (Colonel Hamersley), were sanctioned.</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR. ALL.—UNDER the CLOCK.—A.dVt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS WATERS.—Advt. .</p>
        <p>Lymm and the Salt Trade. - A letter has been received by the District Council of Lymm from Mr. Charles C. Moore, director of „Messrs. Charles C. Moore and Co., Limited, t) the effect that it has been decided to withdraw the plans recently sent in for the erection of the proposed salt works at Heatley, owinn, to the abandonment of the °Ling.</p>
        <p>You CANNOT DO BETTER! Bay your boots and shoes at ALr. TAxLart's. Cheapest and bast town.</p>
        <p>Dinner at the Town Hal!. Alderman Heaton, chairman.of the Paving and Sewerage Committee, gave a dinner at the Town Hall on Monday evening to the members .and a number of invited friends, • including his Worship the Mayor. Several toasts were proposed and responded to, and Mr. Bate sang a number of songs in creditable style. The accompaniments were supplied by Mr. B. Wadsworth.</p>
        <p>Asa for CUNNINGITSAI'S MINEII.I WATERS.—Advt. Local Musical Successes. At the recent examination held at Liverpool, November 30th, in connection with the Victoria College of Music (London), the following pupils of Miss Annie Hill, , A.V.C.M., C Napier-street, Warrington, passed very successfully; Names and grades as follows. Associate —Joseph Ed ward Roylance,Mo pre. This entitles him to wear the Cap and Gown of the College, also to affix the letters, A.V.C.M., to his name., Senior—Percy Moores, Warrington; Robert Hanson, Warrington. Intermediate—Lillie. Frith, Dutton. Primary—Catherine Rowland, Warrington.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S Ltrv. TONIC is the KING OE TONICS.-33. SANKEY-STRECT, Warringtou.—Advt. Pleasant Saturday Evenings. The third of a series of concerts was held in.Sliarp- . street Mission room on Saturday eveninth., Mr. J. Jones occupied the chair, and a capital programme was gone through. Mr. Barnes was intensely amus- ing in the hum3rous songs, " The Burial Club" and "The Baby's Name." He was also loudly applauded for his humorons selections. Master and Miss Spruce acquitted themselves admirably in the duet "Hark I hear the night bells ringing," for which the audi- ence demanded an encore. Mr. Spruce (Legh-street choirmaster) sang " I Remember "in fine style. Re- freshments were provided.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OP TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Ironworkers and the Tariff Question. _</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Executive council of the Iron and Steelworkers' Association held at Manchester on Monday and attended by representatives of the trades from all parts of England and Wales, the following resolution was passed We desire to recognize the services rendered by Mr. James Cox.general secretary of the association, m ' his active *opposition to the _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>liwal proposals of lfr. Chamberlainueh. opposition meeting- with. the .approval T of an • overwhelming majority of the members of the association who lo3k upon these proposals as being detrimental to the best interests and well-being of the workmen and preju- dicial to the general prosperity of the country.' Mr. J. Elweli represented the Warrington branch at the. .meeting. •</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK for Br.s BOCT ; in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. hocal Rechabitism. The annual' tea' -meeting In connection with the "Sunbeam ".Tent (Stockton Heath) of the Warrington Consolidated District Independent Order of Rechabites was held in Ebenezer Schoolroom the other evening, when a good number sat down ;o it substantial tea. A Subsequent public,meeting; held under the presid- ency of Bro. 'W.-H. SherbUrn, was made exceptionally interesting by the presence of Bro. W. A. Platt, 8.D., orßolton, who set forth the- merits of the order in a very enthusiastic address. He dealt with the rapid progress made by the society since its establishment. up to the present. He also pointed out the value of this teetotal friendly society to young men, and explained the many advantages to be derived from it by being menibers. Bro. Rev. J. S. Hughes appealed to all. young men to join the order. During the evening musical items were contributed by Miss E. Grounds and Messrs. F. Sherburn, J. Wortall, and George and S. Moss, *which were much enjoyed. Selections on the gramophone were given by Bro. W. Ridgway.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>THIS IS WONDERFUL.</head>
        <p>THIS IS WONDERFUL.</p>
        <p>To be entirely cured and restored to good health after the doctor has told a man to prepare for another world is a remarkable experience. Mr. George Smith, Tisbut's Cottages, Box Hill, Box, Chippenham, relates how this happened to him as follows : "I had Rheumatic Fever and my doctor ordered me to be put into bed, and wrapped in blankets, soaked in oil. After about a month of it "was sent to the hospital, where I lay for six weeks. They tried all sorts of treatment, but I got no better. "My limbs were So large and swollen I could not walk. My doctor said I had Heah Trouble and • told me to go home and prepare for another world. I "However, one day I happened to read an advertise- ment of Dodd's Kidney Pills and made up my mind to try them.. The first box seemed to help me a little and I kept on until I had taken six boxesovhen I was up and well. To-day I feel like a new Man, in fact, I never felt better.</p>
        <p>"I can say that Dodd's Kidney Pills -have saved my life and I recommend them to all who suffer as did."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>The Examiner SaturdP7 December 125 1903 DANGES FOR MISREPRESENTATION. EDUCATION IN WARRINGTON "The Word of a Christian Brother." SINGULAR COUNTY COURT ACTiON MR. MONKS REVIEWS THE AT WARRINGTON. PROGRESS MADE. At the'Warrington County Court; on Thursday, before his Honour 'Judge' Bowen .lowlands, K.C., Edmund Green Barrett, wholesale biscuit, cake and sweet manu- facturer, Thewlis-street, brought an action against Edward James•KnoWles, who carries on a similar busi- ness at Atherton, and formerly lived in Warrington, to recover£4s by reason of the defendant. 'inducing the plaintiff to purchase the outside wholesale biscuit and sweet business and a fonr-wheeled van from, the defend- ant, and to pay the sum of £45, thereby fraudulently representing to the plaintiff that the defendant had certain customers, and that the profits from the busi- ness were between £2 and £3 a week, and that the van was worth the sum of £lO, whereas, in fact, many _of the names given were not the names of - customers, the profits were not so large as represented, and the van Was not worth £lO, but was actually sold for 18s.—Mr. 1.1. H. Layton, barrister, Liverpool (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) represented the plaintiff, and Mr. Brockle- hurst, barrister, Manchester (instructed by Messrs. Fowles and Laycock) defended. s: • Mr. Layton said the plaintiff and defendant were members of the same Methodist church in Warrington and the fact had a great deal to do With the case. For two years previous to February last they were very friendly. After the evening service on February Ist. defendant asked plaintiff—who was then san agent in the employ of the Prudential Assurance Company—how much he was•earning per week. Plaintiff said he was receiving 35s or £2 and defendantsaid " You ought to go into the biscuit business. That's the thing to make your fortune," After further conversation they parted but on the following Tuesday eveaing, again after service, the subject again cropped up. Defendant said -"If you go into this business yonwill 'make £2 or £3 clear profit. That is what I am making on the round,l am anxious to dispose of." On the following • Saturday or. Sunday plaintiff definitely said he would take over the business and it was arranged that he should pay £l5 for it, including the van which defend- ant said was worth £lO. On the previous Tuesday defendant had produced books showing a. gross turn- over of £39 in one week. This he said represented alO per cent profit ort3 odd and gave the plaintiff tounder- stand thati that was the usual turnover. ; On February 13th plaintiff saw the van which was in very bad condi- tion, but defendant said "Its alright, it's the best van I have got." On the following Monday defendant had the Vehicle painted. Subsequently plaintiff found that the van was rotten and the biscuit tins were constantly tumbling o lithe shelves. The van was sold by the plaintiff for 18s. although six weeks after his transaction with the defendant it had been valued by a wheelwright at 305.. That valuation, however, was carried out on condition' plaintiff bought a van from the wheelwright. On February 13th defendant produced an agreement which was not satisfactory to the plaintiff who drew up another.- -A condition was inserted, that defendant _should guarantee thpprofits to be between £2 and £3 hut defendant woUrd not agree to that. Ultimately he drew up an agreeinent and both parties signed it. There was no condition in regard to the profits and when plaintiff called attention. to that fact defendant said, "you can rely upon me I shall do nothing wrong." Plaintiff said he did ,not think it was businesslike and defendant said" You Can take my word for it as a Christian brother that it is all right." . In some dis- tricts mentioned in the - agreement there were no cuts-, temers at all. On several days defendant accom- panied the 'plaintiff to introduce him to customers and plaintiff booked the names. A great many of these people Were not customers at all and some did not trade to the extent alleged by the defendant. Plaintiff gave up his business of insurance agent and Spent all the money he possessed' in paying for the biscuit business: In the first week the turnover was about £9, and it never exceeded £l5. until plaintiff began to take in. districts not mentioned in the agreement. By doing so • he had managed to make a living. Duriirrb.the first few weeks that he carried OR the business he made practically nothing. On one occasion he mot the defendant at Wigan and told him that the van was absolutely no goo and that he was making nothing out of the business—in fact, he was losing money. TI defendant liked to give hi-.n £5, he said, he could have the business back. Defendant laughed at him, and said "No; it's no good to me." Defendant refused to take the business and on other occasions'plaintiff accused him of selling something that, was not worth what it was reported to he. He also wrote a strong letter to the plaintiff, but received no reply. Plaintiff gave evidence bearing out this statement. At the time of the transaction defendant and himself were members of the Primitive Methodist Church at Warring- ton andjdefendant was secretary of the Sunday school. Cross-examined he said he had to pay for the keep of the horse out of the profits. Defendant did not give him the option of trying the business beforepurchasing it, and did net ask him to enter into his service for a time to learn the business and ascertain the value of the round. He wrote defendant a letter stating that he had brought the matter before the church circuit officials. "If you intend to be a Christian "he added " take Christianity in business, and act as a man." His Honour : He appealed to a lower court—Chris- tianity—first and to the law afterwards. (Laughter.) „Mr. Layton : It is lunch cheaper—Christianity. Brocklehurst asked plaintiff if he brought the matter before the circuit officials to bring defendant into bad odium. His Honour: says is right. ' : Plaintiff admitted writing a farther letter in which he asked defendant to pay him something in settlement, of the case "To keep it from the world " He wrote "Let us come to terms. on Christian principles: If not I shall be compelled to go further." His Honour : That is an imputation on a court of law—that it is not conducted on Christian principles. Re-examined plaintiff said he never made P.,1 a week profit from the round mentioned in the agreement—in fact he suffered a loss. Albert Howells and Mary- Hewitt. of Widnes, and Herbert Duke and John Pearson of Earlestown, whose names it was stated appeared on the list of customers supplied by the defendant, were' called and said they had not done business with the defendant since he left the employ of Messrs. Peak, Frean and Co. Pearson said he had never traded with him. Mr. Brocklehurst said the round which defendant wished to dispose of was valued at from £4O to £5O. Plaintiff examined the books and contrasted the gross takings of the round with defendant's outlay. Defend- ant told him that he made from £2 to £3 profit on the amount he gave for his goods. lie 'made plaintiff an offer that the £45 should be deposited with him as a loan bearing interest at 5 per cent, for a few months. During that time plaintiff should eater his service at a nominal *age and if he was not satisfied with his ex- periences he could ilftVi3 the money back together with the interest. Plaintiff would not agree to that and said De was "buying right out." Mr. Brocklehurstcontended that defendant had fulfilled the conditions of the contract as he could prove that the gross profits on the round were between £2and £3 a week. Defendant acquainted plaintiff with customers and also gave him the names of possible customers. Defendant was Called and said plaintiff complained bitterly of his earnings • as an insurance agent and stated that he was going to give it up. Witness told him that he wished to dispose of the round in question and as it was Sunday he then discontinued the con- versation" on religious grounds." Cross-examined, defendant admitted that he refused to sign the second draft agreement which contained a, condition that he should return the money if the busi- ness did not turn out as profitable as stated. He denied telling the plaintiff that if he wanted to make his fortune he should take up the . business. He admitted saying that there MIS ft profit of 10 per cent on the gross account. His Honour; How much could You make out of the round? Defendant: From £2 to £3 with My expenses to come out of it. His Honour: How much net profit ? Defendant: I never tied it down to a net prat. His Honour; Do --. you .mean' to say after being in business with Peak, Freaii. and Company and on your own account that. you, cannot tell me the net profit? Defendant : No, sir. His Honour : What was the use of showing plaintiff the books? Defendant: I took' it on the bulk of the discount I received. His Honour : Did you tell him " You can rely on me looking filter your interests as a Christian brother? Defendant : I said it was a genuine concern. His Honour: Did you tell him that he could rely on your statements ?—N.o,: sir. I did not make that remark. - Did Airt tell him he could rely on you as a Christian brother gave him that to find out. To find out whether you were a brother or a Christian, or what ?..My dealings with him. Did you say "You can rely upon this being a genuine business, because I tell you so as a Christian brother?" left Christianity out of it. don't think leversaid was a Christian brother. Would that have been a false representation if you had said it ?,-No, T don't think it woul4. • Archibald Yearsley; accountant, Maufhester, said he had examined defendant's' purchase. ledger and memorandum book. They showed that the weekly profit -on the round sold to plaintiff was £2 2s. 9d. His Honour said the defendant's evidence had been such a mass of confusion, contradictionsand irreconcil, able statements, that he could place very little reliance upon the accuracy of his recollection. What the intention might be he did not. presunie for a moment to define. Defendant admitted that the plaintiff desired the introduction of a guarantee, of the amount which had been verbally specified by the defendant, but that was refused. On the other hand defendant said he wished the plaintiff to become, his servant and the £1.5 could be left as a deposit, to be returned if the plaintiff was not satisfied with the busi- ness. That proposition was embodied in the second agreement and refused by the defendant. He need say no more to show the value he attached to the accuracy of the recollection of the defendant in his various state- thents. on the point. After further 'criticisms his . Honour gave judgment for the plaintiff for £35 l.os. with costs.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Monks, J.P. (chairman of the Education Com- mittee) presided at the annual tea meeting in connection with Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Monday, and delivered an important speech in which he reviewed the position of education in Warrington. Tea was provided in Wycliffe Hall and 00 sat down. The subseQuent meet- ing was held in the Memorial Building. Addressing the company, Mr. MONKS said that when Mr. Yonge reminded him of his promise last year to preside at that annual meeting he began to think over what he should say that would be of any interest to them. One had so few opportunities of frankly speak- ing one's mind upon any subject of passing interest that he took the present one to say something on the Educa- tion Bill and its working in Warrington. He did so mainly for two reasons. First, because education was not a matter that they could play with or postpone without injustice to their own children and secondly because there were certain influences at work in the town which sought on the plea of economy. tl restrict, if not to paralyse, the educational activities of the town and consequently stunt the intellectual development of its children to their permanent disadvantage. Whatever opinions they might hold about the Bill there could be no question that it was a</p>
        <p>STEP FORWARD in the right direction as regarded the organisation of, their schools making them more effective for the pur- pose of men 'tally training their young children. To begin with, instead of the isolated management of each school, dependent to some extent upon voluntary effort to eke out the cost of maintenance over and above the grants earned, they had now a well organised com- mittee of education responsible to the ratepayers for the efficient control and management of every element- ary and secondary school in the town. It was entrusted with power to make suitable provision for the growing population in districts where the need for a school was greatest. It was not content with a low standard of teachinr, capacity in order that expenses might be kept down, but required every teacher to make himself and herself efficient as a teacher and paid them a fair salary for the work expected of them. It was true that the management of each school was left to a large extent to the managers, two of whom were appointed by the Education Committee, but they were subject to supervision and were required to maintain the minimum of efficiency. The healthy condition of the schools was or would be greatly improved and the cleanliness of all schools was now amply provided for by a well-paid staff of school keepers who were also under the eye of the central committee. Books,stationery and other school appliances of the best kind were now provided and the town was</p>
        <p>Ile is justified in doing so if what he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>GETTING VALUE FOR ITS MONEY.</head>
        <p>GETTING VALUE FOR ITS MONEY.</p>
        <p>There was a great outcry at the time of the appoint- ment of those entrusted with this department on the ground of economy but he was in a position to say that results had justified the appointments and was proving the folly if not the ignorance of those who sought to prevent the efficient organisation of this department of work. The teaching staff was not all that they desired, but its improvement was a work of time and persistent effort on the part of the committee to attain a higher level of teaching power than MS possible to-day. Every inducement was held out to teachers to improve their methods and the weeding out process of the inefficient andincapable would proceed without injustice-to anyone but with the greatest advantage to the children with a real and not a false economy to the ratepayers Of the town. All this, however, was not education; it was only the means to an end, the instru- mentality through which they worked. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>AIM OF THE EDUCATION COMMITTEE]</head>
        <p>AIM OF THE EDUCATION COMMITTEE]</p>
        <p>was to give to every child, however poor its parents might be, the advantages of a good, systematic, mental training, so as to develop the capacity of its own mind and enable it to take advantage of its opportunities through life to promote its own welfare and that of the State. (Hear, hear.) The old system of training children in order to earn the highest grant had gone, he hoped, for ever. To reduce the intelligent child to the level of the naturally dull, was the way to train Children to that vacuitr of thought which was the parent o stupidity. The MENTAL TR,AININS OP A CHILD</p>
        <p>should begin as soon as it began to think. Their aim should be not to grade children according to their age so much as their mental capacity, and that suitable teachers should be provided to keep them abreast of the level of their intellectual development. (Hear, hear). Instead of a teacher having a class of 50 to GO children it was possibly the wisest and most economical system to give them half the number, if the result should p rove a quickened intelligence and a hi&amp;er level of thought in the children's minds. Children as a rule were much more intelligent than they got credit for, and Unless they fed their mentality with the right quality of knowledge they were apt to find out for themselves that sort of knowledge it was very undesir- able they should possess. Even under the most favour- able circumstances the great bulk of workingmen's children were bound to be associated with much that was undesirable and there was therefore all the greater need that their school life should be associated With all that was pleasant and elevating to the mind and to the development of that self-respect which was the greatest safeguard they could give them in their jour- ney through life. Mental gifts and noble qualities were not the perquisite of the rich and ntibly-born; ' they were God's ',rifts to rich and poor alike and their development too frequently was dependent upon the means to make suitable provision or the lack of insight to perceive the right quality in the midst of its environ- ment. The activities of a child's mind required con- sideration. (Hear, hear.) Here again they required to discriminate between the slower, thoughtful, common- sense quality and the bright, quick-witted and intuitive quality which was often more nearly allied to genius than common-sense. (Hear, hear.) In fact, so varied were the conditions under which education was given that they could not draw anybard and fast line without its disadvantages. All that they could do was to try and make the best of the material at handand so qualify the children for entering the Secondary schools well grounded and prepared for the higher studies that awaited them without having to unlearn much that it had taken time and money to acquire. (Hear, hear.) He held also that it was the greatest economy to spend time money and thought in their elementary schools to attain that harmonious working and efficiency which led up naturally to the higher subjects taken in the Secondary schools and finally at the University. Even to children who would never get beyond the sixth standard it was far better for them to be highly trained in the best methods by most competent teachers, and under most favourable influences which would affect their futiweln life. The Edacation Committee had been blamed for establishing A SECONDARY SCHOOL</p>
        <p>mainly on the ground of expense, but also that it was their duty ,to make proper and suitable provision for elementary education first. The Education Committee had met a long-felt want in the town by utilizing the rooms at their disposal and had raised the quality of the education to be given at the minimum of expense to the town. ([Tear, hear.) They had relieved the elementary schools to the extent of nearly all the boys and girls that had been taken into the _ _ . _ _ _</p>
        <p>Secondary sc.hool and had in no way neglected their duty as regarded making suitable provision for elemen- tary education. By doing this, he believed .they had stimulated other education authorities to move in the direction of giving still higher educational advantages to the town and completing the chain by which the child of the poor man might receive an education equal to the richest in the land, and fitting him for the higher occupations of life which were open to all who were qualified to take them. (Hear, hear.) ,He must now ask them to.consider 'WHAT IS BEING DONE FOR VIE GIRLS.</p>
        <p>The same state of efficiency in mental culture was main- tained for the girls as the boys. Their domestic duties were not overlookei, but these were entrusted to speci- ally trained teachers fot such subjects as health, cookery, needlework, laundry-work, and nursing to the sick—all subjects of the greatest importance in house- hold management on slender means, if there was to be comfort combined with independence, and some pro- vision for the future. The subject of</p>
        <p>INF ANT SCHOOLS</p>
        <p>had not received the thought and care it deserved, but he hoed the day was not far distant when they would 4ti see well-appointed infant schools close to • the home of the children with capable staffs imparting e rudiments of knowledge awl made interesting .nd attractive by excellent teaching and a growing sense of capacity in the children themselves. When they got rid of the theory of the natural depravity of man and looked upon the mental and physical activities of chil- dren as signs Of a healthy life, lnd treated their ques- tions and actions with the respect and consideration •they deserved, they would begin to understand what were he possibilities of self-control And moral order as the result of reason and a balanced mind. [JUVENILE DEPRAVITY AND LA.WLESSNESS</p>
        <p>was too often the penalty of parental neglect and the want of control of those faculties which sought its pleasure in pure physical enjoyment which all healthy children so thoroughly enjoyed. Their rudeness in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>BETTER THAN MONEY IN THE SAVINGS BANK.</head>
        <p>BETTER THAN MONEY IN THE SAVINGS BANK.</p>
        <p>The working man to-day gives more attention to the food he has to eat than his forefathers gave, as he realises that his health—the lbank—depends very largely upon the properties of what he eats and drinks. It follows, *en, that the popular article of food is that which is easy to digest • but more popular still are preparations which are found to aid and strengthen the digestive organs, and also to act as an energiser for the whole system. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is such a food.</p>
        <p>It places a means in the hands of everyone to build up and maintain a sound constitution, which enables its possessor to travel life's journey without the aches and pains which are in many 'cases preventable. Thus we come round again to. sound common sense based on experience.</p>
        <p>manner and speech, their slowness of thought or the want of thought, and their low standard of morals were all the result of want of care and proper training. They were frequently drawn in their play to associate with the corrupt, and when children were taught as a religious belief that they were born in sin, and were the children of Wrath, we must not be surprised if they took it literally and looked upon it as the natural cOnditickn of their existence. He believed as firmly as any priest or pastor in the necessity of a religious education, but he did not. 'believe in a parrot system of repeating dogmatic forms of expres- sion of belief, which could not be understood until. the</p>
        <p>mord and spiritual life had been quickened into a per- ception of nitoral and spiritual truths. He claimed that</p>
        <p>THE NEW EDUCA.TION ACT</p>
        <p>had been brought into operation in -cVarrington very, ti,nickly, without any religous bickering. Its organisa- tion, though far from perfect, was well-advanced, and efficient, and if any department was behind it was not the fault of the committee but owing to the difficulties of solving sectional matters which • were hard to reconcile under new conditions. The status and pay of the teachers had been improved with the result of a greater interest in the work of education and a deter- mination to introduce better methods and get better results than hitherto. The committee had established a secondary school with an excellent:staff and the promise of splendid results if the present rate of progress was maintained. The con'imittee was not unmindful of the requirements of the teachers themselves in the study of their own profession and one of its latest acts had been to require the regular attendance at the classes, specially devoted to the requirements of those intending to devote their lives to teaching as a profes- sion. The committee had been called extra,vagant because it had refused to be guided by those ecimonusts who could not successfully manage their own affairs and some of the most active membershad been described as the</p>
        <p>"OLD GANG"</p>
        <p>who ought to be removek-bi-ihOse who were entirely ignorant of the amount of thoughtful preparation given by them-to the requirements of the new act. He felt free to make this claim on behalf of the likrucation Com- mittee because personally he had clone littleto win such well-merited success. The work had been done by the sub-committees—one of the most active members of which was Mr. Yonge—and the staff who had devoted a great amount of careful study and given much thought- ful consideration to the various interests concerned. It was the happy combination of youthful energy with the matured judgment of men who thoroughly understood the work in which they were engaged and who from a high sense of public duty were devoting themselves most earnestly to the work. It was for the ratepayers to say how far this excellent work should be continue or Checked, for it depended upon the type of men they erected to fill their public positions, whether they were well or ill- governed. He ventured to give</p>
        <p>A WORD OP-WARNING before it was too late, that much of the future progress of the town depended upm their 'electing men: of 'high character, broad views and a self-sacrificing devotion to high ideals of public life, combined with a courtesy of manner that could discuss public questione without rudeness and imputing the worst motives to, those who differed front them. (Hear, hear.) The, difficulty of getting the best men to take their share 'of public'work was always present where they had to face- the, ordeals of an election contest. The democratic form of government was based upon sound principles,, but it re- quired a high level of public opinion to maintain it without running to the extreme of tyrannical oppres--. sion or that indifference which was the opportunity of base and designing demagogues. Tts greatest safety was in the well-balanced minds of an educated people, whose vitality was renewed from day to day by the blessings ofi self-government, where the willing mind followed reason in the obedience of law and sacrificed itself to promote the public good. For the first time. in our national history we had the opportunity of establishing what'he believed would prove to be a truly</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>NATIONAL SYSTEM OF EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL SYSTEM OF EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Much of its success would depend upon the men elected to administer the Act and the spirit in which it was worked. It was not perfect by any means. The tyrannical use of a democratic majority had done its worst in the framing of clauses which the best friends of the church had already condemned, but let them hope that its defects would be remedied in the light of experience' and not by the exercise of party strength combined with religious bitterness. He could see that heavy demands would have to be made for the building and equipment of provided schools but he would ask them not,toallow any feeling of false economy blind them to the immense advantages an efficient system of elementary, secondary, and higher education would confer upon their children. There was no public money they could spend to better advantage than equipping their children with, those moral and intellectual safeguards which developed their self-respect and destroyed any feeling of inferiority when brought in contact with the more highly-fryoured and cultured minds. The ignorant were always at a disadvantage, and some, because the prey of those social parasites who lived by legal robbery and were one of the greatest curses that afflicted the ignorant poor. What they had to do was to spend freely and wisely in educat- ing our young people to think for themselves in purity of thought, with an open mind, 101 by high ideals that esteemed nobility of character of more importance than the getting of riches or the vain ambitions of a petty mind. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION AT HEATHSIDE CONTINUATION SCHOOLS. Mr. A. J. Jolley and Educational Matters.</head>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION AT HEATH- SIDE CONTINUATION SCHOOLS. Mr. A. J. Jolley and Educational Matters.</p>
        <p>The progress made by the students at Heathside Continuation Schools received tangible recognition on Monday evening when the annual distribution of prizes took place in the presence of a large gathering. The Rev. W. Bracecatup presided and Mr. A. J. Jolley pre- sented the awards which consisted of useful books. The Rev. W..BRICECAMP, at the outset, alluded to Mr. Jolley's interest in educational matters. Continuing he said they were delighted with the results that the students had achiev‘Al during the past twelve months.. He believed Heathside night school was one of the first in Warrington, in fact he thought it was the pioneer school of its kind. He hoped it would continue to maintain its position both in regard to numbers and efficiency. Great credit was dueto Mr. Parker and his - staff for the way in which they had worked. They would have their reward in knowing that the students had attended regularly and that the school was in such an efficient state. He hoped that in the future, the Education Committee would be kind cAnough to offer encouragement by presenting prizes. He urged upon the students the importance of improving their educa- tion after they had left the elementary schools and said the evening classes afforded a splendid opportunity of acquiring additional knowledge. (Applause). Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said they were standing at an im- portant point in the history of education both in Warrington and throughout the country. All the students present that night had magnificent opportunities— opportunities far better than those who weresome years olderever had. Mr. BraCecamp had reminded them that that school was one of the flrst,if not the first continua- tion school in Warrington, and the prizes to be dis- tributed that night were the last that would be given under the old system. He most sincerely hoped that , under the new system, when the schools instead of being conducted by private managers at considerable risk were being conducted by the town as a .public authority, those schools would keep the position they had held in the past and be not only the most success- ful evening school's •in Warrington but one of the most successful schools in England. It reflected very great credit not only on Mr. Parker and his staff but on those who conducted the classes before Mr. Parker' came amongst them that the schools had always stood very high not only in Warring- ton and in Lancashire, but actually in the front rank of schools of the same class in England. Whatever changes might be carried out by the Hdtication Committee and the Town Council it was quite certain that they would not be for the worst in regard toaffording opportunities to every boy and girl in Warrington to improve their education, and it rested with the young people to take advantage of the facilities offered. They might rest, assured that they would not go backwards in Warring- ton. In many towns they had had a great deal of trouble, quarrelling and unpleasantness about the change in the methods of education. So far in Warringto— they had got along with very little trouble of that kind. Considering the large number of schools .which had to be taken over and the changes in the methods of administration, he, thought they might congratulate themselves upon having worked very smoothly indeed, and upon the fact that Warrington was going to keep up its name of being in the front rank in educational matters. In regard to evening schools, the education they received there was notcompletoin itself, but ought to be only a step to something higher. The students present *tight consider that they wore standing on the _ • . _ _</p>
        <p>lower rungs of the ladder and it rested with the-mselves as to how high they asc.nded. They had not all got equal abilities, but at least they could make the best use, by diligent study,of such abilities as they possessed He sincerely hoped they would do so, not mertly from the selfish motive of bettering themselves in the ordin- ary sense of the word, but that they would remember that in a few years' time it would rest with them to play their parts as citizens of the greatest empire in the world. They heard people talk nowadays about democracy. They said the power rested in the hands of the people. Let them. bear in mind that this meant that in a few years everyone of them would be a responsible citizenand to some extent would be responsible not only for the government of their town, county and country, brit, of all our territories all over the world, many of which were.inhabited by people on a verymuch lower level of civilization than we were. It was for those he was addressing to be the pioneers of Progress not only for their own benefit but much morelor the benefit of those people who were scattered practically all over the world. They should take a pride in the welfare of their town and also in feeling that they were voters. In their Lands rested the power to say whether in future they were to try and spread progress in the best sense of the word. in asking his hearers to realize the responsibility which would devolve upon them in</p>
        <p>their votes, Mr. Jolley pointed out that in America, _in- speaking of elections, people said "Vote early and often" and the vote was regarded simply as something to sell. That was a very degrading conception of a man's duty and the man who sold his vote degraded himself more than the man who bought it from him. The matter which was DOW engaging the earnest atten- tioa of the Education Committee was the arranging of a scheme whereby every boy and girl who had ability might rise from the elementary to the higher grade schools, and then; if they had brains, industryand energy ,enough, to the universities. In future, it was hoped, it would not be poverty• that would, prevent any boy or girl from: attaining the highest. level which their faculties would enable them to reach. However high the students at those classes might climb he hoped they would always take a pride in remember- ing that they were once scholars in Heathside school. The success of the schools, concluded Mr. Jolley, de- pended to a very large extent on the scholars, and; to use a colloquialism, he hoped they would "buck up" and make Heathside school even awe successful in the future than it had been in the past. (Applause). Mr. JOLLEy then djstri bitted the prizes as follows : (1) Winners of prizes presented by Mr. J. Reid Walker: C. Hardman, J. D. Longshaw, E. Dower, W. Eddie, J. Wakefield, J. Glover, J. H. Granger, W. Griffiths, J. Smith, W. Clayton; (2) presented by Mr. J. H. Smethurst: Stanley lace,ll. James; (3) presented by the Rev. W. Bracecamp: H. Mason; (4), other prize winners : J. Hudson, E. Lloyd, J. W. Johnson, S. Jones, W. Waring. T. Hardman, A. Bennett, W. Gibson, W. Leigh,. A. E. Taylor, J. Beecham, J. Wootton, W. E. Wright, W. Allen, A. Jolley, A. S. Percival, A. Roberts; A. Long, H. Lightfoot, J. Riley, T. Williamson, W. Crofts, D. Caddick, J. Ashworth, G. Arber, T. Gregory, J. Barker, J. Lloyd, G. Roberts, T. Gill, R. Egerton, J. Green, A. G. Pemberton,T. Hughes, H. Bloor, H. Broadhurst, J. Bradshaw, T. Williams, T.. Crofts, J. Dutton, H. Holt, T. Helsby, W Morgan,. W. Pagett,G. Vernon, S. Ashbrooke, W. Beadeu,S.Holt, J. Johnson,T. Jackson, S. Wood, J. Oxley, E. Eden, J. Dolan, G. Edw. irds, J. Waywell, E. Caldwell,. J. W. Perry, P. Barber, J. Seacombe, F. Hartley, J. J. Waring,. G. Rutter., C. Roscoe, A. Drinkwater, J. Weed, Brobbin, S. Ince, F. P. Sutton, T. Waywell. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Jollenon the proposition of the Rev. W. BRACECAMP, seconded by Air. J. W. PARKER (head master.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND PARLI AMENT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND PARLI AMENT.</p>
        <p>THE COMING ELECTION.</p>
        <p>An Outside View of Liberal Prospects.</p>
        <p>Monday's " 31auchester Guardian" in a series of special articles on election prospects in Lancashire constituencies; says :—Warrington is one of those boroughs to which Mr. Chamberlain's new policy makes an immediate appeal. It is true the alleged decay of Warrington will, no more survive examination than that of the country generally. The iron and wire in- dustries employ more men and occupy a larger place in the life of the borough than they have ever done before, and greater local competition naturally tends to diminish individual) profits. The manufacturers may find that their separate incomes are smaller than they were thirty years ago,when the industries were in fewer hands, but at the same time employment has been in- creased and the qpiality of their products improved There are many intelligent persons in Warrington who. doubt if the wireindustry of the town would have suffered so little as it has if the Germans had not been good enough to, dump upon our shores a superior quality of raw material at a low price. But with more manufacturers turning old wire rope in Warrington itself thereare necessarily more caseslof interrupted em- ployment than there were when the factories were not sufficient in number to meet the de- mand and some employers explain these eases to their workmen with the repetition "German dump- ing." There are the facts of the occasional stoppages, and the German explanation is temptingly easy and saves one the trouble of thinking. So it comes about that there is a large bodT of Protectionists in Warring- , ton. Mr. Robert Pierpoint and many of his supporters have committed themselves to a policy which would restore the Warrington wire trade to its old position— that is, a position in which there would be fewer and richer employers,and, at the same time fewer and less well paid workmen , What are the local prospects that such a calamity will be averted? The Conservatives have held the repre- sentati9n of Warrington for nearly seventy years. It is a circumstance worth remembering in these days that. Sir Gilbert Greenall,.who was the member from 1847 to 1892, with two interruptions, entered Parliament as a Peelite„ the convenient rame in those times for a Con- servative Free Trader. He was beaten by a Liberal in 1868 and again in 1880. But in its history Warrington is Conservative, and at "the fraudulent election" Mr. Pierpoint had a majority of 1,165. At that election the Liberals had an excellent candidate, Mr. A. 11. Crosfleicl a thoughtful, . painstaking politician who expresses strong views in scrupulous language and gives the bearer an immediate impression of sincerity and firmness. Mr. Crosileld had a brother in the South African war, but in the controversy upon the rights and wrongs or that matter conviction led the candidate himself to stand upon the unpopular side. There were dissensions in the Liberal party at that time, and unfortunately they have not quite been got rid of yet. Registration has been diligently attended to, and the organization would he strong if the chief Liberals la Warrington would lay more stress upon their common principles and less upon theii differences in regard to details. If they , should be fortunate enough to have Mr. Cresfield for Itheir candidate at the next election there is reason to hope that, with unity among themselves, they would, wrest the seat from the Protectionists.</p>
        <p>There is a Labour Representation Committee at War- rington, and Mr. Alexander Wilkie, of Newcastle-orr Tyne, is mentioned ma possible Labour candidate. Mr. Wilkie is the general secretary of the Associated Ship- wrights' Society, he was a member of the Mosely Cony miSsion, and he has been president of the Trade Unions Congress. He polled 8,84.2 votes at Sunderland in 1909, though there were three other candidates for the two seats. He would be an excellent member of Parliament.</p>
        <p>"-At' Warringtón,-};(:;‘,;;W:ti:;;; is no chance of hiTs-u-o" ceedting .evert if the Liberals stood aside. The ship- wrights of the borough who no doubt would vote for him, are few ;unlimber, and, -.thether it is to be lamented or not, there is no general, desire in Warrington for e Labour member. The only hope of winning the repre- sentation of the borough for the forces of Free Trade consists in the union of the Liberal and Labour parties under the lead of Mr. Crosfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>WESTERN v. STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>On the Western ground which was in good conditioo, but fog made play rather difficult during the last quarte:" of an hour. Result :—Western, four; Stockton Heath,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ROYAL v. SALE MOOR.</p>
        <p>Played at Sale on Saturday. From the start the visitors attacked, Hatton scoring. A few minute afterwards Davenport. added a second. After the in" terval the homesters had to defend for the most part, Gerard and Davenport penetrating their defence. Fog interfered with the last ten minutes of the ga,me• Result :—Warrington Royal, four goals, Sale Moor, nil.</p>
        <p>, F. ILCRES IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According . , "K np's Mercantile Gazette," the total 'Umber o,' Failures recorded in England and Wales during the wee' ending Friday, Dec. 4th, was 196, viz.:—New Ban'ruptcy Proceedings published in the London 'Gazette, 103; deeds of arrangement registered, 93. The re' keptive munbers in the corresponding week of last yeaf were :-13aukruptcles, 93; deeds of arrangement, 93;; total 186 ; being a net decrease in bankrvptcies of lv' and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in 1903, t° date, of 351.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Watts and Co. have just issued, in their R..P.A. Cheap Reprints Series, the third edition (colii: plating 60,000 copies) of Herbert Spencer's Edncatio,P. Intellectual, Moral, and Physical. The work itself 1-1, been translated into sixteen languages. The presey reprint, issued with the approval of the late Spencer, follows exactly the 1878 edition, and is p 0 ished at sixpence.</p>
      </div>
