      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>WHAT I HAVE SAID-</p>
        <p>AND WHAT I- HAVE TIEPENTED</p>
        <p>SAYING</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S SUPPRESSED - SPEECHES. _</p>
        <p>Remarkable Disappearance MR. CHAMBERLAIN has issued, in volume form, a number of the speeches he has delivered durinf, his Fiscal cknpaign. In a preface to the volume Mr. Chamberlain infornis us that his speeches, as they are now presented to the public, describe fairly, and with sufficient fulness, the objects which Tariff Reformers have in view.</p>
        <p>His campaign commenced with the celebrated speech at Birmingham on May 151 b, when he launched his famous thunderbolt against Free Trade.. That speech, appropriately enough, appears first among the bound volunie, of speeches. But what is most remarkable about this volume is the total disappearance ff four most important speeches on this subject, three of which were delivered in the House of Commons. The speeches to .which we refer were delivered as follows:</p>
        <p>House of Commons ... May 20th, •1903. June 17th, 1903. Constitutional' Club ... June 26th, 1903. House of Commons ... July 23rd, 1903. The British oublie ought not to be deprived of the pleasure and profit to be obtained from a careful and leisurely perusal of these speeches; they are far too good to be banished to the oblivion to which .their author would unkindly consign them.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberin!n a Cobdenite. WHEN Mr. Chamberlain. in the House of" Commons, first unfolded his plan, . he was, apparently, a disciple of Mr. Cobden. He said it was quite a mistake to suppose he pro- posed to reverse the Fiscal policy of the country. He said he was "absolutely unaware of any such proposal having been made," either by himself or his colleagues! He further explained that he adhered to the fundamental principles of Free Trade, which would not be interfered with either by himself or anybody else. He even went so far as to say that Free Trade had been a fundamental principle of the Tory party for a very long period, aid his whole speech was to try and shew that the last thing he would attempt would be an, attack upon Free Trade.; It is quite unnecessary to remind anybody that Mr. Chamberlain has travelled a long way backward since May 28th. From Glasgow to Cardiff he has stumped the country advocating Protection, and holding up Protectionist' countries as a perfect heaven of prosperity and contentment.</p>
