      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>P.S.A. MOVEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE P.S.A. MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>Temperance Meeting at Wycliffe Church,</p>
        <p>ADDRESS BY MR. 20RELAND.</p>
        <p>In:connection with the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society,there Was a temperance rally on the Fair Ground on Sunday afternoon. Despite the damp and foggy weather, there Was a satisfactory response. The members of the Rechabites and Good Templars' organisations turned out in good numbers. A procession was formed and after parading some .of the streets of the town, pro- ceeded to Wycliffe Church where a temperance meeting was held. Mr. P. O'Brien presided Over a large attend- ance. He observed that he considered it a great honour to preside over a meeting of that . character. H 9 had learnt to appreciate the worth of the pastor of Wbcjiffe Church, who for many years had been in the forefront of the temperance advocates 'of the town. There were also many men associated with Wycliffe Church who had done yeoman service in the • temperance __cause in Warrington. For 33 years he (the Chairman) had been a total abstainer, and he had never had cause to regret his adherence to the principles of total abstinence. Mr. E. BORELAND, secretary of the United Kingdom Alliance;pointed out that for a great many years past the temperance forces had been advocating ta curtail- ment in the number of licenses, and magistrates in Many places had come to the conclusion that there were too many public-houses. A number of licences were refused at the beginning of the year, and magistrates had wArned the liquor:partythat unless they put their houses iirder mote licenses would be refused. Their warning had frightened the liquor men everywhere,except in War- rington. They had -approached the Prime Minister. 'Tie did not complain of that but it was only right that 'the temperance people should have the same privilege. The Prime Minister had been asked to receive a temper- an3e deputation but he had not found time to accede to the request, and the position of the sober-minded class of the community had not been placed before the Prime Minister. Mr. Boreland proceeded to discuss the proposed compensation to the trade" when licences were extinguished, and said if the Govern- ment placed compensation on the statute book of the country it would establish a vested interest in every licence. Such a proposal was absolutely Unnecessary, and temperance people ',A-6111d never agree to it. He appealed to all temperance people to support only those candidates at elections Who were prepared to support measures calcu- lated to diminish the drink evil.—Miss Fairhurst's sweet contralto voice was heard to advantage in the solos "0 Dry those tears" and "Queen of Angels." Mr. W. H. Payton. Mus. Bac.,presided at the organ. The President (Rev. ;John Yonge) announced that two new members had been enrolled. • ANNUAL TEA MEETING</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting in connection with Wycliffe P.S.A. was held on Monday evening, and was as suc- cessful as its predecessors. About 250 sat down to tea in-the schoolroom. A public meeting was afterwards -held in the Memorial Hall, presided over by Mr. F. Monks, -T. P. In his address, Mr. Monks very ably re- viewed the work of the Education Committee. His re- "narks will be found in another column. An address was delivered by the Rev. I). C. TENCKETt of Wigan (president of the P.S.A. Federation, He referred to the successful work which was being accom- Plished ny the P.S.A. societies throughout the country. They had done much to raise their ftglow men from the gutter and lift them out of social degradation. He encouraged the Wycliffe Society to continue their good work. _ _</p>
        <p>A programme of vocal and instrumental music was submitted. The 3rd ciass males in connection ,with the Wycliffe Sunday'School contributed two glees under the conductorship of Mr. T. F. Wallington. Songs were contributed by Miss A. Travis, Miss A. Griffith., Mr. Roberts and Mr. A. -Cotterill, the last named artiste being encored. Mr. Critchley was also encored for his 41umorons song. A pianoforte solo was played by Miss Jessie Farrar, and Mr. T. F. Wallington contributed a -violin solo. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., played the pianoforte accomnanime,nts. Excellent n,rrangements had been de by the P.S.A. Committee and the hon. secretaries (Itltessrs.. B. Farrar and A. Downes).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WARRINGTON P.S.A. SOCIETY. •</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON P.S.A. SOCIETY. •</p>
        <p>An open meeting of tlle Warrington P.S.A. Society ivas held in the co-operative Hall, Saßkey-street, on Sunday afternoon. _</p>
        <p>Councillor•Jo)FtN itouturrs, who presided.' expressed the pleasure it afforded him to see such a good attend- mice. This was the first time be had presided over a P.S.A. meeting. He urged the young men present to be ttuthful, honest, .steadfast, and sober, and he Nrmlv believed that if they acted upon that advii;e they would become good arid true men, be respected and hold good positions in society. He had seen the sad effects which hail resulted from persons not acting laPon his advice. Their homes had been ruined, they end. their wives had been made miserable, and the wolf of poverty bad entered the house. (Applause). Councillor JAMES EVANS gave an address. on 'Christian Citizenship," and urged all present to take a stand for Christ in the world, end set a good example to all around. The speaker referred to the evils of betting and drink, and said that until the law was made tilore stringent we could not expect much reform in these two great evils. The law should make it easier for people to do right and harder for them to do wrong As to poor law administration, it was a shame that for very shilling which was obtained for that cause, nine- Pence was spent in working the law, 4.nd only three- pence was put to the cause for which it was intended -7-the assistance of the poor. He trusted the day would 'come when these reforms would take place and then we Should see England—good as it was—the finest country in the universe. He wished the canse for which they • Were gathered success. (Applinise),• An excellent programme of sacred -music was pro- vided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
