      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>All for the Colonies.</head>
        <p>All for the Colonies.</p>
        <p>IN this same speech Mr..Charnberlain made it perfectly clear that his one and only object was to give Preference to the Colonies at the expense of our people at home. There was no question of British industries or foreign dumping. Indeed, we were not even to be consulted in the matter. We were to give Mr. Chamberlain carte blanche to make what arrangements he chose with the Colonies. Said he: When the Plan will be Produced.</p>
        <p>When we have got a mandate it will-be time toroduce a plan—a plan in the sense of a ,definite and complete scheme is absolutely impossible until we . know a great deal con- cerning which we have still to inquire, .and into which we cannot inquire profitably and advantageously unless. we have got the country behind us. Mr. Chamberlain quickly realised that the country would not be likely to give him a mandate until it knew vhat his plan was. Accordingly, he has been stumping the country since October 6th, describing his plan, and the issue of his speeches in volume form signifies its completion. He gave some indica- tions of his plan in his first speech in the House of Commons. It is interesting to note how completely the plan has chan2ed. Then he thought of the Colonies only. Said he: The first thing to be done is to consult the Colonies.</p>
        <p>During the debate Sir Charles Dilke had urged that Australian opinion was opposed to Mr. Chamberlain's scheme. Mr. Chambet lain replied:</p>
        <p>It is very difficult to anticipate what that opinion would really be, but 1 .am quite sure that everything would depend upon what we have to offer. . . . Does he think we should be such fools as to offer the Australians something which would not be of any advantage to them, and expect them in return to alter their system, and give Up Protectiou in our favour ?</p>
        <p>From this it is evident that Mr. Chamberlain's whole plan was simply to give a Preference to the Colonies, and leave foreign Free Trade relations untouched, except o far as they might interfere with the working of his Colonial schemes. , • How , completely different is his scheme to-day, when .he is goirg round the country urging us to protect ourselves agitinst foreign imports! Why this change The reason is simple. Mr. Charyi erlain is a great electioneering hand, and he quickly dropped Colonial Preference nhin he fcund it created no enthusiasm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Workman Will Pay</head>
        <p>The Workman Will Pay</p>
        <p>IN those brave days of May, Mr. Chamber- lain told us that part of the ease we should have to. meet was that the cost .of for the working classes would Le increased. He was going to the workmen and was going to tell them: . Now this policy, if it is carried out, will cost the working man so much a week more than at , preunt.</p>
        <p>Chamberlain seems to have thought at that time that the workmen would be de- lighted to have to pay more for their food; said he:</p>
        <p>Who is going to pay this tax ? The working classes arc going to pay throe-fourths of it. How things have changed since May! Now Mr. Chamberlain tells us his scheme is not going to cost the workers a farthing--indeed, says be, their cost of livin will be less. Last May Mr. Chamberlain said his scheme was not intended to give Protection to the British farmer, but Preference to the Colonial farmer and Old Age Pensions to the 'British 'labourer. The proposals of May were made when Chathberlain apparently thought himself so pcpular that the, public would accept any policy that he chose to dictate. Accordingly, he took no pains to hide the actual results of his policy. To-day Mr. Charnberlain has dis- covered that he is no longer the master, but only the servant of public opinion, and :tecordingly he tries to make the public believe that his views of taxed food and their views of cheap food ere one and the same! hence the carly candid speeches of May are thrown aside for the delusive and untrustworthy utterances of November.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday December 12, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES A.ND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES A.ND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football,</p>
        <p>. By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Warrington were reported to have won by eleven points to six, at the conclusion of the match with Wigan on Saturday. I say reported, advisedly, for no man, :unless perchance is was the referee, could swear on the evidence of his own eyes that such .was the case. Eyes were of only slight value on Satur- day, for the. Fog Fiend 'descended and obscured everything which was not within very close quarters. There was considerable doubt as to whether the match would be played for some time, but Referee Kennedy was in no humour for wasting an afternoon. He found that from the centre of the field both goal-posts were visible at 2.45. so the players were ordered to turn out and get to work. This they did under con- ditions which suggested that the match would never be finished.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the fog a good crowd assembled, and the first few shouts from the arena brought in the doubters, so that, after all, Warrington had an ;:.8.3 gate. There should have been aby up in the first minute for Mahoney readied the line after a cross-kick but was held befme he could pass to Fish. This auspicious opening was not ' the forerunner of such 'good things as were anticipated, for Wigan re- pelled the invasion with vigour and were soon in the home half. They had an advantage forward which was made full use of. Davies attempted to dribble from the rear of the pack, but was robbed by his namesake on the Wigan side, who beat Hallam and kicked across for Hilton to open the score with a try inclose proximity to the posts. There was no goal, but the reverse was quite sufficient for Warrington to see visions of a repetition of the • defeat by Wigan on Easter Monday. They promptly bestirred themselves therefore and after a slashing run by Dickenson the ball was carried over the Wigan line and Cook equalised amid cheers that fairly rent the fog. Fish missed an easy goal and the gloom settled down once more. Half-time arrived with the scores equal, and the players also arrived in full view of the stand at the same time. .</p>
        <p>On resuming there did not seem probability of the match proceeding very much further. The players could only be seen when they auproached the touch-lines and one bad to judge largely from the tone of the shouts in different. directions what was trans- piring. The visitors were not much concerned, apparently, and they kept a single eye to business, with the result that Hallam was caught napping and a try recorded under the posts after ten minutes play. I don't suggest that the home full back was really asleep, but when the ball descended upon him out of the darkness he did not seem to recogize it and the Wigan forwards gave him no time for a formal intro- duction. The goal-posts Were distinctly visible to the player who kicked for goal but he missed the target once more. About this time the Warrington crowd was under the impression that their favourites were defeated for they seemed to have lost the fire and dash necessary to win and there was a possibility that the proceedings might be concluded any minute. Desperate efforts forced Wigan back and Fish bad two oppor- tunities which he muffed badly amid the heart-felt groans of the stand occupants who now had a view of the pioceedings. The third time did the trick, how- ever. Fish made a dazzling run on the left and when hemmed in he screwed the ball across to Dickenson who fielded smartly and grounded near the posts. Fish kicked a goal and Warrington had won, to the exuberant joy of the crowd, the majority of whom saw nothing of the actual scoring. They were quite satis- fied to join in the frantic yells of the more favoured few at the railway end. wiloiatz</p>
        <p>It was—really a farce to go .on at this stage, for the game was quite invisible at times. Every now and then a phantom ball would come hurling through the i mist with a lot of ghostly figures after t, who would promptly disappear again into the profound gloom elsewhere. Warrington seemed to be attacking mostly, and another ?ell from the south-east announced ihe scoring and a third try by Boardman— the first I believe he had ever scored for Warrington. Hard lines on the burly All, that he should have to notch the point while invisible to the majority of his admirers ! There was a little more play, but all that was seen on the members' side was a glorious upheaval of the lengthy Cooper by Fish, and then hostilities ceased with Warrington winners' by the good -margin of 1 goal Z tries to 2 tries. .</p>
        <p>One cannot comment very fully on an almost in- visible game, but several points of play were quite dear. Warrington badly missed Isherwood and Harris, though their substitutes were as good as any- thing on Wigan side. Dickenson, too was good and bad by turns, in fact the Warrington backs seemed rather befogged- on many oeCasioUs. Fancy Fish missing two passes with an almost clear course before him ! At half back, Bate was better than Davies, though the pair generally beat the .opposing couple when they got the ball. In the 'scrum Warrington were overplayed by a line forward team, and it was only speed and resource behind which enabled them to win. The Wigan backs are only a moderate crew notwithstanding the money spent in collecting them. Harris (centre) and Davies (hall) were the most promising.</p>
        <p>The match counted of course, in• the South-west Lancashire Competition of which Warrington a-re still the leaders. The table up-to-date is as follows : Pts.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. for agst Pts Warrington ... 2 ... 0 ... 1 19— 9 ... 5 Wigan St. Helens Runcorn 2 ... 1 ... 1 ... 0 15-12 ... 2 Leigh 3 ... 1 ... 2 ... 0 15-18 ... 2 Widnes</p>
        <p>The league table up-to-date is as follows :-- _ For. Against _ _ _</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts Oldham 14...10... 8... 1...14 26-106... 5 K't'n Et 13... - 9... 3... 1...11 19— 79... 5 11-43...19 Leeds • 13... 9... 3... 1... 7 21— 77... 8 12-52...19 Bradford Salford l2 S. 3.. 1. 21 29-129... 5 11-43...17 Beghton R. 13... 8— 4... 1...-9 27-- 99...13 11-59...17 Hunslet 14... 5... 5... 1...17 21— 97...12 17-75...17 N'Va rriugton 13... 7... 16— 69....9 14-60...16 HA lifa x l3 G... 5... 2... 8 9 43... 9 9-45...14 Runcorn lincldersfild 14... 5... 9... 0... 7 22 80...15 25-105...10 Hull .12 4... . 1:..12 18— 78.-..19. 19-95... 9 Batley 'Swinton Widnes 14.. 3... ... 2...10 10— 50...18 32-132.. 8 Wigan Leigh Keighley -13....2.-.. 9... 2...13 7 47...19 29-125.. 6</p>
        <p>Widnes laid in wait for the Rovers on Saturday and deposed them from the leadership by winning lw a try to nil. Oldham went to the top again with clever win at Hunslet. Runcorn were -five points behind at Salford when the game was a bit ad ou ed</p>
        <p>Warrington hare a stiff task on hand to-day when hey go to Leeds, whose career since they -left the second division has proved them to be worthy of the best company. Their success all the season has been consistent and the last match at Headingly resulted in the downfall of Salford, while only a week ago Leigh were beaten at home by the Leeds men. Warrington have good and bad recollections of Headingly. Their last appearance theme. was in the cup final with Batley and the result is anything, but pleasant to lock back upon. In the .same season they met Leeds in the cup competitiolt with gratifying success, for the home team sustained an adverse score of 21 points. It is the 'Leeds match Warrington must recall to their minds to-day, and endeavour to repeat the result of the former encounter.</p>
        <p>Warringtoa will be strongly represented and ought, on all points, to give a good account of themselves. The forwards tvill be severely tried again. The team selected is : Hallam, full back • Fish. Isherwood. Dickenson and Hockenhall, three-quarters ; Bate and -Davies, half backs ; Boardman, Morrison, Edmondson, Swift, Thomas, C(ok, Naylor, and Jolley forwards.</p>
        <p>The " A team will be at home to Widnes Rangers, and will be represented by the following : .Knight. full back ; Savage, Mahoney, and Kenyon, three- quarters ; Evans and Duckworth, half backs ; Lunt, Harmer, Coleman, Forster, Woods and Swift, for- wards. Evans is the new capture from Pendlebury mid some interest will be taken in his debfit.</p>
        <p>Fish and Brardman assisted Lancashire to defeat Durham. and Northumberland by 6 goals 10 tries, to noth]nz at South Sideles on Wednesday. Fish scored lour goals three tries, One of which was after a run born) his own line.</p>
      </div>
