      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On •Sturday the annual novice race was run from the club's headquarters, the Bridge Inn. On account of the fog it was not considered fit to run the race across country, so it was decided to run it on the rbad to the "under .bridge" at Daresbury and back—a distance of about V; miles. Ten runners faced the starter,and after an absence of about 25 minutes it was seen that C. Berry (the local cyclist.) was first, closely followed by J. Stewart second, and H. Mounfield, another prominent cyclist., third. SANKEY v. ST. HELENS SACRED HEART.</p>
        <p>Sankey Harriet journeyed to St. Helens on Saturday. Representative teams turned out, and after traversing about three miles Sankey had their first seven men leading. A dense fog suddenly enveloped them, and they lost the trail. The result was that several of the runnels gcit lost, and had great difficulty in finding their way back to the Abbey Hotel,where they were quartered. The membecs afterwards sat down to tea, and an enjoy- able smoki*, concert followed.</p>
