      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.S</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must mall cases send their names ani addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side vely of the paper and sent in, if possible,not later than Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Trams and Christmas Day.</head>
        <p>The Trams and Christmas Day.</p>
        <p>'Sir,—Could you allow rue space in your 7vaktable Taper to draw attention to the running of cars on Christmas Day. Surely we could manage without them one day in 365, to enable those men who are employed on the tramways to have one day in the year with their friends. They are expected to work every holiday that comes and.' think it only right that they should: have the day we can best manage without them. Perhaps the Tramways Committee will give this request .a. little consideration. For myself I believe in the motto: Do unto others as you would be done by.—l remain, yours etc., W. ROBTNSON. Winwick-road, Warrington, Dec. , 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>inqpiry : Its Cost.</head>
        <p>The inqpiry : Its Cost.</p>
        <p>Sir,—After resorting tt. every device in their power to prevent an investigation into the alleged trading of -some of its members With the Corporation the 'fawn. -Council at last, under the pressure of public opinion, granted the inquiry demanded by the ratepayers. And thereupon arose a lord cry from more quarters than one that such an inquiry would involve the town in a huge 4expenditure Of time and money. Ose modest expert put down the cost st £5OO, but others more ambitious tor fame ran it up by leaps and bounds to £10,000! And the ratepayers will not have forgotten that all these predictions were lasnounced before s. single book had been opened or a single document examiuedl TheSP wonderful feats in arithmetical progres- sion proved that the computers • must have been familiar with some of the ways of the "" wise men "who deter Mine the attitude of celestial objects by inno•ictory triangulation. In fact the calcu- lations of our 'financial experts were moving illustra- tions of the power of that peculiar branch of science Jcalled in the vulgar tongue • The Rule of Thumb" to predicate the unknown. And strange to say, this bug- bear of cost seems to have blocked the inquiry for the present. But surely the ratepayers who are all deeply iinterested in the matter will, in public meeting or -otherwise, command that. the investigation shall be carried out promptly and thoroughly. So far as I know, it has never been alleged or even insinuated that there is anything. apart from :the trading, in the -accounts of the Corporation that should be bronght into the light of day. And therefsre to 'turn the in- quiry into a quad tribunal and the six honourable ,commissioners into amateur detectives, with a sinister object in view, woad bring scandal iipon the Boreggh, and do grievous wrong, not only-to the competent and trusted officers of the Corporation, but also.to the able and reliable accountants who have for many 'years .audited and vouched itsfinancial•transactions. Happily the farce is not ;needed. The truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth regarding the private trading -of Aldermen and Councillors with the Corporation will satisfy the public. And this, as I have pointed out in a previous letter, ecn-be obtainedtby a very simple and inexpensive process. Yours truly, lONA.</p>
        <p>activity upon which more thanaoy other factor de- pendsithe happiness of a people—or of personal neces- saries. A further 54 per cent consisted of luxuries, chiefly made up of articles of a special character, such as are not produced in this countiy, and are therefore not competitive. The balance 2.6 per cent, was made up of miscellaneous articles, such as tobacco, petroleuni, ecc." The case really becomes complete when we add that the United Kingdom exports .C 5 12s. of manu- factured goods per head of her population, while Germany and the United States export only £2 12s. and £1 3s. per head of their respective populations. Mr. Money rightly lays stress on the fact that Free Trade has given this country not merely wealth but Free Trade for its energies. No unsuit- able industry has been artificially fostered; the consumer has been left free to exchange the pro- ducts of hisiabour for the best value the world has to offer. And the process of natural selection has so'operated that we import a larger proportion of food and raw material and export a larger pro- portion of manufactured goods than any other country. If this is the object. there is no tariff</p>
        <p>system.which could accongilisli it half as well.</p>
        <p>- If a third extract be permissible then I think Major Coates' remarks to a reporter are too good to be lost. According to the newspaper he delivered himself as fellows "As a candidate T endorse what Mr. Balfour said in his Sheffield speech, and if returned T will support the policy embodied in it. If hit I will retaliate. So far as lam personally concerned, ho,wever, I sympathise withthe idea'of Mr. Chamberlain and would do all I can to see effect given to it. But I reserve my right of judgment as to details. As a business man I should want to know if we are to give something to the Colonies,what are we to have from the Colonies in exchange." Surely this ought to go down to posterity. Yours faithfully, A. H. CROSFIELD. London, Dec. 9th, 1903,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MEMORIAL TO FALLEN SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>MEMORIAL TO FALLEN SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED STATUE IN QUEEN'S GARDENS.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT THE TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington will, ere long,possess a public memorial in commemoration of the services of the South Lane shire Regiment (line, militia and volunteers) .cluringthe war in South Africa. A committee was appointed at a meeting held in the Town Hall on Wednesday after- noon to carry out this proposal.. The MAYOR .(Alderman Bolton) presided and there were also present: The Mayor of Widnes (Alderman ;Quinn), Colonel A. F. 0. Richardson (commanding the Bth 40th Regimental districts) Colonel Reid Walker (hon. Colonel of the Ist V. 8.), Colonel Ridgway, Colonel Edelsten, Major Roome, Captain Bundock (ad- jutant to the Ist V. 8.) Captain Osborne, Mr. William 'Roberts (retired Quarter-Master Sergeant Ist. South Lancashire Regiment), Mr. H. Roberts, J.P., Mr. F. Monks J.P., Alderman Fairclongh, Alderman Smet- hurst, Alderman Bennett, Councillor Rogers, Mr. J. December 9th, nO3. Lyon Whittle (town clerk). 1 • -- LETTERS OF APOLOGY. The Bewsey Ward Elections. The Towx CLERK said he had received severe Sin—l desire to deal with some remarks made by the messages of apoloo- for non-attendance. successful Liberal candidate for Bewsey Ward after the Major HALL telegraphed :—" I regret being unable to declaration of the psfillast Monday.At the Risen Hall"he attend meeting to-day but am sure the third Battalion =thanked the electere of Bewsey Ward for electing him will cordially approve of your proposal." in spite of all the unfair opposition hehad at the works Colonel R. Pilkington, M.P., wrote: • "In reply to where the leader of the purity brigade was employed." your letter of the 4th inst. I regret that I shall not be At the Liberal Club the speaker weld further. He said able to be present at the meeting to-morrow which is to "the men had been practically told in the forge that if consider the question of erecting a public memorial to they did not work for Dickenson and vote for him they the officers and men of the 'South Lancashire Regi- would not be supported." meat who lost their lives in the South African war, but These words are clearly intended to convey the ins- I shall be very pleased to join the movement and work pression that Tas "leader" of the purity movement with you. Would yoa kindly send me a list, of the usedundue influence tteson the workmen of my firm, who officers and men to whom the memorial is to be erected are voters in Bewsey Ward, in connection with last with the battalions to which they belonged." Monday's election: lam sure I need not say there is Major F. M. APPLETON wrote: "I ant sorry to say not the shadow of foundation for any such gratuitous that an engagement away from Warrington of some long and impudent suggestion. In fact I was so careful in -standing will prevent my being present at the meeting the matter that 1 did -not even speak to a single ;on Wednesday respecting the proposed memorial to the workman, directly or indirectly in regard to his vete, members of the South Lancashire Regiment who lost nor indeedne any elector, in the ward, and apart from _their lives in the, late war in South Africa. Willyou signing a circular containing a general anneal to the kindly be geed enough to tell the Mayor how sorry I entire electorate in favour of the Non-trading cause em not tobe present. Among the fallen are many with and candidates, I have never personallyeought to in- whom I had the honour of serving, incleding Colonel Iluence any ratepayer. If Councillor Peter Peacock can O'Leary, also our old Volunteer Adjutann and some six -find a single &amp;Lector of Beeney Ward who will say that of the Volunteers, including four of my old company. /I have approached him or in any way, directly or in- f need not' therefore say how dear this cause is to me. directly, sought to use inY POSition to coerce or even I hope and believe that a memorial worthy of this town anfluence his note or to influence him to work for or and of its distinguished regiment will be put up." . Against any candidate, I will hand twenty guineas to Mr. J. Charlton Parr, J.P., Mr. I. Fulton . Smith. and the Warrington Infirmary. Captain Berkeley Goren, of Orford Barracks, also wrote Suspicionitartats the guilty mind! I have said a regretting their inability to be present, but approving good deal as to the discreditable means and methods of the objects of the meeting. 4 employed by some of the halal Liberal leaders in the THE MAYOR MOVES THE FIRST RESOLUTION. ,Grford Ward on, November 2nd to effect the defeat of The MAYOR said he had convened the meeting at the the labour candidate, Councillor Stott, and I know request of several gentlemen interested in the matter that similar means and methods have . been for the purpose of providing a public memorial to the emnloyed by the same party in the two officers and men of the South Lancashire Regiment who subsequent electiocs in Bewsey Ward. And so, of lost their lives in the war in South Africa. The object course, it has to bejnsintutted that the Non-Trading appealed to him and he had asked the Mayor of St. candidates and their supporters were not themselves Helens, the Mayor of Widnes, and the commanding entirely above reproach on the occasion of the first of officers of the Volunteers of those towns to meet the these two by-elections. I believe there is no just groxed commanding officers of the line, militia and volunteers for nay such sugeestion,, lam so assutred by both the of the . district, together with a few:local gentlemen official "Non-Trading, ' candidates (all candidates who, he thotight, would be interested in the matter, for are now "Non-Trading") but if there had the purpose, if thought desirable, of forming a corn- been the slightest approach to anything in mittee for the purpose of raising funds to provide a the slightest degree irregular it weuld have been public memorial to the soldiers who lost their lives in entirely contrary to the bed-rock foundation and con- the war. They all knew the gallant work done by the ~ . slitutioe of the Non-Trading movement, and I should South Lancashires, ever foremost in the fighting line, have been the first to impeach any candidate or leader and to him personally it would appear as a great of the Non-Trading party who for a moment counten- reproach if no effort was made beyond what had been anced it. )1 anyone can and will now afford me reason- done by the militia battalion to commemorate their able proof of any irregularity whatever on the part of valour, their bravery, and their patriotism. (Applause.) any Non-Treeling candidate or responsible leader, Ishall The Mayor then moved the following resolution:— be prepared to deal drastically with the matter, so far "That a memorial in commemoration of the services of as lam perseually concerned..—l am, tizc„ tlo South Lancashire Regiment (line. 'militia, and. JAMES DODDS. volunteers) be raised by public subscription in War- December In. rington and the townships included in the area of the _______ 40th regimental district." w oe , The Fiscal Problem. The MAYOR OF ,WIDNF.S, on being called upon to second the resolution said he would willingly have done Sir,—Speaking to his constituents on Monday night , so, if, he had had authority to do so. He came there Mr, Bryce said: "The flag, of Protection waved from with a watching brief. He was instructed at the the Citadel, yet the Government pretended that it was meeting of the Town Council on the previous day to not committed to the taxation of food, but that it was attend and report to the General Purposes Committee in favour of fiscal reform. So ,were the Liberals, and with a view to taking the matter in hand. At the be (Mr. Bryce) desired a fiscal reform which should for present time he was not in a position to second the instance, tax land values and throw mere of the burden resolution, . •of taxation upon the luxuries of the rich, The Govern- Mr. F. Momes : It is not a Town Council matter. meat contended it was committed only to retaliation; The MAYOR OF WARRINGTON : Nothing has been put retaliation which had always failed when other coun- before our Town Council. tries had tried it. It was to be used by the Government Mr. F. neves seconded the resolution and ie was like the drop scene in a theatre. It had nothing to de carried: with theaction of the play, but it was to be kept before On the proposition of Mr. H. Roberts, it was decided the eyes of the spectators, while behind it the scene- that the gentlemen present be constituted a committee shifters were busy, so that when it was raised we should to carry out the resolution. see a Protectionist stage and Protectionist actors. Retaliation, if it meant anything, meant Protection, THE SITE OF THE PROPOSED MEMORIAL because it must lead to Protection. COLONEL RICHARDSON then proposel that the afore- ' These are familiar facts if you will, but still facts so said memorial be in the form of a statue to be erected , , Serious that they must be driven home. - in some public position—preferably the Queen's Gar- , Will you allow me to sand you a somewhat longer dens—in Warrington and that the Town Council be re- quotation front the "Westminster Gazette." People quested to grant the site." He pointed out that War- ; complain sometimes that there is a deal of special rington had been the headquarters /of the South pleading just now on the fiscal question, first from one Lancashire Regiment ever since the territorial system aide and then the other. They ask for a bird's .ctYe view had been introduced. The men connected with the of the whole problem before us. That is precisely what regiment were essentially local and- belonged to the Mr. Chiozza Money is now giving us, and I venture to etrrington district or St. Helens and Widnes, which draw your readers' attention to what the "Westminster were recruiting areas. The men connected with the Gazette" says of this book in the hope that it may line fought from the beginning of the war to the very find many readers in Warrington. end. A great many lost their lives, including the A FISCAL HANDBOOK. Colonel, four other officers and a large number of In spite of the flood of fiscal literature there is non-commissioned officers and men. It was only fitting still ample room for the admirable summary of the that a monument should be erected in some public place whole case which Mr. Chiozza-Monev presents in in Warrington, and that was preferable to the erection his handy volume "Elements of the Fiscal Problem." of a memorial in a church. The religious beliefs of the It is impossible for anyone who reads this book to regiment were about equally divided . between Roman complain that the Free Trade doctrine is abstract, Catholic and Protestant. Ho would suggest that the antiquated, or visionary. Never was a case pre. Corporation give them the site suggested. • sented in a more modern, concrete, or practical Major ROOME, insecondine, said the reejmeht was form. Free Trade is tested as a practical policy largely localand included Widnes and St.Helens men. for this country by the results of its trade. The This was specially so in connection with the Volunteer Protectionist proposals are examined according to Service Company, and the names of those who had their probable effect upon the course- of foreign fallen would be included in the memorial. If Widnes and Colonial trade, as it is at this moment. Theory and St. Helens were to join in the proposal it would there is, of course, but it is never obtruded ! give pleasure to those towns and also to the relatives upon the reader in any abstract form. !of those who had fallen in the war. There was no Thus, in twenty chapters, clearly, written and well I doubt Warrington ought to be the place, for the statue. illustrated with the necessary figures, we get a I The MAYOR (Alderman Bolton) : Warrington is the practical summary of the wholOquestion, and who- . headquarters of the district, ever reads will be ableat least to bring the subject I Major ROOME said Warrington was also the birth- down from the cloudlaud—whether of Imperialist place of the old South Lancsshire militia and also the dreams or economic abstractions—in which each i birthplace of the Ist Volunteer Battalion. Warrington side alleges the other to be dwelling, i! was the headquarters of the Regimental district as We do not see how anyone can read these chap- I well. ters.without feeling that the Protectionist case' The Menc.in : I think the Town Council will find a crumbles in all directions. Allegation after allege- site. tion is enamined and is found to be either inaccur- 'air. F. Mosns did not think it advisable to tie the ,‘ ate or exaggerated or a mere • mistinder- i Town Council to the site mentioned. There was a standing of the mechanism of trade. At i very good site behind the Tow,n Hall. the root of Protectionist reasoning lies ! Alderman FAIRCLOUGH considered that Queen's the fear of imports, the belief that they are a • Gardens were far more suitable for a public memorial loss to the country or that they displace labour. ' than behind the Town Hall. And even this when narrowed down is found to be I Councillor ROGERS said where one would see the mainly a fear of manufactured imports for it is I memorial at the back of the Town Hall, thousands admitted that we must have food and raw material, I would see it in Queen's Gardens. but it is supposed tobe desirable that we should do I The resolution was then carried, the question of the all manufacturing for ourselves and pay for our ; site being left with the 'Committee, subject to the * food and raw material bteexports of manufactured : approval of the Town Council. articles. And yet; if this is the object, it is found I THE FORM OF THE MEMORIAL. on analysis that we have achieved it far more off ec- SPEECH BY MR. H. ROBERTS. tively under Free Trade than any other country i under Protection • 1 Mr. H. Ito-rimrs said the resolution which he had the : find that "After fifty years of free imports, we .so honour to propose read as follows :—" That the statue be a figure of the late Colonel W. McCarthy O'Leary as far from the process of natural selection of indits- he appeared when leading the Ist Battalion, South try having overwhelmed our manufacturers, out. , . . Lancashire Regiment at their brave charge and storm- imports for home consumption, valued •in 1902 at l'a"confspie 's7Hill which cleared mg . ter tke way to Ladysmith .2462,000,000, consisted as to BGper cent of food for just prior to the death of that gallant officer. Also , our people and food for our factories. Of the re- that bl ta ets be placed on the pediment of the same maining 14 per cent, 5•4 per cent consisted of recording by Battalions the mutes of those who died materials used in domestic industry—the form of- ...ie.—</p>
        <p>Pon service during the War." He considered he was personally honoured and complimented,. Mr. Roberts said, in being permitted to move the resolution. He , had not .the slightest doubt that Warrington and the ! surrounding district would raise a sufficient sum of money to place on permanent record the feeling they had, not merely to Colonel O'Leary but to those who , were associated with him and. who were specially identified with this district, in their conduct in the war in South Africa. During the two years he (Mr. Roberts)had the honour to occupy the position of Mayor, this tOwn, he thought,.as much as any town in I the United Kingdom, showed its appreciation and its sense of its responsibilities, in the I manner in which the townspeople provided the necessary funds for taking care of those • whom the South Lancashire Regiment left behind. (Hear, hear.) He found on looking over a rough synopsis of accounts relating to that matter that Warrington subscribed no less a sum that £8,928 for the various funds required in connection with the war. Amongst other things in connection with the various charities for which this money was applied, he was glad to find they had no legs than .E 1,300 still invested for the benefit of the widows ! land children of those who died out there. -(Hear, hear.) I Having shown their appreciation at the time when this ' matter was so urgent, he did not think the people of i Warrington would be quite loyal to the convictions I they then held, and to their sense of their responsibil- I ity as they then understood it, if they did not put up a ' permanent record of their feelings of regard for those who served their country in South Africa at a time when the very existence of the empire was imperilled. (Hear, hear.) He • did not think they could have a more happy suggestion than that referred to in the ! resolution. Colonel O'Leary was connected with them for many ways. He was adjutant of their own Volunteer Battalion, and he made himself personally popular—he might say beloved. ' (Hear, he.ar.) Then his connection I with the line regiment, and his association with the particular incident referred to in the resolution—an incident pregnant. with the mast important issues to this country of ours—because there was no doubt whatever that this attempt through the Clearing of ; Pieter's Hill to reach Ladysmith was fraught with possibilities too grave to be considered for a moment. He was not one of those men who thought that Lady- smith would have fallen under any circumstances, but still it was on record that through the action of Colonel -O'Leary ,and the regiment associated with him one very important step in the relief Of Ladysmith was begun and successfully begun, although at the cost of British lives. ,He thought they could not have an incident which would appeal more strongly to the patriotism of this district than that referred to. It was our duty to leave those who came behind us some evidence of the appreciation of the services given so freely to this country by men who had gone before. (Applause). I Councillor ROGERS seconded the resolution.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT in supporting the resolution, said he was delighted with the shape which the proposed memorial was intended to take. He did not think any- thing could be more appropriate to the occasion. They would secure a distinct adornment of the town and a monument to very brave and worthy men. He had the privilege of speaking to Colonel O'Leary, just prior to him leaving our shores for South Africa. He (Alder- man Bennett) was struck -kith two things, in the first place, the light-hearted bravery with which he went— although he did not know it—to his death, and in the second place, the far-sighted wisdom he showed iv the struggle. He (Alderman Bennett) asked Colonel O'Leary if he thought the war would last long and he replied solemnly that he thought the war would last ac three years. "We had underestimated the difficul- ties' he said, "and it wouldibe a very long and arduous struggle indeed." Experience had justified the view taken by Colonel O'Leary. It only showed the courage with whichl the Colonel went into the battle and the courage also of the men associated with him. He (Alderman Bennett) was proud that the regiment was associated with the town and they would be doing the regiment credit *-Lnd themselves credit by erecting a memorial worthy of the occasion.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. ROBERTS, formerly Quartermaster-sergeant to the South Lancashire Regiment, Said he was thank- ful to all who were going to take action in raising a memorial to his late Colonel—McCarthy O'Leary. The other day he (Mr. Roberts) received a letter from Sir .Redvers Buller whilst at Liverpool and he mentioned the fact that on the day on which Colonel O'Learvmet his death he (Sir Redvers Buller) rode through' the columns of the South Lancashires as they were lying down prior to the charge and storming of Pieter's Hill. Sir Redvers said he spoke to Colonel O'Leary and com- municated the fact to him that he had just heard that Cronje had surrendered. He complimented the Ist South Lancashire Regiment on their appearance—and in speaking of the Ist South Lancashires he was referring to the -Old Fighting Fortieth. (Hear, hear.) Bir Redvers Buller further stated in the letter that he said to the Colonel, "You will soon have a chance of giving them as hard a knockns they have given you." "We are ready sir" was the prompt reply, to which Sir Redvers answered "Well in another hour your chance will come." "We will take it sir" rejoined the Colonel. Continuing uuder emotion, Mr. Roberts said they all knew the final issue of that attack. • One of the smartest officers and one of the most genial gentlemen that over trod on English 'nyouad fell. They all mourned him. He (Mr. Roberts)was one of those individuals who having passed 33 years in the service, the officers of his own corps were dear to hint. He was pleased to see Colonel Richardson present and he might add that he remembpred him joining the South Lancashire Regiment. (Hear. hear). _</p>
        <p>The MAYotisaid it w.sis interesting to learn that Sir Revers Bullerspoke to the late Colonel O'Leary on the morning of his death. By erecting a memorial they would be honouring the South Lancashire Regiment and paying a tribute of respect to those who had died in South Africa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday December 12, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday December 12, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE ARTIST.</p>
        <p>The I‘lvoit said it had b3la sii4gPstd that gr. ALFRED DRURY. ' be invited to submit a sketch of the statue. He was the sculptor of the bust of the. King which was on view in the vestibule. There was a general view that he was a very good an. Colonel WALKEa thought it would be bettar to leave that matter to the committee. The TOWN CLERK. observed that he did not think they could select a better artist. He was a young man but was recognised as being perhaps one of the most promising sculptors df the day. _</p>
        <p>Mr. HE;itn- ROBERTS saidlr. Drury was known to some of them, and his work was known to them even better than he was personally.. He (Mr. Roberts) knew Mr. Drury before ho was an acadatnician and he knew' that .Ifr.Drury was looked upon in the art world as on of the finest men of the day. Mr. Drury had no less than three memorial works in hand at. the present time. Personally he (Mr. Roberts) should be sorry if the committee passed him because he was convinced from what he knew of him and his Work that they could not get a better man. It was decided to leave the selection of an artist to the Committee.</p>
        <p>FORMATION OF' COMMITTEES AND APPOINT-</p>
        <p>INTENT OF OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the Mayor of St. Helens and the Mayor of Widnes be invited to form separate local cornmittees and invite public subscriptions. The following will constitute the Executive Com- mittee :—The Mayor (Alderman Bolton), Mestsrs. F. Monks, Mr. H. Roberts, Councillor W. Rogers, Alder- man Bennett, Alderman Fairclough, tile Mayor of St. Helens, the Mayor of Widnes, Alderman Smethurst. the Town Clerk of Warrivton (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), Colonel Richardson, Colonel Blackburne, Colonel Edelsten, Colonel Pilkington, Colonel Ridgway, Major borne, Captain Osborne, Captain Bundock and Captain Oakeley. The Borough Treasurer (Mr. J. Fairhurst) was appointed hon. treasurer, and the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) hon. secretary. The meeting terminated with an acknowledgment of the Mayor's services in the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s New Plant.</head>
        <p>Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s New Plant.</p>
        <p>"The Iron and Coal Trades Review" in a recent issue publishes an illustrattd article on the Mond gas-d.riven rolling mills and power plant which have recently been laid down at the works of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Company, Ltd. "Some three years ago," says the writer of the article, "Messrs. Monks, Hall and Company, the. well-known iron makers of Warrington, with foresight which experience has since amply confirmed, decided upon the erection of a gas olant for supplying power to their large works. In their ease the conditions were different from those that had usually obtained in such installations. It was necessary that the gas should be suitable not only for power„ bait also for heating furnaces, and, further, a large portion of the power was to be used for driving rolling mills, to which purpose gas. engines had not at the time been applied." The article is copiously illustrated, and contains a great deal of technical information, of interest and value to all engaged in producing power.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Beresford and Co., the well-known' brush manufacturers, are making a special extra display of Christmas novelties this season. For this purpose they have secured 52 Church-street, in addition to their old- establishment, 77, Church-street. ,The large window at 52, Church-street, is exceptionally attractive,having,a very fine display of dressing bags, ladies fitted blouse cases, purses. card, letter, cigar and cigarette cases in sealskin, crocodile, and lizard skins, all of latest designs, and in the newest art shades ; also a fine selec- tion with hall-marked gold and silver mounts. A feature with Messrs. Beresfords is that their dressing , bags and cases are fitted with Sheffield cutlery. A similar show on a smaller.scale in one of the windows at 77,Church- street, proves most attractive, while the other window is devoted to brushes of their own manufacture. The new toilet sets of the finest African ivory are deserving of special attention this season for those who desire a most enduring and useful present at a moderate cost, while those in silver and ebony make most desirable presents for those who do not wish the more luxurious ivory sets. A suitable present for a gentleman is a pair of :ivory, Silver or Ebony Military Hair Brushes in a case of which there is a fine selection.—Altogether one would be wise in viewing Nfessrs. Beresfords superb Stock, who wish to secure Christmas Gifts up-to-date, asteful, and of the utmost utility.</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>LABOUR REPRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>LABOUR REPRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>THE TRADES COUNCIL AND MR.</p>
        <p>!,WILKIE'S CHANGES,</p>
        <p>Mr. Buckley and the "Examiner."</p>
        <p>There was an interesting discussion at the meetina of the Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday even- ing with reference to the proposed candidature of Mr. Alexander Wilkie, who was recently invited to contest the _borough in the labour interest at the next general election. Mr. 0. Buckley, secretary of the local .branch of the Labour Representation Committee,intro- , duced the matter by presenting a report of the eon- ference of trade unionists at which the decision was arrived at. He added that no further steps had been taken as Mr. Wilkie had not yet replied. The next steps that would have to be taken in the event of his accept- tance of the .invitation would be the work of organization. They were practically a new party and the only ward in which organi- zation had been carried out was Orford Ward. He appealed to every delegate to prevail upon his fellow trade unionists to assist in the work when the time arrived. The Labour Representation Committee, he continued, had passed a resolution congratulating Mr. Stott and the Independent Labour Party upon the victory achieved in Orford Ward. He was instructed to communicate the information to the press and the editos of one newspaper had seen fit to challenge the letter. On one point there was 'a slight discrepancy, but in another sense it was true. Instead of wording the letter " the Liberal leallers " he might have stated "some Liberal leaders." Mat would have been more accurate. Many Liberals had tackled him- on the point and had asked why such a letter should have been published seeing that most of them had supported 'Mr. Stott. He .quite agreed with that and thanked those Liberals co behalf of the Labour Representation Committee. They assisted Mr. Stott, he thought, not from the Liberal point of view but because they were fully in sympathy with Labour representation. The fact remained that some Liberal leaders supported a Conservative candidate in opposi- tion to Labour. It was noted by those who worked in the Ward on the election day that various prominent members of the Liberal party—official members—not only supported the Conservative candidate, but worked for him by canvassing and "rushing in" voters here and there. This was the party that they wore being asked to ally themselves with. 'How much sympathy was there with Labour, when a Labour candidate was opposed by prominent Liberals? It had been said that the Labour Representation Committee was nothing but a Liberal dodge. He strongly 'resented that. Thecom- mittee was no more attached to the Liberal party than to the Conservative party, but they were prepared to accept the assistance of every worker in Warrington, provided that he came with a pure motive, and was entirely in sympathy with labour representation. Councillor STOTT pointed out that if Mr. Wilkie accepted their invitation he would conteSt the seat on purely independent lines, and; if elected,would form one of the Labour group in the House of Commons. His action would be guided by the decision of the majority of that group. Mr. T. Sm ITH (wiredrawers' delegate) said he 'did no think Warrington was ripe for a Labour candidate. I they were to tell Mr. Wilkie the feeling of Warrington and how the electionshad resulted in years gone by, he thought they would frighten him. He would have a "very big pluck" if he fought the election. They knew very, well that a "thorough-bred Conservative" would not vote against his party even for Trade Unionism, Mr. Chamberlain's scheme or anything else. He was in favour of a Labour candidate, but he would not support a three-cornered fight and thus give Mr. Pierpoint a walk-over. Many people were suspect- ing that it was the intention to give the Conservatives a walk-over .because now was the time when Mr. Cro-- field would have his only chance of being returned. If a Labour candidate were introduced he contended that Mr. Pierpoint would be elected. Were they prepared to educate the electors to that pitch that they could carry a candidate? The first thing they would have to do would be to find the money to contest the election in their own town. They did not want the expenses of the fight to be borne by outsiders. If they could raise the money in Warrington their candidate might have a °wharf. Mr. MASSEY said Mr. Wilkie was unanimously invited at the recent conference and he did not think members ought to try to "put the damper on" now. Let them support Mr. Wilkie. Mr. SMITH: You will lose. _ _</p>
        <p>I TiE 13-11—C-KTE-Y said Mr. Smith seemed to take a rather pessimistic view of the matter. Ho considered that al Labour candidate would have as good a chance as either I of the other two. At the recent conference over two thirds of the Societies in Warringtonwere represented ' by over sixty delegates and not a word of opposition was uttered. Over 2,000 Trade Unionists were represented and the unanimous voice was in favour of a Labour candidate. With work and organization he believed a labour candidate could win, if it came to a question of touching their pockets he believed trade unionists were prepared to pay. If Mr. Wilkie consented to stand, his society would pay his 'election expenses and returning'officers fee,and would also bear his expenses whilst in London if he were elected. He(Mr.Buckley) would take that opportunity of asking those societies who were not affiliated with the Labour Representation Committee to join it. He pointed out that various other expenses would have to be met by the committee in connection with educational and other necessary work. Mr. YORK said that judging from what he had seen and heard a Labour candidate would stand a very poor chance in Warrington until a vast amount of organizing and educational work had been carried out. Among the thinking working- men there were no two opinions as to the desirability of having libour represented, but unfortunately the great bulk of the population—nineteen out of every twenty—did not vote according to the knowledge they possessed or the good they thought could be done. They simply waited until the election day and then went "shouting mad" and voted for their political prejudices. Until more organization had been done it would be almost an insult to invite a man to become a candidate. Councillor STOTT said the feeling at the recent Labour demonstration was so strongly in favour of Labour representation that, the committee felt it their duty to take immediate steps to put that feeling into practical effect. It was pointed out that there was a pos- sibility of a good candidate who would be financed by his union, being secured. In regard to the financing of candidates by certain societies... it should be borne in mind that nearly every branch in Warrington was , pay- ing so much per year for Parliamentary representation. It would be hard upon them to ask for an additional contribution towards the expenses of a candidate for Warrington alone. It did not make any difference to him (the speaker) who a nUin was, so long ache was going to represent _labour in general. If any legislation wal passed which would benefit the workingmen of Woolwich, Barnard Castle or any other town it would benefit Warrington workingmen to the same extent. It did not matter where a man came from so long as his views and intentions were in accord with their require- ' ments. If societies paid a certain amount per year to- wards Parliamentary representation it did not make any difference where the candidates were trought for- ward so long as they had a reasonable chance of success Mr.Stott alluded to the growth of feeling in favour of labour representation and said the National Labour Re- presentation Committee now had a membership of over one million. He did not think the feeling of any working class constitfiency in regard to labour representation could be accurately gauged, from the results of previous elections. It had, he contended, been proved beyond the shadow of a doubt that there was no possible chance of a Liberal candidate beinr successful in Warrington. If there was, it would no- alter his intentions in the least, because he felt cont; fident that they must not consider either Liberal oe. Conservative candidates or -M.P.'s. They had been try- ing for years past to draw workingmen away from those parties. It had been said that men who had been Liberals would take to Labour candidates much more easily than Conservative workingmen. He could assure them that in the recent Orford Ward election, although his opponent was a Conservative, there were scores of Conservative workingmen who voted against the Conservative candidate. On the other hand, many Liberals did the same, but it was no credit to Liberals to say that they voted for the [About candi- date. There was some credit due to Conserva- tives who would vote against their own colours. The object of the Labour Representation Committee was not to bolster up one party or the other, but to work on straight. independent lines. Unless they did that, they could never hope to draw the workingmen from both parties. If they, as a Labournrganization, began to consider the position of the prospective Liberal candidate and whether he would withdraw or not, he thought they would be making a great mistake If the Conservative workingmen found that the Com- mittee were afraid of damaging the chances of a Liberal , candidate, their chances would be hopeless. Mr. Wilkie I had been well informed in regard to the state of affairs in Warringtoh, and if decided to become the candidate he would do so. knowing perfectly well the conditions which obtained in the town from a political point of view. He felt confident that as soon as Mr. Wilkie came among them the workingmen—whether Conserva- ' tives or Liberals—would flock to him. He was an honest, straightforward labour representative, and with a candidate of that stamp they would be able to look forward to the election with confidence, if the trade unionists who believed in Labour representation would rally round him and give him all the assistance they _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN fMr. J. Podmore, jun.), said it was unfortunate that the wiredrawers were not represented at the Conference. A circular of invitation was sent to them and if delegatei; hadattended,the comments which they had heard that night would never have been made. In the circular, the branches were definitely asked to discuss the matter—if necessary at. a special meetirig— and take a. vote before sending delegates. He firmly believed, from his experience of Mr. Wilkie, that the workingmen of Warrington would rise and support him.</p>
        <p>Let them give a man a chance before they con- demned him. When he had publicly stated his views and become known in the constituency they could judge him on his merits. Mr. Podmore referred to the numerous shades of political opinion represented in the Shipwrights' Society and said the organization was bound to keep Mr. Wilkie straight for labour. Ho (the speaker) had fought for many years for independent labour action because he believed it was the workers' salvation. If labour Was to obtain a just reading of the trade union Acts there would have to be strong representation of labour in Parliament. The political Parties professed to be in favour of labour candidates and to be the friends of workingmen. If they were honest in their intentions they ought to let a genuine labour man go in unopposed. (Hear, hear). The discussion then concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>SAD AFFAIR AT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>SAD AFFAIR AT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS MANAGER FOUND DROWNED.</p>
        <p>The inhabitants of Sankey and Penketh learned with surprise and regret on Tuesday that Mr., George Albert Morkill, managing chemist.for the Warrington Chemical Company, was missing from his apartments in Chapel- road, Penketh, and that his cap and umbrella had been foultd on the turnbridge spanning the St. 'Helens and Sankey Canal in Liverpool-road. Unfortunately their worst fears were realized for grappling operations re- sulted in the recovery of Mr. Morkill's body from the canal at five o'clock on Tuesday evening. The deceased gentleman was held in high respect in the district, having resided in Penketh for seventeen years. In Warrington he had made many friends who were shocked to hear of his untimely end. An active member of the Mersey Golf Club from its formation, he held the office of secretary for some years, and at one time he was a member Of the Warrington Cycling Club. He had long been associated with Sankey Church. THE INQUEST—AN OPEN VERDICE</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, conducted the in- quest at the chapel house inn, Great Sankey, yesterday, Mr. George Thompson was the foreman of the jury. The first witness was</p>
        <p>Mr. William Morkill, brother of the deceased, who gave evidence of identification. Deceased was 41 years of age and witness last saw him alive about three years ago. Mary Simith, Chapel road, Penketh, said deceased lodged with her. . She last saw him alive at 1.50 on Thursday of last week when he left the house after partaking of dinner. Mr. Morkill had been very much depressed of late. On sense occasions he wovld cry for a long time, and she had gone into his room And found him with his head resting in his hands. He had lodged with her for seventeen yvars. Mr. James Young said he had known the decease d for three years. On Thursday evening last week he met him at the Patten Arms Hotel, where he was play- ing at billiards with a friend. He intended to catch the 9.50 train to Penketh, but afterwards said he would go by car. Ultimately he decided to walk home, remarking that he thought he would enjoy the walk. Witness accompanied him to the corner of Parker-street and Hanover-street, where they parted at 11.30. Deceased seemed very cheerful. The Coroner: I understand there is some gossip with respect to something which took place that ,yoh want to deny.</p>
        <p>Witness: Quite so. T was not playing billiards with him. I have never played billiards or cards with Mr. Morkill. •</p>
        <p>Joseph Antrobits, a railway signalman, stated that at 12.20 a.m„ on Friday, he saw Mr. Morkill standing on the canal bridge at Sankey Bridges. When deceased heard him approazking he left the bridge and walked towards Penketh.</p>
        <p>_ John Twine, a railway signalman. said he saw deceased leaning over the canal bridge and looking into the waterat 12.45 When witness approached, Mr. Morkill walked off the bridge on to the Sankey-road. George Kirkham ' said that at 3 a.m. on Friday he found a cap and umbrella, subsequently identified as belonging to the deceased on the canal bridge. P.C. Dugdale said he dragged the canal on Tuesday assisted by Mr. Byfield. the Captain of the Runcorn Grappling Corps. The body was recovered at 4.50 p.m. between twenty-five and thirty yards from the bridge in the direction of Sankey Bridges station. The gold keyless watch which deceased was wearing had stopped at 1.55.</p>
        <p>After a short consultation the jury returned an open verdict.</p>
        <p>The Foreman said they wished to convey their deep sympathy to Mr. William Morkill and the relatives of the deceased in their bereavement.Alllthe jurymenknew thedeceased gentleman and'respected him andjhe thodght that not only on behalf of the jury but on behalf of the inhabitants of Penketh and Sankey and many of the people of Warrington, he could tender the deepest sympathy with the family. THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr. Morkill were laid to rest in the Cemetery at Great Sankey yesterday afternoon. The last rites were performed by the Revs. J. It. Jones (vicar of Sankey) and T. W. Barber. The chief mourn- ers were :—Mr. W. Morkill (brother), Mr. Bainbridge, Mr. James Smith, Mr. Donaldson, Mrs. White and Miss Smith. Amongst others present at the graveside were: —Mr. Dunn, of Manchester (representing the War- rington Chemical Company), Drs. Murray, Peacock and Donnelly (Widnes), the Rev. H. R. Wild, Messrs. C. E. Parker, G. B. Aitken, Ernest Parry, W. D. Jeans, F. Johnson, H. Bolton, V. E. Fowler, J. Barnard, Gilbert, W. H. Grimsdale, J. E. Withinshaw, James Dutton, H. P. C. White, Craig, and Shoebridge. Floral tributes were forwarded by the following :—Mr. Morkill, Mr. W. Morkill, " a loving sister," Mrs. and Miss Smith the Mersey Golf Club, Sankey Bridge.; Post Office, the, Rev. J. R. Jones, Mr. Harry Bolton, Mr. Ernest Parry, Mr. and Mrs. J. A. Forster, Mr. and Mrs, H. P. C. White. Mr.and Mrs. Dodds. and MN. P. Grange.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WARRINGTON TEMPERANCE FEDERATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TEMPERANCE FEDERATION.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Temperance Federation hold a meeting in Brick-street Independent Methodist Clun'cli on Tuesday evening last. Owing to the wetness of the evening, there was but a scant attendance of men. In the absence of Councillor Jaines Evans through indisposition, the chair was taken by Mr. Shaw and the speakers were Pasthr Grady and Rev. J. F. Matthews the president. Pastor Grady spoke of the proportion of prisoners, in various Jails which he instanced, who had been brought up in the Sunday schools of the land, and how in more than ninety per cent of the cases, the fall had been through drink. He pleaded for greater zeal on the part of the Church in tescuing the drunkard. The Rev, J. F. Matthews took as his subject, "The personal factor an element in temperance work." He spoke of the worthlessnes of many speeches aml sermons delivered. Many of them were reducible to aimless though amiable platitudes, but the personality of the man behind the speech or the sermon went for a great deal. His platitudes and style were lifted far above the ordinary level by virtue of something in the man himself. Everyone present was probably a "hero " to somebody else, and that special power should be used where possible in the influence, of temperance and sobriety. The speaker then referred to those on. whose behalf the temperance campaign is being con- ducted., He asked them to consider the worth of the individual man, to society at large, the possibilities and stored values tnat lay within a single human soul. Men were everywhere worth seeking and saving from' the misouing vice of strong drink. Let them unite and use all adequate social, political and religious methods to secure: the • permanent protection and ultimate salvation of the men who were now lost to themselves, society and God. • A special Meeting of the Council of the Federation was held at the Coffee Tavern, Horsemarket-street, on Thursday evening for the purpose of promoting the vigilance work of the Federation. An address on " Vigilance Work " by Mr. C. Boreland—the well- known Liverpool representative of the United King- dom Alliance, formed the chief feature in the pro- m-ammo. Mr. Brweland—who has been one of the prime • movers in the forward movement on licensing • matters in Liverpool—gave a mist, useful account of the work in that city from the com- menceinent of the Vigilance work: how the Watch Committee and Licensing Bench were improved; and gave many valuable hints from experience as to the way the Warrington Committee might go to work.— A hearty vote of thanks—moved by Mr. T. Dale, seconded by Mr. Wyllie—followed a general discus sion in which Councillor Luke Ellison, the secretary" and others took part.—Letters of apology for absence, .with flXpressions of sympathy,were received from Rev. J. F. Matthews, Councillors W. Brough and F. Stott and others.—The Federation is now carrying on use- ful work and bids fair to speedily take its due position, amongst the reformative agencies of the town. The Liberal Candidate for Leigh.</p>
        <p>I A. special meeting of the Leigh Liberal Association was held on Monday night to decide whether Dr. Pollard; of Southport, Or Mr. John F. L. Brunner, son of Sir John Brunner, M.P., should be the Liberal I candidate for the division in succession to Mr. C. P. Scott, M.P., who is retiring at the general election. ; The matter has been before the local , Liberals for. some time, and both gentlemen have addressed meet- ings of the Association. It was ,resolved that Mr Brunner be selected. Mr. Brunner has expressed him- self in favour of the. Labour party's programme, and it. is believed that the Leigh Labour party will support him. He is a director of Brunner, Mond, and Company. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>BUILDING SOCIETY SENSATION.</head>
        <p>THE BUILDING SOCIETY SENSATION.</p>
        <p>CLAIM AGAINST THE LATE SECRETARY. Pi'3o33iillgS at Liverp33l Assizas.</p>
        <p>nht LIVEIWO(11., FRID11",</p>
        <p>, There war:, Inlay familiar Warringt a flees in the Nisi Prins Court at Liverpooi Assizes yesterday, when the action brought by the Warrington Pei-ma neat Benefit Building Society against Mr. .Rober - Proud,formerly secretary of the society,came beforeM t Justice Jelf and a jury for trial. The building Societyr sought to recover 3::2,399 Ws. SJ. for monaysjallegel to have been received by Nit% Proud for the use of the society. Mr. Taylor, K.C. and Mr. Rigby Swift (instructed by Messrs. Robart Dnvies and Company) represented the plaintiffs and Mr. At iddan (instructed by Messrs. Browne and Crompton) appeared for the defendant.</p>
        <p>To the surpHse of mmy ia C the proce.,•dings were very brief. Mr: M illy a Howe I a verdict to go by default and His Lordship, after hearing two ‘vitnesses, directed the jur;- to find a ver- dict for the plaintiff for the amount claimed. This was done and the case concluded. OPENING SPEEYEI OF COUNSEL. Mr. Taylor, K.C., in opening the else for the plaintiff company, said the action was brought to recover the sumof they alleged Proud had embezzled. If the Matter had to be inquired into at length it would involve the eximination of a great many books and documents and a considerable num- ber of witnesses, and he rather anticipated that it might be possible to shorten the casein some way or other. He could not pretend to open the case.at length --going through all the books and .figures. Itiwould be idle to do so, and, if he did, he thought the jury would not bc,‘ able .to follow the c ise in the least. it would be sufficient to say that the defendant was secretary of the Warrington Permanent Benefit Build- ing Society from 1882 up to March last, when he was dismissed. During that time he had misappro- priated about £2,000, but inaknuch- as certain sureties' of his had Paid -£250, the claim, at present was about V„310. Counsel proceeded to give an idea of the manner in which the embezzlement was perpetrated. He said the defendant while secretary of the Society was supposed to keep a cash book, .ana.• to debit himself' with every item either that he received or paid into the bank., either by the solicitors or members of the society, or anyone else. What he did, however, was to credit himself with cheques paid into the bank, and that gave him the opportunity of misappropriating these. various sums. Sometimes instead of taking this course, he entered false credits. He professed to have paid money to people to whom he had never paid it, and there were other ways in winch he exercised his ingenuity in misappropriating the money of the Society. In the result he was prose- cuted and indicted for falsification of documents and found guilty. It then became necessary to bring the present action against him. Two . auditors —accountants—were elected, and 'went through the various books and documents with Proud, and in the result Proud appeared to have agreed that the amount now claimed was due from him. He ,had no defence, but the attitude he took was that he would not do one thing or the other--he neither said he did or he did not, and the plaintiffs were obliged to take the action. Counsel did not know what Mr. Madden's defence was, but it was easy in a ease like that to raise .a cloud of confusion. If that was done, it would be necessary to go through all the figures. MR. MADDEN AND HIS POSITION.</p>
        <p>Mr: Madden said the position he .took was this. He was 'instructed to appear for the defence by the family, out of respect toihe court. 113 did not admit what Mr. Taylor saiCf—that Proud admitted oning the amount clai7ned. rt was true that Proud was tried and convicted, and owing to the defence raised that his mind was in a state of wandering imbecility, and his memory bad, he was let off under the First Offenders' Act. Since that time he had rendered every assistance he could in the way of going through the books, &amp;c., and his family had done all they could to get an explanation from him, but had failed. At present he was in a state of ill-health, and quite un- able to appear, and he (counsel had a doctor's certifi- cate to that effect. At that stage he did not intend to cross-examine, bin lie asked that so far as Proud was concerned, each item should be formally proved. There was an action pending against those who were hrinerlyj auditors of the society They were charged with negligence in allowing Proud to commit these frauds, and the ease was down for the next assizes, but it would 'first be necessary to prove that Proud His Lordship: I cannot regard the interests of any other case except that before me. Mr. Madden: Then I don't press that my lord. His Lordship: Practically you have only got a watching brief. Mr. Madden said it could not exactly be described as a watching brief, but that was the position he was taking now. His Lordship: If that is so I think it is a case in which we may take the evidence Of the auditors, and those who will say he admitted that this sum was due from him.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor: That is what I propose. It is only a waste of time to do anything else. His Lordship It is either a prima facie case of admission or else it is a case for reference.</p>
        <p>Mr. Madden.: I will place no. obstacle in the way. I will do as von suggest. His Lords-hip: I-don't know whither the defendant: has any estate?</p>
        <p>Mr. traylor : None, ray Lord' flis-LOrdship : Then I don't quite know why the society proceeds. Mr. Taylor: They ltave been so advised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>THE EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Edwin Bradshaw, chartered accountant, . Warring- ton was then called. He stated that he was appointed by the society to go into their books in respect to this matter. He went through all the items and found there was a deficiency which had not been accounted for amounting to £,2619 10s. 51 The secretary had adopted various methods in falsifying the books. The main one was that he had failed to charge himself to the full amount of cash received, although he credited himself with the full amount he paid away. Taylor: That would enable him to appropriate any loose cash there was ?</p>
        <p>Witness : Yes. Another mode adopted by the secretary was to charge repayinents to depositors which he had not paid. A third method was to credit himself with payments in cash that had not been made in fact.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor : Did he gl through the books with you? •</p>
        <p>Witness: No. He went through the results which we obtained from our investigation. I told him what we bad found and he said in reply that he "didn't think it was so much." -</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor In point of. fact the deficiency has since been reduced by the payment of a security of £230?</p>
        <p>Witness : Ye. The deficiency now 'stands at 5.'2,399 10s. 'sd, William H. Woodcock, Borough Collector, was next called and examined by Mr. Taylor. He went through the books, .he said, with Mr. Bradshaw, and they arrived at the same result.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor: Did you go through the books with Mr. Proud?</p>
        <p>Witness : We tabulatel the result of our incittirie and offered to give Mr. Proud an opportunity of going through the books with us so that we mightdelete any- thing that he could explain. He was not able to explain any of the items. Mr. Taylor . Did he say anything about the amount Witness': At the first interview with .Mr. Bradshaw and myself he admitted that the amount we charged hitu with was—more than he thought. He did not thinlr -it would be more than D.)00 or ;U.OOO. Two or three, days later, witness went through all the items with the defendant, and he understood that Mr. Proud assented.to all the items except one and that one was an item of c‘...'GO in the case of Thomas Earlam Mr. Taylor: He said he had paid Earlatn :£6O? Witness: I adEtted it 'Might be so, but by the books Earlam was not a creditor. THE VERDICT. His Lordship (turning to the jury) • said : I will direct you upon that evidence to find a verdict for the plaintiff for .f.,2,309 10s. sd. - The jury returned a verdict accordingly.</p>
        <p>Major Roe, Government Inspector of Factaties for Manchester .and district, which include; Warrington, has retired on pension, after a service with the Home Department of 23 years. He will be succeeded by Mr. A. Lewis, who has been in charge of the Satth Wales district. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>NEW INSURANCE OFFICES.</head>
        <p>NEW INSURANCE OFFICES.</p>
        <p>The London and Manchester Industrial Assurance Company, Limited, who have been carrying on an insurance business in Warrington for the past 30 years have . now removed to the Midland Buildings, Winwick-street, which have been undergoing extensive alterations recently under the instructions of Messrs. J. Pendlebury. The cere- mony of opening the offices took place on Wednesday evening, when the Manager, Mr. Armstrong paid a visit and he, with the energetic Superintendent Mr. Penketh, invited the Warrington staff of agents to meet them at the new offices. Mr. Armstrong in declaring the offices opened said it gave him the greatest pleasure to come to Warrington to perform. that important event. The opening of such offices re-- fleeted great credit to the staff of agents and honour. to the superintendent. He also encouraged the agents Itogo on progressing in the business. Messrs. Mc Donald, Speed and Cox, visitors, all addressed the gathering and congratulated the Warrington staff on the energy which had been displayed. Mr. Penketh Superintendent, said he felt proud in being instru- mental, with the aid of the staff around him, in attain ing such a good position. The offices would compare favourably with the best in town, and would fill them all with new inspiration to do better. At this point he said he was pleased to read a letter from Mr. Davies, the Managing director congratulating himself and staff On the good results achieved. The progress made merited good and commodious accommodation and he was glad to con- vey. the good wishes of the Board of Directors. Messrs Heywood and Hughes responded on behalf of the War- rington staff. Afterwards MessrS. Armstrong and Penketh invited them to the Winmarleigh Cafe where theypartook of a good dinner,and spent the evening in: on enjoyable way.A musical programme:was rendered by the following Messrs. Hickman, Whittle, Cotterill, Hasel Townie, Gough and Miss Heywood. Speeches were made by Messrs: Roddy, Haekett,Cotterill, Rowe,. and others -bringing the gathering to an end at 10 30 'p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Ragged School Band of Hope.</head>
        <p>Ragged School Band of Hope.</p>
        <p>A social for the members of the above wTt hold on Wednesday evening in the schoolroom. There were eighty members present, and after partaking of an excellent tea, games, &amp;c., were indulged in. Misses S. and A. Titchard were, responsible for a most enjoy- able entertainment.</p>
        <p>ROCHE'S HERBAL EMBROCATION.</p>
